      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>NATIONAL SERVICE.</head>
        <p>NATIONAL SERVICE.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Many urgent appeals are being made to employers of labour to put their workmen on half-time rather than close down now or in the near future. I suggest the very best attitude for employers to adopt during the crisis is to keep in full work as long as possible all the old or 'physically unfit, and adv_-.3 every young fellow between 19 and 30 years (.1" age to join Une Regular Army, for service at acme or abroad, for the duration of the war. As all men responding to Lord Kitchener's appeal will be discharged immediately the war is over, employers might give an assurance that their situations will be kept open for them. Every supporter of the National Service League should do his—or her—very utmost to induce young men of all classes of the commu- nity to join the special campaign army imme- diately.</p>
        <p>Yours, &amp;c., D. STRACHAN, Major, Secretary North Western Area, National Service League. 57a, Drury Buildings, Liverpool, 18th August, 1914.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>PARALYSED MARKETS.</head>
        <p>PARALYSED MARKETS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—My letter was written before war was declared by England, and there is always a strong element of luck"" or chance in war. My idea was to show that although England might not engage her armies in the fight, the war was even then affecting women, since women are the housekeepers of the nation. Men and boys be:.ng voters, or potential voters, have a choice in their starvation; women and girls have none. There lies the difference. It is even possible to conceive starvation at your own decision being preferable to comparative comfort at another's. If I am correctly informed, England is now at war, not because she desires it, but because she has been forced into it by a man who has no use whatever for the.more refined or moral ideas, which we hope to see strengthened by the inclusion of women in politics. And sup- posing England had already recognised het women citizens, does Mrs. Solomon dare to sug- gest that thereby England would be suffering and that women would meekly stand by and see their homes devastated or their country starved out because trade routes were not preserved? If so, I must say that Mrs. Solomon does not realise the stukthat Englishwomen, and Suffra- gists in particular, are made of. In wars of defence women have always fought side by side with men in the protection of their homes —in the Civil War in the States, among the Boers, and now among the Belgians. It is in the wars of aggression, when property (that fetish of men) is upheld to the destruction of life (which is the special care of women), that women would be a restraining element. And unless Mrs. Solomon desires war for the sake of gain, she cannot think that this would be other than good for England. Surely the last few days instead of proving the impossibility of giving the vote to women, show very decisively the great need of some restraining force, other than that of men, is needed if the world is ever to be rescued from the brutalising force of the "mailed fist." If Germany had con- sulted its women, I venture to prophesy that at the present moment Europe would not be turn- ing itself into a shambles. D. B. PEMBERTON, Hon. Secretary Warrington Women's Suffrage Society. 90, Ellesmere-road, Stockton Heath.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>STREET TRAFFIC REGULATION.</head>
        <p>STREET TRAFFIC REGULATION.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l must first draw attention to an asser- tion of "Old Journalist," which is (in my opinion) not warranted. He accuses me of gra- tuitous impertinence, of which I was quite un- conscious. I am prepared to drop the question entirely rather than use impertinence without cause; it is quite unnecessary at present. The anecdote he relates will, perhaps, repay his compliment to me of being humorous, and his refusal to state a case without knowing that which is required, will, under the circum- stances, make him logical. I hope he will par- don an uncultured and uncultivated individual</p>
        <p>for being just a little vague; charity of expres- sion in matters of this description means much. In dealing with the authorities, "Old Journa- list" makes an important suggestion, i.e., mas- terly inactivity and benevolent neutrality. There is such a thing as winking the other eye. I think the point at issue is the "time limit." Now, I must confess I left it somewhat vague. I ought really to have put "speed limit." To make myself perfectly clear, I will suggest that the councillors of the borough of Warring- ton have power to enact bylaws. That being so. is it not possible for them to enact a bylaw for the purpose of reducing the speed of all vehicles passing through the town, and compel all motor- ists to recognise their authority? I think that will sufficiently convey to "Old Journalist" that which he desires. I hope he will under- stand that I do not attach all the blame to motorists. Many persons are killed and injured by their own negligence, for instance, they ride on the back of the lorries and when atrempting to get off get under the wheels. I wish to point out that the questions asked were only minor points compared to the rest. The major point was that "Old Journalist" should state a case either for or against better "street traffic Pegu- lotion," which he has quite ignored. Whether intentional or not I do not know. That, in my opinion, is the fundamental question, the others the means of accomplishment. What is the use of our discussing the means, if we are not agreed upon the object? I suggest it is utterly futile.</p>
        <p>I think I have said sufficient to convey to "Old Journalist" that which I desire, and if I fail in the object of conveying. then we shall most certainly have to drop thn subject. GEO. CHAMBERLAIN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>TERRIBLE ITCHING AND BURNING</head>
        <p>TERRIBLE ITCHING AND BURNING</p>
        <p>46, Dymock-street: Fulham, London, S.W.. Eng.—" After suffering for nearly eight years face and with eczema on ray body, running eruption of sores on my ears and head, getting no rest at night for the terrible itching and burning, I have used Cuticura Soap and Oint- ment and am now quite free from the trouble. The trouble began on mY forehead and spread into my head. One eyebrow was quite raw at times. I could get very little sleep with the burning and itching feeling and used to try to find a cool spot on the pillow for ease. My poor face was a sad sight and there have been times that I have been ashamed to go out. "I can safely say that I used some dozens of advertised cures and treatments which gave me no relief. Then I sent for a sample of Cuticura Soap and Ointment. I used the Soap and then spread the Ointment on some linen and bound my face up and the very first time I had relief. I kept on and Cuticura Soap and Ointment worked a miracle. Thanks to Cuticura Soap and Ointment my face and neck are fresh and clear." (Signed) Mrs. A. Turner, Jan. 21, 1914. Cuticura Soap and Ointment are sold by chemists throughout the world. Skin book will be sent post free on request. Address post card to F. Newbery and Bons, 27 tgAdg reg, , Charterhouse- WAN* tia _ •</p>
        <p>,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <p>i</p>
        <p>Ittttrs to tbe Oita</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. THE EDITOR of "The Examiner" invites corre- spondence on all subjects of public interest. larrinEas should be brief and pointed. Their object must be to impart or seek information. not abuse. Lirrrzus must in all cases be accompanied by the name and address of the writer. CommuswATioNs received early in the week— except urgent matters—ean always be given the best attention.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>FARM AND GARDEN.</head>
        <p>FARM AND GARDEN.</p>
        <p>FLIES AND WATER.</p>
        <p>Flies do .nOrlilie- water, says a writer in " Farm and Home," but the fouler it is the less they dislike it. I am speaking, of course, of such as annoy cattle, not those that naturally inhabit water—the May flies or caddies, for in- stance. Therefore, to keep brooks and ponds clean and a good supply of water therein is the way to give comfort to your herds. It is said that the botfly rarely crosses a stream. As soon as the pursued ox can make the stream the fly is checkmated. But neither pure streams nor hilly grounds avail much if the timber, hedges, and all Other bodies that impede the free course of the breeze are present.</p>
        <p>THE POINTS OF A GOOD MILKING COW. The points of a good milking cow vary in different breeds somewhat, and a good deal in different people's estimation. In such herds, say, as Shorthorns and South Devons, our big- gest dairy breeds, they would run as follows, roundly speaking: A fairly long head but not too narrow across the forehead; expanding nos- trils, bold, but kind eyes, mellow horns, not over long or thick; slender breedy neck, well set into the shoulders; not heavy shoulders, full chine, wide sprung, deep ribs, and plenty of Ism, strong loin, wide hips, open rumps, fairly full buttocks, full flank when cow is in high con- dition, roomy bag, squarely set teats, rather long and slender than short and thick; well de- veloped milk veins, not over wide brisket, rather fine bone in not over short legs, mellow hide, pleasant to the touch and loose on the carcase, and withal a sprightly carriage, stopping short of any display of abnormal nervousness. To all this should be added a good appetite and corre- sponding good digestive powers. Such are points in a dairy cow which are to be esteemed, and indicate that she is not only a free milker but a good breeder, too, with something for the butcher in the latter end. But it does not fol- low that these are show points.—" Farm and Home."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>The large flowering Penstemons are Apt to bend down with the weight of bloom, and a neat stake for supporting them in an upright position will improve the appearance of the garden. Biennials big enough to be moved from the seed beds should have a shift into a nursery bed before finally planting out. Cut back some of the long shoots of Violas, to induce more root growths for cuttings later on. Syringe Kidney Beans if black fly appears, putting some force behind to dislodge the in- sects. Feed the plants with liquid manure or fertiliser' once a week. _ - .</p>
        <p>Sow seed of Pansies and Violas in the open. Bedding plants are now practically at their best, and all dead blooms should be picked off. Loosen the soil with the hoe. Continue to layer Strawberries. The ground at the present is moist enough, but if there is a spell of dry weather water must be given. The plants must not be allowed to become dry. Some interesting experiments are being car- ried on at Merton Park in crossing Calceolarias, Nasturtiums, Primulas, Campanulas, and other flowering plants. In Calceolarias, especially, new combinations of form and colour have been obtained; while with Nasturtiums some very handsome double flowers have been evolved. Finish layering or taking cuttings of Carna- tions while the soil is warm and moist. Early rooting is essential if the plants are to stand out during the winter. Some of the early varieties of Chrysanthe- mums are opening their buds, and those carry- ing several buds in clusters will be all the better for being disbudded. Many will have become loose after the recent strong winds, and will require to be made secure again. Hoe around the roots to aerate the soil and keep down the weeds. -</p>
        <p>Cut out the exhausted portions of the central shoot of Snaps, to encourage side growths. Gather seed pods of flowering plants and dry them well before putting away in small paper bags, duly labelled. A dusting of guano over the surface of lawns in damp weather will do poor turf much good. Cut the grass once a week, leaving the cut grass on the lawn.</p>
        <p>PLANT AND SOW MORE VEGETABLES. All gardeners will be well advised to plant all available space with vegetables. There may not be a scarcity of green food, but if there is a supply in the garden it will help to reduce the cost of living to a little extent. All existing crops, such as Potatoes, Onions, Carrots, and Turnips should be allowed to fully mature, un- less requirements demand immediate use. Sow Carrots in a sunny position for use in a young state in the late months. Space for these may be found along the rows of Beans. Sow seed of Daniel's Defiance Cabbage. It is a good variety, being quick in growth and of good size and quality. The following are suitable seeds to sow: Tur- nips, Turnip-rooted Beet, Carrots, and Onions. To these may be added Cabbage for cutting in spring, Onions for pulling green, Spinach, and , Corn Salad. On light, warm soil, Hicks hardy white (Cos) and Stanstead Park or Lee's Im- mense (Cabbage) Lettuce might be tried. The variety of Carrot grown should be the early horn or stump-rooted. The Cabbage may be sown in shallow drills 9in. apart, and the plants should be set out next month, choosing a rainy period, 18in. apart in each direction, or lft. to 14in. apart, every alternate plant being cut as soon as growth commences in the spring. Lettuce-leaved and Flanders Spinach will produce more abundantly than the large prickly variety generally grown on account of its supe- rior hardness. Turnips should be sown in drills, 18in. apart in the rows. When "tops" are wanted in the spring a sowing can be made in September in rows two feet apart. The sowing should be thin and the plants left un- th i nned. Likewise it must not be overlooked that there are various varieties of the Cabbage mbe-that may and should be normally planted ant'. The plant vendors have for sale young lants cone up in scores of curled Kale, cottagers' Kale, hearting Cabbage, Asparagus Kale, Dutch Savoys, Curly Greens. Coleworts'also, provide useful plants for cutting from late autumn on to March. Perhaps some produce more green- stuff for the area they grow upon than Kales; the "cottage," which is very hardy, and the " curly" or "Scotch." In the place of early Potatoes put out Tom Thumb Savoys or other Cabbages. Ellam's Early and Flower of Spring arp tv o good Cabbages for spring cutting. Sow seed I now.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>WHAT IS "ARMAGEDDON "?</head>
        <p>WHAT IS "ARMAGEDDON "?</p>
        <p>There never was a battle of Armageddon, and why the word is journalese for a hypothetical catastrophic world-war we do not know. But there are still some people who, like the old lady who found comfort in the blessed word Mesopotamia, delight almost as much in these mystical allusions as when our forebears. ittudied the Apocalypse with imaginative min- uteness. Few people read the Book of Revela- tion nowadays, and fewer, perhaps, know that there is no lurid description in it of the great battle of Armageddon. All that one reads is (Rev. xvi. 16), 'And he gathered them together into a place called in the Hebrew tongue Armageddon." The verse comes in flit middle of the description of the pouring ouf of the vials of the wrath of God. It has no direct reference to anything that goes before or comes after; and who "he" was that gathered, or "them" that were gathered, is not clear. We have often seen the verse quoted with "He" spelled with a capital letter. But our Author- ised Version does not print it so, and therefore the AlLuight7. OPPWAtIY Le Pat refeiLed tgh , _ _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, AUGUST 22, 1914.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, AUGUST 22, 1914.</p>
        <p>;14 40.1•101010;4444(4014 +++,14+444:401014 bI4 44 44 .14 N POULTRY PE 4. 4. .i. .14 Hints and Interesting Facts and .14 Chat for Amateurs. .14 014 3.14.14.14.14+++.14.14.14.14+++401.4.401444 4.</p>
        <p>SOME POPULAR BREEDS.</p>
        <p>One of the popular breeds is the Minorca. This belongs to what is known as the Mediter- ranean type of fowl. There are two varieties, the black and the white. The former is the more common , and the more useful, the latter being a fancy bird. The Minorca is the best layer we possess, and, moreover, the eggs are large in size. The birds have one drawback, however, namely, they are slightly delicate, and, therefore, do not answer well in cold or exposed situations. They will not, moreover, stand much wet, and thus the conditions have to be studied.</p>
        <p>Leghorns. Of the Leghorn, there are six useful varieties, the white, brown, cuckoo, black, pile and buff. The first two mentioned, the white and the brown, together with the buff, are the best and most popular. This breed is also an excellent layer, equally good as the Minorca so far as the number of eggs produced is concerned, but in size they are slightly less. The Leghorn is a very precocious bird, and the cockerels can fre- quently be distinguished at four or five weeks of age. They are small-bodied, upon clean yellow legs, and exceedingly hardy. I have never- yet come across any place where Leg- horns, especially white, would not thrive, and thrive well. They seem to be able to stand any amount of cold and exposure.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>Houdans.</head>
        <p>Houdans.</p>
        <p>Houdans were introduced into this country about forty years ago, and it was predicted that they would soon become the most popular fowl. This has not been the case, and they do not command &amp;la y great amount of popularity. They are very good layers, and the eggs are of a medium size. Of the breeds belonging to the laying or non-sitting class limulans are the best on the table, and by some they are considered to be more of the general purpose type. Houdans are hardy birds, and ones that can be fully re- commended for utility purposes.</p>
        <p>Scotch Greys. As indicated by the name these birds are of Scotch origin, and are a very useful variety. They have white legs, are similar to the Ply- mouth Rock in plumage, and are exceedingly good layers of very large eggs. They are ex- ceptionally hardy, and will thrive well every- where, even under the most adverse conditions. Ancona:.</p>
        <p>These birds are of the Mediterranean type, and lay a large number of eggs, which are, how- ever, very poor in quality. Many people think there is little or no difference in eggs, but in reality there is a marked difference. Some breeds lay eggs of a first-class quality, whilst others produce ones very poor indeed. It is the latter case with Anconas, and the eggs contain a large percentage of water. Aneonas are mot- tled in plumage, of a small size, and in shape are similar to the Minorca or the Leghorn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>Andalusians.</head>
        <p>Andalusians.</p>
        <p>The hens belonging to this breed produce the largest eggs of any variety, but when fowls are kept for utility purposes this is not altogether to be recommended. At the present time, whe.l eggs are sold by number and not by weight, no more money will be obtained for the large speci- mens than for the small. As • will be readily seen, a bird cannot produce as many large rggs in proportion to the amount of food consumed as is the case when they are small, and, there- fore, it pays better to keep a breed that lays a medium-sized egg, one that is more useful for marketing purposes. The Andalusians are not, therefore, of special usefulness in this respect. When one is keeping fowls in order to supply one's own table, and large eggs are required, then this breed may be recommended.</p>
        <p>Redcaps. At one time the Redcap was in much greater favour than is at present the case, other breeds having been introduced which, to some extent, have taken its place. Its distinguish- ing feature is the enormous comb which it car- ries, frequently measuring five inches by three. The hens are excellent layers and extremely hardy. The eggs are of a good size, and of an excellent flavour. - -</p>
        <p>All the varieties belonging to the laying or non-sitting clan lay white-shelled eggs, are poor upon the table, are active, and attain maturity at an early age. There is a very common idea amongst many people that tinted shelled eggs are richer than white ones. This is not neces- sarily the case. The only thing is that un- doubtedly a brown egg has a richer appearance upon the table than a white one.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>CYCLING AND MOTORING.</head>
        <p>CYCLING AND MOTORING.</p>
        <p>Are Cycling Clubs and Mixed Riding</p>
        <p>Popular P</p>
        <p>THE DANGER OF TOE CLIPS</p>
        <p>A writer in a recent issue of a contemporary was praising toe-clips for all he was worth. To read him one would think that they were the only things worth having. Might I ask him, when he,is out next time, to compare numeric- ally cyclists on the road, i.e., those who use toe- clips and those who don't? Some time hack, soon after the free-wheel boon, toe-clips came into fashion, but have been dying a lingering death ever since. The end will come. The sooner the better. Regarding the question of added danger which is likely to accrue through riders falling from their machines when toe- clips are in use, my contemporary emits this surprising remark, " Does it ever strike these people that when the cyclist falls the machine falls too, and there is no peril at all?" The first part of the sentence I partially agree with, the last few words I am too polite to answer. Of course, we all know that falls are like get- ting into a feather bed, or a garden of roses, an every-day occurrence, and should be looked for so as to test the virtue of toe-clips. Now I can say without bias that when this adjunct came into fashion, being a keen cyclist, I gave the toe-clips a thoroughly good test. I have had a good few spills in my time, and I can safely say I have avoided more accidents simply because I had my right or left leg free at the crucial moment. When my feet were hampered I became altogether,harassed, and my face and hands bore the convincing results, whereas the leg in the other case takes off the first jar and the ultimate fall, if any (according to speed and circumstance), is materially minimised. Leaving all this unsaid, and treating the matter psychologically, this toe-olip adoption can be demolished. Having to remember that your foot, is not absolutely free, having to transfer thought from brain to toe when danger is imme- diately imminent, is unquestionably a waste of a precious moment. Therefore, I say this in- vention is an encumbrance and should be avoided by every thinking cyclist. To those who cannot keep their feet on the pedals when cycling—don't ride. ere there any, though?</p>
        <p>Cycling Clubs. • Periodically the question of the value of cycling clubs turns up, and yet it is always fresh. There are two points in question. First, the value of the cycling club at all, and second the preference or otherwise for the " mixed " club. Surely the value of the club scheme has been proved often enough to save its being re- argued; yet there are still some sceptics. A club affords the cyclist the pleasure of a run in com- pany. There are many young riders who are fond of the bicycle and enjoy riding, but are loth to cycle alone. For them the club run is a boon and a means to the end—the enjoy- ment of a run on a machine in the country. Cycling alone is pleasant, but lonely cycling does not give all the pleasure that the bicycle can afford. A writer once made a list, which he headed "People I like to avoid." First on the list he wrote, " (1) Myself." There are many riders who feel like that. There is a feeling of loneliness that comes over one towards the end of a day's run, when the darkness is coming on, and there seems to be a greater and increasingly greater distance between the milestones that is dispelled if one is in the company of four or five others. The question of a mixed club is another matter. The arguments against the mixed club are that it is a very " cliquey " affair, and that couples get together and either linger behind and delay the party or ride on ahead and leave those who are not fortunate enough to be able to pair up to get along as best they can. By all means, therefore, push forward the club idea, for while it is company for yourself, you are doing good to. others, who, probably, without the companionship of a club would not get the best fun out of their machine.</p>
        <p>The Popularity of Motoring. Figures are compelling in their very baldness. It is only when we come to statistics that we are able to form an . opinion on the hold which a special form of sport has on the public at large. It seems almost needless to comment on such a subject, but until I began to think over the matter and find out a few • particulars I never imagined that the motor car and motor cycle were ousting out other pastimes. It is a matter of a couple of years ago since I visited the Upper Thames, until I went the other day. My memory of it was that on a fine day the river was crowded with young people, but that is now a thing of the past. The younger generation appear to derive more pleasure from road trips than ever before, for this year—although . the weather has been exceptional—business is very had for the boatmen. I see that the London County Council now issues over 65,000 licences a year. At least 45,000 are to private owners of motor cars and motor cycles. I think it would be more conducive to health if the two pastimes were combined. What more delightful than a run into the country, then a few hours spent amidst the oharm of the river, and a ride back to town?</p>
        <p>Traffic Regulations. Some South American motorists were recently discussing the traffic regulations of London. One of them said that in the Argentine there is a greatly increased demand for the motor car, and this has had an effect on traffic regulations. One of the things that struck him specially in this country was connected with this point. He states that, in his opinion, the regulation of London traffic is excellent, except in one detail. He has a foreigner's respect for the London police, and considers that their command of difficult situations is wonderful, but he has one fault to find—when the driver of a vehicle wishes to stop at a shop on the right-hand side of the road, he drives completely across the street and draws up at the kerb. In South America they have another way—he must proceed along the street until he reaches the cross roads, he must then turn into the stream of traffic going the opposite direction, and when he reaches his stopping-place he draws up beside the kerb. In certain towns, Rosario, for instance, the regu- lation is carried even further. If a taxi has a passenger who desires to stop at a shop on the left-hand side of the road (traffic regulation is "keep to. the right," as is the law in most foreign towns), the taxi-driver proceeds straight down the road, passes the shop turns into the stream of traffic on the left-hand side, draws up at the kerb, drops his passenger, and then takes his car into the centre of the street, drawing up crosswise to the traffic. This method is better than that in for)ce in this country, since it is more convenient.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>CAUGHT IN • A FIELD.</head>
        <p>CAUGHT IN • A FIELD.</p>
        <p>Deserter from the Irish Guards Charged at the Local Court.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Borough Court on Mon- day, Michael Dolan, no settled abode, a private in the Ist Irish Guards, was charged with lodging out in King Edward-street and being a deserter from his regiment at Pirbright since May 12th, 1905. The police evidence showed that at 12.50 that morning the prisoner was found asleep in a field off King Edward-street, Manniiester•rold, and when he was asked what he was doing there he replied that he had no money. On the way to the Police Station he made a state- ment, with the result that he was also charged with being a deserter, the "Police Gazette" verifying the prisoner's statement. The Chief Constable said that when deserters had surrendered they had been pardoned and given ti railway piss to their headquarters, but he did not think that case was in the same category. A communidation had been sent to his commanding officer, and he (Mr. Nicholls) asked the Bench to allow him to hand the man over to an escort if one arrived; but on the other hand the commanding officer might ask for the man to be sent along without an escort. _ . .</p>
        <p>The Bench agreed to the- Chief Constable's suggestion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>CYCLISTS' NOTEBOOK.</head>
        <p>THE CYCLISTS' NOTEBOOK.</p>
        <p>Get-out-of-my-way or Die</p>
        <p>Motorists.</p>
        <p>BELLS.</p>
        <p>• Since writing the note on the new "ding-dong" I have often been Asked what sort of bell I would recommend, showing that cyclists gener- ally find this useful and necessary adjunct not altogether satisfactory. I will begin at the wrong end of my tale and say at once I am alto- gether in favour of the revolving bell. For a good many reasons. It is the same price as others, it lasts longer, and its sounds can be so greatly varied. The ratchet of the ordinary bell is generally made of soft metal, and wears out against the cogs very rapidly; and when the ratchet is done there is no means of sounding it, except by hitting it with your pocket knife, and I doubt whether the policeman would accept that as "audible warning." The revolving domes give two notes generally harmonious, and it seldom needs the lever to actuate it. A touch with the thumb gives the necessary move- ment, and the one single " dong " needed to warn the street folk, the policeman on duty, &amp;c. It is also a good deal pleasanter for every- one who hears a quiet tinkle, instead of a bang- ing peal loud enough to startle a boilermaker, which forms the joy of the cad or the'hobblede- hoy on a new bicycle. For these reasons let us ban the horn, the ding-dong, and all other hideous noises that spell destruction to town pleasure or town nerves. As for lasting powers, I have und a revolving bell for a couple of years after the bottom half had dropped off and was lost; then the lever would not act, so I rung it with my thumb, and it is only because I have to ride through town that at last I bought a new one. This one can be heard, if I really try, even above the scream of the safety valves on the engines at the Central Station, or the rattle of a greengrocer's empty lorry running down Horsemarket-street as the horse and driver are going home to tea.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>UNNECESSARY NOISES.</head>
        <p>UNNECESSARY NOISES.</p>
        <p>The above make's one wonder how many years yet the motorists will inflict on us their awful noises before settling down to some standard, orderly signal. Just like we cyclists have done. I was overtaking a little girl on the Knutsford- road, and just in the narrow part overlooking the river. One of the biggest of motors was overtaking us both, and because I, helped by other traffic, would not let it dash past us, it was uttering the most soul-destroying "piggies" —that awfully swank noise that imitates a cock pheasant, don't yah know. (Poor little pheasants, they aren't getting shot this year.) Well, the girl kept looking round in abject fear, as if she didn't know whether to jump off there and then, but, of course, I encouraged her, and we got safely into the wider road opposite the Park, where we could breathe more freely. But whether from impatience, or from undue hurry, or from sheer cussedness, far more misery is in- flicted by motors, ST., than is necessary. We have already driven all our children into the "backs," and during the week-day our town streets are a perfect pandemonium of noise. Will it always be so? Surely—</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>ACROSS WALES.</head>
        <p>ACROSS WALES.</p>
        <p>Our road to Towyn last week was by the ordinary main route through Llangollen, Bala and Dolgelly. The return was by a less known route, and, in the reverse direction, can be used as the finest access to Aberystwyth, Szc. Round- ing the hill by Aberdevey we stayed the night at Pennal, near Machynlleth. At that little village the Flag Inn will always attract us in the future. Then we ascended the Dovey Valley to Mallwyd, and up the Bwlch y Fedwen to the top of the moor. There we made our first ac- quaintance with another Severn tributary, the Banwy, which led us by a splendidly graded road past the Cann Office Inn to Meifod on the Vyrnwy, Llanymynech and Oswestry.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>W.W.C.C. AT PEOVER.</head>
        <p>W.W.C.C. AT PEOVER.</p>
        <p>The outing to Peover, with its tea and sing- sOng at the Pinewood Cottage. has become an annual event. Twenty-nine riders sat down to a delicious meal in the big dining hall, and did full justice to it. Then the piano was lifted in, and a neat little concert filled up the evening until 8 o'clock. The captain was chairman, and he called on Miss Plinston to open with a piano- forte solo. She also accompanied Miss Jones, who sang "My Ain Boa," "Good-bye." and "Mifanwy," and Miss Kershaw in "The Prom- ise of Life." Mr. L. Lawton played "Ave Maria" and the "Rosary" on the single- stringed fiddle very tastefully, and Mr. J. White also gave ,a pianoforte solo. Mr. Ravenscroft sang two comic songs, and the choruses included a selection of rag-time songs. "Rule, Britan- nia" and the "Marseillaise," and, of course, the loyal chorus, concluded the evening.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>CLUB RUNS TO-DAY. Warrington C.C.—Davenham. Wesleyan C.C.—Trale Cliff. Sankey Bridges W.G.—Liverpool. St. George's.—Pickmere.</head>
        <p>CLUB RUNS TO-DAY. Warrington C.C.—Davenham. Wesleyan C.C.—Trale Cliff. Sankey Bridges W.G.—Liverpool. St. George's.—Pickmere.</p>
        <p>LAMP TIME. To-day, 8.30; next Saturday, 8.15. SILVER-STREET.</p>
        <p>THIS WOMAN'S TROUBLES GONE</p>
        <p>Terrible Cramps, Dizziness, Nervousness, Misery—Her Story of How She Got Well Again.</p>
        <p>" Your remedies have relieved me of all my troubles. I would have such bearing down misery and cramps add such weak, nervous, dizzy spells that I would have to go to bed. Some days I could hardly stay up long enough to prepare my food. "'he doctor's medicine did me no good so I changed to Lydia E. Pinkham's Vege- table Compound and got good results from the first bottle. I kept on taking it and used the Sanative Wash with it, until I was well again, I think every woman who suffers as I have, could take no better medicine." (Name and address on request.) Testimony of a Nurse. I am a nurse and when I do much lift- ing I , have a female weakness, but I take Lydia E. Pinkham's ;Vegetable Compound and I cannot say enough in praise of it. I always recom- mend it for female tioubles." (Name and address on request.)</p>
        <p>The ma.. _ of Lydia E. Pinkham's Vegetable Compound have thousands of such letters as those above—they tell the truth, else they could not have been obtained. This medicine is DO stranger— it has stood the test for years. Write to-day for a free private book (to women only) to the Lady Manager, the Lydia E. Pinkham Medicine Co., 42a Hampstead Road, London' N.W. For 2s. 9d. she will send you a bottle of the Lydia E. Pinkham Vegetable Compound, or for 4s. 6d. a much larger bottle. Any chemist will supply it at these prices.</p>
        <p>viog.t4.;;l2ll.iter 'ra • - ticers</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>OUR FOOD' SUPPLY. How to Guard Against Future</head>
        <p>OUR FOOD' SUPPLY. How to Guard Against Future</p>
        <p>, Shortage. HOME EGGS AND POULTRY.</p>
        <p>The Board of Agriculture and Fisheries ad- vise poultry keepers to pay special attention to their stock at the present tithe. The country will be very largely dependent on home supplies of eggs during the continuation of the war. Every effort should, therefore, be made to select and retain young and healthy laying stock, and pullets and one-year-old hens should receive careful attention.</p>
        <p>Every opportunity should be given the birds to gather as 'Much natural food as possible, and full advantage should be taken of the stubbles. On many farms the poultry at this time of the year can obtain a very large proportion of the food they require, and there is no necessity to reduce the number of really profitable birds by marketing them. The birds selected for sale should be those which are old or otherwise likely to prove unprofitable. Every poultry keeper should make prepara- tions for commencing hatching operations as early as circumstances permit of his doing so. Birds hatched in autumn and early winter, however, require special attention, and it is advisable to recognise this when determining the number to be reared during the autumn and winter months. The object should be to rear as many as can be properly looked after. In providing food for poultry, much that is often regarded as refuse on the farm may be Profitably utilised; small potatoes, cabbage leaves, "tailings," should be carefully con- served and fed in order to obviate the use of food required for other purposes:</p>
        <p>. . Board of Agriculture and Fisheries, 4, Whitehall-place, S.W., 13th August, 1914.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>TALE OF THE TEETH. Peculiar Case at the ' Warrington County Court. WOMAN LOSES HER CASE.</head>
        <p>TALE OF THE TEETH. Peculiar Case at the ' Warrington County Court. WOMAN LOSES HER CASE.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Court on Monday, before his Honour Judge Gurner, Mrs. John McCabe, 1, St. Austin's-lane, sued Mr. James Walmsley, of Blackburn and Southport, for the return of 1:11 45., paid for a set of false teeth to his son, Percy Walmsley, which did not fit and were rejected. Applicant also claimed £5 ss. damages for breach of contract. Mr. H. Backhouse (Blackburn) appeared for the defendant, and Mr. A. Browne, of War- rington, was for. the applicant. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Browne said that -defendant's son had had a dentistry business in Warrington, and a 1 traveller named Massey, who was employed by him, persuaded Mrs. McCabe to have a set of teeth. Walmsley agreed to supply the set at „ClO, and further persuaded the applicant to have some teeth in the lower jaw for £1 4s. She paid or supplied goods to the defendant to the amount of £9. Walmsley was apprehended for obtaining money by false pretences from a bookmaker in Warrington. His father, the defendant, went to Mr. McCabe and asked him to stand bail. After a conversation the defend- ant guaranteed Mr. McCabe by paying ,t5O in the bank. When he was asked about the teeth the defendant assured Mr. McCabe that they would be seen all right by him. Correspon- dence ,passed between the defendant and Mr. McCabe, but when the teeth arrived they could not be put into Mrs. McCabe's mouth and were not at all according to order. There was one tooth short in the top set, and the oottom teeth were absolutely ludicrous. In a letter to the defendant Mr. Massey assured him that on the Bth July he (the defendant) promised Mr, and Mrs. McCabe that he would see them all right when his son's case was settled. Mr. Massey also told defendant that the teeth were never made for Mrs. McCabe. The applicant's case was that Mr. Walmsley, sen., was respon- sible, for he had stepped in the shoes of his son. He received some of the money for the teeth and sent them on.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>MRS. McCABE'S EVIDENCE.</head>
        <p>MRS. McCABE'S EVIDENCE.</p>
        <p>Mrs. McCabe gave evidence bearing out the above statement and said Mr. Walmsley pro- mised to see her all right with regard to the teeth, and said he had bought- the business from his son.</p>
        <p>Mr. Deckhouse: Did you know the business was carried on by another man when Mr. Walmsley, jun.. left it?—Yes. Why did you not go to see Mr. Walmsley's successor, so that, as promised, Mr. Walmsley would endeavour to do what he could • for you? His Honour Well she did not. _ .</p>
        <p>Mr. Backhouse : Was it beets* you had given the order elsewhere ?—No; I have not given an order elsewhere.</p>
        <p>Mr. Backhouse objected to a question put by Mr. Browne, asking if Mr. Walmsley's suc- cessor, had said that the teeth could not be put right. Mr. McCabe confirmed the previous state- ment and said that Mr. Walmsley's words were: ",Whatever comes or goes I will see you all right." That referred, to the teeth. He received a letter from the defendant in which he said he had taken over Percy's business and would send the teeth when the balance of the money was forwarded to him. If the teeth were not right he would forward the money they had paid. That letter was lost, for he (Mr. McCabe) left it in a pocket of his rain- coat, which he left behind at a funeral.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>CHIEF CONSTABLE'S STORY.</head>
        <p>CHIEF CONSTABLE'S STORY.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable said that defendant and</p>
        <p>Mr. McCabe saw him at the Police Station in regard to the bailing out of Percy Walmsley. In reference to the teeth Mr. Walmsley said he would see that McCabe did not lose any- thing. McCabe did not go bail for defend- ant's son after they had had a conversation. Mr. and Mrs. Samuel Massey gave evidence as to defendant promising to see the,McCabes all right, and also as regarded the way in which the teeth had been badly made if they were for Mrs. McCabe.</p>
        <p>JUDGMENT FOR THE DEFENDANT. Mr. Backhouse explained that Percy Walnis- ley had been acquitted of the charge. He contended that there being no deed of assign- ment it was not correct to say that the de- fendant had stepped into his son's shoes. Mrs. McCabe had admitted that she knew the business had been sold to someone else. When Mr. Walmsley said "I will see you right," he was speaking about Mr. McCabe going bail. Mr. Walmsley would deny writing any such letter as was supposed to be lost to the effect that he had taken or the business of his son. Mr. Walmsley knew nothing at all about dentistry._</p>
        <p>Mr. Walmsley strongly denied liability and supported counsel's statement in detail, as also did his wife.</p>
        <p>His Honour gave judgment for the defendant with costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>FORTY/ FOOT FALL.</head>
        <p>A FORTY/ FOOT FALL.</p>
        <p>Warrington Man's Death in the</p>
        <p>Infirmary.</p>
        <p>An inquest, conducted by Mr. S. Brighouse, was held at the local Coroner's Court on Mon- day afternoon' when the circumstances attend- ing the death of Ralph Barlow, boilermaker, 20, Tanners-lane, were inquired into. Thomas Barlow, a boilermaker, who lived with his father, said the deceased was 47 years of age and was employed by Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Ltd. About 6.20 a.m. on Tuesday, July 21st, deceased left home to go to his work and was then in his Usual good health. At 7.30 the same morning witness was informed that his father had met with an accident and had been taken to the Infirmary. When wit- ness visited the institution Dr. Law informed him that his father was not likely to get better, as he had a broken back. On several occasions deceased told witness how the accident hap- pened. He said he was employed covering a roof with corrugated iron, and as he -was pul- ling up one of the sheets over a single block he fell off the plank on 'to the floor, a distance of 40 feet. Deceased died at 4.45 last Saturday afternoon.</p>
        <p>The Coroner: Did your father blame anyone? —No, sir.</p>
        <p>STANDING ON NINE-INCH PLANK.</p>
        <p>John James Sedgley, boilermaker, employed at the same works, said the deceased and him- self were both fixing a corrugated roof on a building in course of erection—an extension of the cement plant. Deceased was about 40 feet from the ground, and was standing on a nine- inch plank, which was passed across two iron girders. Deceased was pulling one of the sheets up when the accident happened. The Coroner: What weight are these sheets? Mr. Whittingham (foreman beilerma:“.o : About half a cwt.</p>
        <p>The Coroner : I would not care for the job myself, you know, but I suppose you are accus- tomed to it. Sedgley, continuing, said as the deceased was lifting up the sheet his (witness's) attention was drawn away, and the next thing he saw was the deceased falling. In reply to the Coroner, witness said he surmised that the wind got under the sheet and caused the deceased to lose his balance. The Coroner: There was nothing there for him to get hold of to save himself?—No, .ir. Was he a very experienced man?-oh, yes. ,In reply to further questions witness said they were building in the ordinary way.</p>
        <p>ONE OF THE BEST CLIMBERS. James Whittingham said deceased was one of their best climbers and had been with the firm five or six years. The Coroner: I suppose you have to leave these men to use their own judgment to some extent P—Certainly. Witness said the usual way was to lift the sheets up from the side, but he did not know why deceased went into the centre, and in answer to a juror he stated that deceased went there on his own initiative. The Coroner &gt; (to the son): Have you any com- plaint to make about it? Your father was an experienced man and knew what he had to do? —He used to do nothing else, but go about for Messrs. Pearson and Knowles erecting these places. He used to be in charge. A verdict of "Accidental death" was re- turned</p>
      </div>
