        <p>MASO:</p>
        <p>CLAttKE'S B 41 PILLS can be relied upon to cure in either sex all acquired or constitutional di.- charges from the Urinary Organs, Gravel and Paine in the back. Free from Mercury. Established up. wards of 50 years. In boxes 4e. 6d, each, of all chemists and Patent Medicine Vendors throughout the world, or sent for sixty stamps by the makeze.— The Lincoln and Midland Counties Drug Co.. Lin- ooln.—Advt.</p>
        <p>eN'er duty might come to us in this great mat- ter. It would mean for all of us many a self- denial. It would involve many a curtailment of pleasure. We should have to go in for cheap living; for diminishing personal and domestic expenditure. We should have to do without many things which ve prized and en- joyed, to help the sad, the sick, the bereaved.</p>
        <p>The Destruction of War.</p>
        <p>"It is no use living in a, fool's paradise!" concluded the preacher. "We are in for hard times until, in the merciful providence of God, this ghastly nightmare is dispelled and destroyed, and the nations can come to peace in the restoration of reason and in the Empire of Christ. But, be of good cheer! 'Lord, open the eyes of our people that they may see!' Let every Christian echo Elisha's prayer, seeking to become such a ' seer ' as Elisha was—the servant of God, who can see the divine forces always around the Christian, greater and mightier than all the enemy. This ghastly war may be leading on the day when all human tyrannies shall be overthrown, and Peace and Brotherhood finally set up!</p>
        <p>„ "Meanwhile let us play the man—the Chris- tian man—taking our individual part as Christ would have us do; praying for the peace of the world, for the hastening of the destruction of war, for the time to come when God will break in nieces the oppressor; working for the healing of the nations; bringing the Heavenly Kingdom in!”</p>
        <p>• 44 , ,</p>
        <p>people, and which threatened to bind other peoples against their will. They were fight- ing the system of militarism in its worst form, not the German people.. Therefore they might Jove their enemies. They could honestly say that their object was not one of selfish gain at the expense of another .nation, but rather that the whole of the civilised world might gain. And they hoped that the end of this war might see the end of the curse of that growing evil, militarism, with its sin and folly -and waste of vast expenditure upon aimanients and human slaughtering-machines. It was because of this that the iviiole of humanity stood to gain by the success of our arms; the truth of which was proved by the fact that most of those who had, before the war, been anxious for neutrality, had become, now that the facts had been disclosed, ardent sup- porters of their cause and that of their allies. Was not all this something to be thankful for? They were thereby free from the least suspicion of shame, and rid of that haunting nightmare of uneasiness which a cause of doubt- ful justice would bring.</p>
        <p>The Nation's Calmness.</p>
        <p>Secondly, they could thani:Go7l-lor the calm- ness and trust which He had given in a time of great anxiety, not due to over-confidence, but to a real trust in Him as the defender of the right. This had been wonderfully illustrated when the banks had been opened after their being closed for nearly a week. The calm trust of their people surprised even the most sanguine; and when they remembered that, at the least sign of panic, their cause would have been ruined almost before they had begun, surely they could thank God for such trust. Might that trust continue, either in defeat or victory!</p>
