      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>Self-sacrifice.</head>
        <p>Self-sacrifice.</p>
        <p>Lastly, they couicl-b-e-iliniciul for the general self-sacrifice which was being made throughout the Empire by every class of people. Every colony was eager-to spare no effort, in order to assiSt the cause of freedom against the hand of oppression. Their personal sacrifice must be a real one. Let them not merely transfer their usual subscription to some other charity to the War Fund. That would be no extra sacrifice, and their ordinary charities would suffer enough as it was. If, during the war, they could see to it that the War Fund was not raised at their expense, then they would also have cause to render to God a heart-felt thanic- f ulness.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>ST. MARY'S, WARRINGTON. Father Wilson and the War.</head>
        <p>ST. MARY'S, WARRINGTON. Father Wilson and the War.</p>
        <p>"VENGEANCE HAS COAIE." Last Sunday there was read at all the services a short letter from the Archbishop of Liverpool, directing that until the end of the war there should be offered daily prayer proper for the occasion; moreover, that to-morrow (Sunday) should be observed with special solemnity as a day of general supplication. Father Wilson made the letter an occasion for a short address to .his flock on the war.</p>
        <p>A war, he said, had- commenced, in which well-nigh half the world was involved. Upon ourselves war had been forced. We could not have stood aside without breaking the faith we had pledged to others and bringing upon our- selves indelible shame and dishonour. From the facts as known to the public he thought we need have no misgivings as to the justice of our cause. But the justice of our caug3 did not pre- vent the war from being an appalling scourge. We should recognise it as an awful and well. deserved chastisement from the hand of an offended God. Anyone who observes and reflects must acknowledge that of recent years the nation had rapidly drifted far from God, had ignored and.forgotten Him, had abandoned His worship and its own Christian traditions. It had largely become secularist in its ideals and legislation. The well-to-do had become pos- sessed by avarice, luxuriousness and childish frivolity, and consequently the peor and help- less had been ground down and enslaved. These, continued the preacher, are sins cry- ing to Heaven for vengeance, and vengeance has come. Lentil •how futile and frail is the whole edifice of human society and institutions unless it is based on the practical recognition of God's Sovereignity and the maintenance of Christian ideals. Undoubtedly it is right we should turn to God at such a time of calamity as this, and beseech Him that it may be shortened. But as we ire brought to remember that He is the God of armies and the arbiter of war, so we must not forget that He is the sender of peace and ruler of prosperity. His arm is never shortened nor His might diminished. Never is He with- drawn from the doings of men. His hand is always upon us to direct or constrain, to chas- tise or to bless. Catholic Attitude. We Catholics have doubtless shared in the national sin and forgetfulness of God. We have failed in degree to act up to Christian and Catholic principles; failed to throw the weight of our influence, our example, our voice, and, at times perhaps, our vote, on the side of Chris- tian and Catholic ideals. Then to the sum of wickedness we have added our own personal sins. As we have shared in the national apostasy, so surely shall we share in its chastisement. ,We must do so with resignation and patience. There is one thing to support us in the hour of trial— the knowledge that our cause is just. Without misgiving and with all confidence, we can be- seech God for victory and beg Him to shorten the days of war. But the first condition of acceptable prayer is repentance. We must humble ourselves under the mighty hand of God, confess our sins, and seek pardon in the Sacrament of Penance. This is our first duty. Our next is to join in the public and united' supplications of the faithful in Mass and Holy Communion and the other prescribed devotions, and also to avoid selfish panic, wreckless and shameful excess; to be careful in the use of our resources, if not for our own sakes, that we may help those who will suffer more elan ourselves; but be assured we shelf all feel in many ways the heavy scourge of the war. Yet another duty iPto subscribe as generously as we can to the National Relief Fund, a collec- tion in aid of which will be made at all the services on Sunday. 23rd inst. We must be will- ing to deny ourselves and make real sacrifice that we shall feel for this object. Then may we be confident that God will hear our prayer and bless our charity and come quickly to our aid. At St. Mary's to-morrow (Sunday) there will be Solemn High Mass at 11 a.m., followed by Exposition of the Blessed Sacrament until the evening service at 6.30 p.m.. which will conclude with a Procession of the Blessed Sacrament.</p>
        <p>/'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>RIAIN OF INTERCESSION.</head>
        <p>RIAIN OF INTERCESSION.</p>
        <p>(Tune, "Melita.") O Lord of lords and King of kings, Beneath the.shelter of Thy wings We gather in Thy holy place To plead for Thy protecting grace ; Stretch forth, 0 Lord, Thy mighty hand, And guard and shind our Fatherland. Thy power was shown in days of old Ity wonders great and manifold; Re with us when the battle lowers,— Thou art our fathers' God and ours; O Lord of hosts, though we forget, Thy mighty arm is round us yet. For King and country, Lord, 'We pray. Re Thou their Refuge and their Stay; Our trust is in Thy power •alone To keep unmoved our ancient throne; - Stretch forth, 0 Lord, Thy mighty hand, And guard Our King and Fatherland. . t oe i battle, From war and perils of the deep • Our sailors, Lord, in safety keep; IPnr ostec rt om th rlteresfoeire'er they go go Stretch forth, 0 Lord, Thy miglitY hand, And guard our sons on sea and land. O Lord, Thy sure protection yield To soldiers on the battle-field; Though fierce the Cdes of battle break, Though rocks be riv'n and mountains shake, Guard Thou our soldiers where they stand, To fight for King and Fatherland. 0 Lord of lords and King of kings, We seek the shelter of Thy wings; We bow to Thy Almighty will, In life, in death, be with us still; Help us, 0 Lora, to bravely stand For dod, for King, and Fatherland. ALFRED CORNEY.</p>
        <p>hymn was sung at the intercession Irervaibcoe above at Wycliffe Church, Warrington, yesterday. Copies are', on sale at "The Examiner" Office. Price, 2s: 3d. rei 109.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>WORKERS AND THE WAR</head>
        <p>WORKERS AND THE WAR</p>
        <p>Local Socialists Anticipate a Revolution in Gmunany. AUTOCRACY it. DEMOCRACY.</p>
        <p>BY "A WORKER."</p>
        <p>_ Little did Labourists think a few weeks ago that one of their tenets wotld have to be abandoned, and that such an abandonment— temporary, of course, it must be—would be occasioned by the European conflagration now in progress. It is just about five weeks since the Warrington Trades and Labour Council re- ceived a communication from the German Social Democratic party renewing the pledge to strive to maintain peace in Europe. The Trades Council endorsed the proposal with enthu- siasm, but, alas! "the best laid schemes o' mice 'and men gang aft agley," and now we are in the midst of the greatest conflict in arms the world has ever known. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>TRADE UNIONISTS' VIEWS._</head>
        <p>TRADE UNIONISTS' VIEWS._</p>
        <p>Amongst our 10-cal Socialists there a few who stick to the Ramsay Macdonald belief that the war should at any price have been avoided. So far as I can find, they, as regards Warrington, are in a minority. Amongst the members of the Independent Labour party a quite unex- pected pro-war spirit prevails, and when m e come to the rank and file of the trade unionists one finds the great majority decidedly in favour of the action taken by the Government. In fact, there are hundreds of Warrington trade unionists now up for active service with our Regulars and Territorials, and others are vol- untarily joining the new force in course of formation.</p>
        <p>" Politics we have dropped for the time," said one local trade unionist to me this week. "I am a peace man, but now we are at war I remember that even if I am a Socialist I am first of all a patriot. The bulk of my fellows hold exactly the same opinion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>AUTOCRACY v. DEMOCRACY.</head>
        <p>AUTOCRACY v. DEMOCRACY.</p>
        <p>" War is a curse to the workers, but times occ"asionally do arise when we have to swallow the curse and make the best of the situation, and this war is an apt illustration. We work- ers know what it means to us—loss of life, shortace of work, reduction in our trade union funds, but we are not now going to go back when our country needs our aid. The Nation- alists have set us an example we are proud to follow." "It seems rather a strange theory to come from a Socialist," I ventured to remark. "Not so. The question has become this: Shall the autocrats rule the .world, or shall the democrats be the masters? That is my personal view, and many hold a similar opin- ion. If autocracy triumphs, God help the workers; if, on the other hand, democracy triumphs, we may soon reach that era of inter- national peace which has been the Socialistic dream for many years."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>GERMAN REVOLUTION.</head>
        <p>A GERMAN REVOLUTION.</p>
        <p>It is rather remarkable that there are nearly two millions in Germany who hold the same opinion as our own Labourists. Speaking upon this .point, the party interviewed said : It seems singular that two million Ger- mans are out for the same thing as ourselves— the smashing of the autocratic military Imperialism which has been the curse of Ger- many under the present rule. It may be true many of these are now on the battlefield, but their heart is not in the war. They are really driven into the firing line at the point of the swords of the autocrats, who are the officers in the German Army. They will rebel at the first opportunity, that is certain. ' "There is a good deal more in the scrappy news which has come through from Berlin about the mass of the people being in a grave state of unrest, than most people suppose. 'Stop that war V I am sure, is a popular cry with the masses of the German people. We do not know what is happening, but I think I am safe in assuming that the Social Democrats are not idle. I would not be surprised to hear of a revolution in Germany. Matters must now be worse there than we can realise, and I for one would not be surprised to hear startling news before long."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>WHAT GERMAN WORKERS THINK.</head>
        <p>WHAT GERMAN WORKERS THINK.</p>
        <p>In local Labour circles the feeling is growing that the workers of Germany will yet create trouble. The great strikes of the past few years show how the workers look upon the autocratic rule. In fact it is not more than a couple of years since I heard a leader of the German Social Democratic party state at a pub- lic meeting in Manchester that should Britain and Germany ever come to war grips the democracy of Britain would in time find a powerful ally in the workers of the German Empire. It is early days yet, such a move- ment as that foreshadowed will take time to put into operation, but the general opinion amongst Warrington workers is that Germany may ere long be confronted with a revolution. If it is true that prominent Socialist leaders have been shot, such a shooting may mark the dawn of the workers' revolt. According to a Cardiff lady, whose husband is a German official in Berlin, and who has escaped from that capital, the people are in a state of turmoil, and our local Socialists are noting this as a sign of the coming of " The Day" for the amelioration of the. German</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>SIGNIFICANT LETTER TO WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>A SIGNIFICANT LETTER TO WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Significant news has come through from Bel- gium this week to the effect that the Social Democrats there have got into touch with their German colleagues, and that the latter are now in a state of open revolt against the war. Of course, no confirmation can he had from Germany, but an English refugee who escaped from Germany early this week has written to a Warrington friend, and in his letter gives what looks like ample confirmation. When passing through Bremen, he says, he saw 50,000 workers attend a demonstration to protest against the autocratic-made war. At Hamburg he saw a procession of over 100,000 German workers marching through the streets shouting "Stop the war," and "Down with the kaiser." This veluntary statement of the Eng- lish refugee must be accepted as containing the truth, and it indicates that the Kaiser ~has enemies within as well as without the Gerisan empire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>CHARGED UNDER OFFICIAL SECRETS ACT.</head>
        <p>CHARGED UNDER OFFICIAL SECRETS ACT.</p>
        <p>‘Varringtoln Pollee Arrest a Man at Widnes.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Borough Police Court on Monday, before the Mayor (Councilhar Peter Peacock) and other magistrates, Harold Dut- ton, barman, 71, Victoria-road, Widnes, was charged under the Official Secrete Acts with, on the 11th day of August, 1914, being a person who had held office under His Majesty, un- lawfully did have retained in his possession a certain document which he had no right to retain.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable (gr. Martin Nicholls) said the prisoner was charged under the portion of the Act where it was made an offence to retain'any sketch, plan, or other document., It was nOt proposed to offer any evidence because a communication had been received from the War Office directing that an eight days' ad- journment should be requested, and that only evidence of arrest should be :e.biered. Superintendent Williamson said he arrPsteo prisoner at Widnes and brought him to War- rington, where, after warning him, he rhArgsd him and received an answer adm:tting the offence.</p>
        <p>------- - The Bench agreed to an eight days remand, and the Chief Constable remarked that pro- bably a similar request would be thade again.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY,</p>
        <p>GERMAN TRADE.</p>
        <p>Warrington Manufacturers to Join in its Capture.</p>
        <p>CHAMBER OF COMMERCE ACTION</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Council, held at Market- gate Chambers on Thursday, Mr. F. A. Frost in the chair, the, Secretary (Alderman Bennett) explained that the President, Captain George R. Crosfield, was now on active service with the Territorials.</p>
        <p>The Secretary read a communication which had been received in reference to the insurance of war risks by the Government, and the Coun- cil expressed its warm appreciation of the scheme, coupled with the hope that the rates would be kept as low as possible. He added that he lfad received particulars of various openings for trade which had arisen in consequence of the European situation and that he was circulating these amongst the members interested.</p>
        <p>_ It was proposed by Mr. E. James Hall, sec- onded by Mr. R. T. Fairclough, and resolved: " That the Council desire to call the particular and immediate attention of all the members of the Chamber to the factqhat, owing to the paralysis of German and Austrian trade, in consequence of the war, an exceptional oppor- tunity has been afforded to the commercial com- munity in this locality to endeavour, to secure a portion of the same; and advises any local manufacturers who may be interested at once to communicate with the Commercial Intelli- gence Department of the Board of Trade, 73, Basinghall-street. London, 8.C., who have inti- mated their willingness to furnish every pos- sible assistance.</p>
        <p>The Secretary called attention to the fact that the British Imperial Council of Commerce was now established upon a definite basis, and that the Warrington Chamber had been invited to join.</p>
        <p>It wa unanimously resolved that the Cham- ber should accept the invitation. He also reported that, in consequence of the war with Germany and Austria; the autumnal meeting of the Associated Chambers had not been held; and that members of six firms con- nected with the Chamber had been appointed to represent it on the local committee for the ad- ministration of the Prince of Wales' Relief Fund.</p>
        <p>It was resolved that the Council record their deep regret at the loss which the Chamber has sustained by the death of Mr. James Brandreth and Mr. James Richardson, sen., and their sin- cere sympathy with the respective families in their sad bereavement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>IMPROVING 'TRADE.</head>
        <p>IMPROVING 'TRADE.</p>
        <p>The Rule Is—Keep Industries Going.</p>
        <p>Trade is steadily recovering from the partia) confusion into which it naturally fell at the outbreak of the war, says the " Times." The measures taken bY the Government, the bankers, and the Committee of the Stock Exchange in relation to home defence, finance, and com- merce, have animated the business community with the greatest confidence. There has not been that general stoppage of work and wages which was anticipated a fort- night ago. The rule is—Keep industries going, and diminish the field of unemployment as far as possible. Manufacturers and business firms whose returns show a falling off are utilising their reserves to retain their workers on condi- tions which, under the circumstances,- are fair and reasonable to both sides; and meantime, by adapting themselves to the changed situation, they are seeking new sources of trade. They look forward to better times when part of the £100,000,000, secured by the Government on the vote of credit, comes into general circulation; and the expansion of home, colonial, and foreign trade comes as the result of the closing of Ger- man markets.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>TRADE PRESS AND THE OUTLOOK. At a meeting of the Trade Press, held on Monday in London, the following resolution was adopted :</head>
        <p>TRADE PRESS AND THE OUTLOOK. At a meeting of the Trade Press, held on Mon- day in London, the following resolution was adopted :</p>
        <p>That this meeting of editors of trade journals representing most of the industries of the coun- try, after studying the present commercial pro- blems, has come to the following conclusions : That our commercial prospects were never brighter, and that given patience, confidence, and reasonable sacrifice, there is certain to be a splendid return in the early future. That we strongly urge (a) the public to pay prompt cash for their present requirements; (b) the retailer not to take unfair advantage of the moratorium; (c) .the manufacturer to keep up his production; and (d) the banks to afford all possible support to both wholesaler and retailer. That we request the Controller of Patents to exercise any power he may possess for revok- ing any British patents owned by hostile coun- tries should they operate against British in- dustry. -</p>
        <p>The Lord Mayor has been informed by the Federation of Master Printers and Allied Trades of the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland that his appeal not to discharge em- ployes has met with the most sympathetic re- sponse. But they say it is obviously impossible to continue payment of wages for long if pay- ment of accounts and orders for work are with- held through any mistaken idea of assisting the community.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>ORIGIN OF THE EXPEDITIONARY FORCE.</head>
        <p>ORIGIN OF THE EXPEDITIONARY FORCE.</p>
        <p>The Expeditionary Force in its present form is the creation of Lord Haldane and his ad- visers, who remodelled the whole British Army system in 1906. Lord Haldane, besides reducing the numbers of the Regular Army by 20,000 men and founding the Territorial system, organiSed the Home Army into an Expeditionary Force consisting in round numbers of 150,000 men. Nothing can be said, of course, as to the nuni- bers or composition of the British troops which are now on the Continent, but it may be øf interest to describe the numbers and organisa- tion of the Expeditionary Force as planned by Lord Haldane in.time of peace. Its composition was to be as follows : Regulars Reservists Men trained on Militia basis and under obligation to come out on mobilisation 30,000 , 150,000 _</p>
        <p>Made up as follows : Infantry— Guards 6 Divisions, each , composed of 3 Line Brigades. • Cavalry 4 Brigades. Artillery— Horse 10 Batteries. Field 63 Batteries. In describing the organisation of the new. force Lord Haldane used these words :</p>
        <p>"SOMETHING REAL AND TANGIBLE." •"What one wants is something real and tangible; something that does exist there in time of peace; something of which the battalions and the cavalry and the guns and the men are there and available in time of war, and have to , be brought from a very short distance. What we have thought best for the purpose, after four months' close consideration, is 'an organisation, not in army corps, and not in small divisions such as exist now, of which we could have nine out of the materials we have to keep at home for maintaining our drafts abroad, but six big divisions—l am talking of infantry—organised on a pattern to correspond with our forces in India. We think it important to make our army here accord as nearly and as closely as possible with the organisation in India,.in case —which Heaven forbid I—our forces should have to act together in some great emergency in de- fence of the Indian frontier • upon Indian plains."</p>
        <p>Alles on Death Certificates. Mr. S. Brighouse, the coroner, had some strong comments to make at an inquest in War- rington on Wednesday about people giving wrong ages for the death certificates so as to benefit through the insurance. At one case at Bootle, he said, the death of a person, upon whom he held an inquest, was given as 49 years. Later he found that the person was an old-age pensioner, and inquiries showed that he was 80 years, ,of age.. If he got any similar cases in Warrington or elsewhere he would tee that the person giving the -wrong age was committed for perjury. He hoped what he.said would be a warning.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>MUSICAL FESTIVAL.</head>
        <p>MUSICAL FESTIVAL.</p>
        <p>' Working Men's Committee Effort for Local War Fund.</p>
        <p>SOME CHARMING MUSIC.</p>
        <p>11 A very substantial amount was raised for local war funds as the result of the musical festival held in Bank Park and the concert in the Hippodrome on Sunday. As an- nounced in Saturday's "Examiner," the festi- valovas arranged by the Working Men's Com- mittee of the Infirmary for the benefit of that institution, but, in view of the present crisis, the committee generously decided that the pro- ceeds should be given to a local war fund. The concert in the Hippodrome was given by Messrs. Crosfield's Band, who also submitted an excel- lent programme in the Park. The public re- sponded to the appeal in a most encouraging fashion, and long before the singing of the first 'hymn by the united mixed 'choir of over 100 voices and the audience, the Park presented a crowded appearance.</p>
        <p>Everyone was delighted with the excellent manner in which the choir, which had been drawn from the Nonconformist choirs in the •town, rendered the appropriate selections under the conductorship of Mr. W. H. Payton. The programme included the choruses "Send out Thy Light" iGouno(1), "He watching over Israel" (Mendelssolin), "0 Gladsome Light" (Sullivan), "Comrades' Song of Hope" (Adams), "0 taste and see" (Goss), "0 Father whose almighty power" (Handel), and "By Babylon's Wave " (Gounod). The accompanists, Mr. J. W. Jowett and Mr. T. Tanner, discharged their duties in a very creditable manner, as also did Mr. A. Morris, as hon. secretary. Crosfield's Band, under the able conductorship of Mr. J. C. Taylor, maintained their reputation in the playing of the following items: "Honest Toil" (Rimmer), "La Rose de Peronne " (A. Adam), " Tannhauser " selection (Wagner), and Handel's "Hallelujah Chorus." The cornet solo "Abide with me" was played with much feel- ing by Mr. J. Blackburn, and the trombone solo " Thom " by Mr. J. E. Hacking was also well received.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>MAYOR'S THANKS.</head>
        <p>MAYOR'S THANKS.</p>
        <p>The Mayor and Mayoress, as well as Sir Gil- bert Greenall, Alderman Burton (chairman Working Men's Committee), Mr. (it. Walker (vice-chairman), and Mr. J. H. J. Hampson (hon. secretary), were amongst those present. At an interval the Mayor thanked the band and choir for their services, and Messrs. Peter Walker and Son for erecting the platform from which the choir sang. He was also pleased that the public had assembled in such large numbers, and that the Working Men's Committee had de- cided' to devote the proceeds to the local war fund. He felt sure the public would remem- ber that act, and at a futfire time would extend to the committee all the support they deserved. (Applause.) ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>HIPPODROME PERFORMANCE.</head>
        <p>HIPPODROME PERFORMANCE.</p>
        <p>An Attractive Programme.</p>
        <p>The Hippodrome, which had been placed at the disposal of the organisers of the concert by Mr. Macnaghten, was crowded in the evening, and excellent services were tendered by Mr. A. Turpin and his staff. The Mayor and Mayoress and Sir Gilbert'and Lady Greenell were amongst those present. One of the best pieces played by the band was the descriptive selection, "A Sailor's Life" (Cope), given with great effect. The other items were: "Le Marseillaise " (Rim- mer), "Light Cavalry" (Suppi), "Nabucco " (Verdi), "National Songs of Great Britain" fantasia, "And the glory" chorus (Handel). Mr. J. E. Hacking played "The Trumpeter" in a pleasing manner on the trombone, and " Baby- lon " was the selection chosen by Mr. J. Black- burn for his cornet solo.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>LADY GREENALL'S SPEECH.</head>
        <p>LADY GREENALL'S SPEECH.</p>
        <p>Lady Greenall addressed the audience at an interval, and expressed the appreciation they all felt of Messrs: Crosfield's Band for giving their services to such a worthy cause. It had been a magnificent performance, for the men had played with their whole heart and soul. (Hear, hear.) The reason was that they had a cause for which to play—a cause for which they had never played before, and for which they hoped they would never have to play again. (Hear,, hear.) After having thanked the con- ductor for his valuable services, she thanked Mr. Afacnaghten for having placed the Hippo- drome at their disposal, and said they knew how kind he was on all ocAsions, and so also was Mr. Turpin, to whom they were again indebted for the excellent arrangements made for their com- fort. Mr. Turpin had a great deal of work to do, but lie was always willing to assist a good cause. On behalf of the Mayor, she thanked the audience for the support they had given to the fund, and expressed the hope that their in- terest in it would be continuous. Such a fund must have the support of every man, woman or child who had the welfare of the country at heart. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>" For King and Country." The other day the Warrington Territorials left the town with smiling faces because they knew they were going to fight for their King and country. (Hear, hear.) It was the duty of those who were at home to see that the families of those who had'gone out did not suffer, and also see to those who had been thrown out of employment. They must not in any way in- crease the anxieties of those who had dear ones at the front, and she asked them not to repeat rumours of ill news that came out, for one never knew what the result of such carelessness might be. They should, however, tell good news as quickly as ever they could. (Hear, hear.) The circulating of unfounded reports was un- kind and unjust.</p>
        <p>After appealing to the audience to further support the fund, she thanked the Mayor for the very deep interest -le was taking in that great work. She was sure he would have the support of all the townspeople, who were deter- mined that the name of Warrington should be honoured for the amount it contributed to the war funds. Warrington was one of the most im- portant towns in Lancashire, and they must lire up to their reputation for being at the fore- front in helping and supporting all good causes. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>NET BALANCE OF £44. A Help for the War Fund. , The following balance sheet of the Musical Festival in Bank Park and Crosfield's Band per- formance at the Hippodrome on Sunday was submitted to the oonimittee meeting last night. Income.</p>
        <p>Amount collected in the Park, £43 is. 11d.; amount received from Mr. Arthur W. Turpin, Hippodrome, £l5 18s. 3d.; Lady Greenall's dona- tion, £2; total, £6l Os. 2d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>Expenditure.</head>
        <p>Expenditure.</p>
        <p>Mackie and Co., Ltd., printing, posting and advertising, .t 5 15s. 6d.; J. W% a lker and Co., Ltd., printing and advertising, ..62 15s. 3d.; " War- rington Observer," advertising, 3s. 6d.; C. H. J. Dawson, purchase of music for choirs, ..66 3s. 7d.; C. H. J. Dawson, hire of piano, 12s. 6d.; I. H. .J. Hampson, postage, hire of rehearsal room and miscellaneous small expenses, „tl 2s. 5d.; total, .tl6 12s. 9d.</p>
        <p>A Handsome Balance. Consequently there remains a balance for dis- tribution of ..04 7s. sd.</p>
        <p>mailmia</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>CHURCHES AND THE WAR,</head>
        <p>CHURCHES AND THE WAR,</p>
        <p>CHRISTIAN INTERCESSION</p>
        <p>Warrington Churches and a Speedy Ending of the War. HISTORIC SERVICES.</p>
        <p>'yesterday was a day of intereessipn for a speedy ending of the war, and services were held in a number of Warrington churches.\ The Nonconformists met in Wycliffe Church, and the Established Churches in the borough also held: services, most of the latter congregations meeting daily for prayer and guidance during the present crisis. The Catholic Churches hold their intercession day to-morrow (Sunday), although daily a Mass is being aecited for the safety of the British soldiers at the front.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>SERVICE AT WYCLIFFE.</head>
        <p>SERVICE AT WYCLIFFE.</p>
        <p>Conducted by the Rev. John 'lunge.</p>
        <p>Yesterday morning a special intercession ser- vice was held at Wycliffe Congregational Church, which was ctnducted by the Rev. John Yonge. Special portions of Scripture were read. and suitable hymns, including Mr. Alfred Corney's specially written Hymn of Interces- sion.</p>
        <p>The Rev. James Watson, of Jamaica, delivered the address upon the text "Abide in Me, and I in you" (John xv.), and said that the kingdoms of this earth belonged to Christ, and could never belong to the German Kaiser. In upholding Christ's kingdom, we must remember that 7.rstyPr was the chief method of Worlc for God. Mr. Yonge and others led the prayers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>SCHOLARS' INTERCESSION.</head>
        <p>SCHOLARS' INTERCESSION.</p>
        <p>Yesterday there was an inapressive interces- sion service at Arpley-street Council School. All the ckildren attended and joined in inter- cessionary prayers. An address was given by Mr. Wallington, the head master. The service was more impres4ing from the fact that many of the children had immediate relatives on service in different sections of His Majesty's Forces.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>KAISER'S PERFIDY.</head>
        <p>THE KAISER'S PERFIDY.</p>
        <p>Rev. John Yonge and the Overthrow of Human Tyrannies.</p>
        <p>Preaching at Wycliffe Congregational Church on Sunday evening, the Rev. John Yonge took for his text, 11. Kings vi. 17: "And Elisha prayed, and said, Lord, I pray thee, open his eyes that he may see! And the Lord opened the eyes of the young man, and he saw! And, behold, the mountain was full of horses and chariocS of fire, round about Elisha." The preacher said that he would accept the essential theme of the incident as setting forth valuable and helpful teaching for the present national predicament. What the passage here declared of the individual, it also, essentially, declared of the people as a nation, who in any true sense rested their supreme confidence in God.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>Divine Assistance.</head>
        <p>Divine Assistance.</p>
        <p>The man who feared and honoured God could never be left, slavishly and alone, to the mete forte of outward circumstance. However much outward things might seem, 6 a be- liever in God, to be adverse—even perilously so—always, in reality, greater and stronger forces were on his side. Such was the repeated assurance of the Divine Book, and such also was the abundantly proved experience of God- believing men. Let them rememoer that, Lad as things might sometimes seem, they could never be desperate, never dark with impene- trable gloom, never, hopeless, to the man or to the nation whose hope was fixed in God. The enemy might gather in a terrific host, with countless swarms of allies, and they might compass the object of their hostility on every side; but let not the man or the people, who were so beleagured, be really dismayed! The forces of help Which were on the side of the threatened were "more than they" which were against him. Neither the man nor the nation wak ever left helplessly exposed to the hostile forces of temporal circumstance, whose supreme affiance was in God. Ever was there, " round about," an invisible but invincible protection! The experience of the prophet Elisha might just convey the encouragement and comfort that they supremely needed in this troubled hour. England hltd been seriously, menaced before, but never so terribly as now, with the enemy at the very gates! The thing must seem to them almost incredible! But that the national leaders deemed it credible was clear from the large demands which were being made for the reception and treatment of soldiers and sailors who, close to our coasts, may be wounded in battle.</p>
        <p>Our island home was to become, or might possibly become, a "hospital" all round its coasts, and also even in the larger inland towns and cities. These were facts which made us understand that the near, almost instant future might be full of manifold danger, and of incal- culable difficulty.. Might God inspire us with necessary courage and fortitude, wherewith to endure calmly and bradely whatever might betide! _ _</p>
        <p>Our Unselfish Cause.</p>
        <p>Thank God! we had at least the certain assurance, and complete confidence, that the cause in which we were suddenly called to pre- pare to "endure hardness," and even to engage in conflict, had no tinge or taint of selfishness!, We, at least, had no selfish end to foster and to 83rve, but Were taking our stand, and making ready to fight, in fidelity to truth and honour. Our nation had pledged itself to defend the safety and the independence of the brave little nation of Belgium against all-comers, and we were therefore bound to resist the cold, heart- less selfishness of the great German power— which was equally bound in all those respects with ourselves. Germany, however, had broken the el:lmpact. For her own end she had spread wholesale destruction upon the little kingdom she was absolutely pledged to protect. Did we wonder at the scathing scorn of our honest and intrepid Premier? The Emperor William had invaded peticeful Belgium—a State which, as he (the preacher) had said, he was, by the same treaty as ourselves, bound to protect. Not because he had any cause what- ever of quarrel with that country, but simply because it bravely refused to let his army pass through its borders to attack the French. So, as Mr. Asquith finely said in his recent address in Parliament, "we are fighting, in the first place, to fulfil a solemn international obliga- tion, which, if it had been entered into be- tween private persons in the ordinary concerns of life, would have been regarded as an obli- gation not only of law, but of honour, which no self-respecting man could possibly have re- pudiated. Secondly, we are fighting to vindi- cate the principle—in these days when material force sometimes seems to be the dominant in- fluence and factor in the development of man- kind—that small nationalities are not to be crushed in defiance of international good faith, by the arbitrary will of a strong, overmaster- ing Power. We have a great duty to perform; we have a great trust to fulfil; and confidently we believe that Parliament and .the country will enable us to do it."</p>
        <p>Our Premier.</p>
        <p>He had thought it -,l;;i'i;bie to quote these words to them because they 'mist go to dismiss from their minds, once and for all, any hesita- tion they might have felt to assent to the policy which had carried the nation into war. It must deeply interest them as Nonconformists, and especially Congregationalists, to find that the speaker of the words he had just read was himself born a Nonconformist and a Congrega- tionalist. All his hereditary proclivities would make him the last to endorse a warlike policy, or to lead his countrymen into a costly and possibly sanguinary strife. But Mr. Asquith saw no way out of the course he had undertaken. He called it "a great duty" and "a great trust." He spoke as a religious man, as a hater of was, as a friend of humanity. He! made them see that Great Britain was to fight, in this war, for righteousness between nation. She was unsheathing her sword to vindicate international honour! The Consequences? What the consequences in suffering and in death might be no one could foresee. They would be grave and great. For how long none could predict. "The beginning of strife is as the letting forth of Water," and it was impossible that we could ever foresee the end of this thing, from 'the beginning. We owed it, however, to 1 the God in whom we trusted, to accept what.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>WAR BEFORE SHAME.</head>
        <p>WAR BEFORE SHAME.</p>
        <p>Re. H. Williamson Outlines the Nonconformist Position.</p>
        <p>The Rev. H. Williamson selected as his text on Sunday evening at Bold-street Wesleyan Church the phrase, "The Prince of Peace." In speaking of his intention to say a few words on the subject of the war, the reverend gentle- man 'said that his reluctance to broach such a topic was only outweighed by his duty as. a Christian, and by his desire, as a patriot, to do all in his power to help his country in her hour of need. The present unhappy state of affairs had raised great and vital questions in regard to our faith and Christian practice. First, the point must be considered as to our justification for engaging in war, and the re- conciliation of our actions with our Christian profession. For two thousand years 'Christ had reigned as the Prince of Peace; and the great nations of the world who named His name, were now at war. Well might the question be asked, Was the Prince of Peace worthy of , the name, and had He any victories to record? Peace was undoubtedly the ideal; the dream of men before the Advent There were many who pointed to the virtues of war; to the en- durance, 'courage land fortitudp inspired by armed conflict. These by-products must be admitted to exist.</p>
        <p>Even burglary had similar bye-products, and provided scope for the training of character. ,War was unideal and hideous, and there was no end to the crimes which might be coin- mitted in its name. So long as victory was assured all was fair in the ethics of war. Final Conquest of Peace.</p>
        <p>. _ As a people we had come into the horrible immorality, and the ludicrous folly of armed conflict. Did this mean that all that men had done for the promotion of peace had gone for naught? Did God care for peace, and, if so. had He power to control the actions of the powers of darkness? If there had been no ad- vance in that direction, then God did not care and therefore He could not be the God of those who worshipped peace and loved it. To say there was no advance was absurd in the light of facts. The parable of the leaven hid in the three measures of meal illustrated the position admirably. It was not a question as to whether the whole had been leavened, but was the leaven working? Was peace a living force in the life of nations? The fact that Sir: Edward Grey had persevered for years in the: promotion of peace, even though without suc- cess in the present issue, proved that peace counted fort a great deal. Even at that moment men were earnestly seeking after a settlement. To the seventeenth century states- man peace was a pure accident in foreign policy and never did it become a positive ambition. To-day there was a different spirit in Inter- national politics. The Hague Peace Conven- tions were a signal triumph of the spirit of peace, and all proved that the leaven was at work. The war was not the central and essen- tial fact. Some of the meal had not yet been transformed; but we could confidently look forward to the final conquest of peace.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>Our Vindication.</head>
        <p>Our Vindication.</p>
        <p>The next question to be answered was whe- ther we, as Christians, could back England in the war. The simplest and most convincing description of the position was to liken it to that of a big bully thrashing a defenceless woman. There was the unwarlike and innocent com- munity of Belgium; and along came a great armed nation to trample it out of existence. It was England's duty to prevent such an action; and she had gone to war with a great moral motive. England might have shirked that duty; but, had that been the case, no Englishman could ever have lifted his head again, and the nation would have borne a name of shame for evermore.</p>
        <p>However great the shock of war might be, it could never shake, the stability of the Empire as much as her standing aside in a cowardly and dastardly manner would have shaken it. A nation was great as it was righteous; and the most hopeful sign of all was that England had not been content to save her own soul, but had been willing to help the weak against the strong. So long as our country was true to righteousness, " Ichabod " could not be written on her gates. If God still had work for Eng- land to do, He would keep her yet; and her people could wait in quietness of soul, doing all in their power by manly behaviour and common-sense to help her in her time of need.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>FIGHTING MILITARISM.</head>
        <p>FIGHTING MILITARISM.</p>
        <p>Rev. .1. M. Buckmaster and the Duty of</p>
        <p>Thankfulness.</p>
        <p>INIPRESSIVE SERVICE AT PARISH CHURCH. Preadhing on the European War at the Pirish Church on Sunday, the Rev. J. M. Buckmaster took for his text: Philippians iv. 6—" With ' thanksgiving let your prayers be made known unto God." The preacher said he proposed to consider one aspect of the present distress, which one was perhaps liable to overlook, namely, the duty of thankfulness. During the past week they had said and heard the same things about the war over and over again, and they were perhaps already a little tired of them. But they knew exactly by now what their duty was, and had made up their mind to help their country by economising, by helping others less fortunate, and (he took it for granted) by prayer. That was 'where they now stood.</p>
        <p>Duty of Thankfulness. • Let them remind themselves of their duty of thankfulness, so apt to be neglected, for it kept them cheerful to think of God's blessings. It might at first sight almost seem that there was precious little to be thankful for. There was hardly one of them but had some friend or relation who had gone away, and whom it was doubtful whether they would ever see again in this world. Could they be thankful? The very fact that thousands of Christians were slaying other Christians was abhorrent, and the more they tried to think what it all meant, the more they pictured to themselves the whole- sale carnage of human life, or, perhaps,worse, the pain suffered by the wounded, lying pro- bably for hours untended, the more sad it was. Try as they would, it was impossible for them fully to realise the horrors of war, and how could they be thankful? With the prospect of poverty and want before many of their poorer neighbours, what room had they for thankful- ness? A Just Cause. First of all, they must remember that they could thank Almighty God, because they were fighting for a just cause. Not that their ene- mies were all consciously fighting for a bad one They must allow that most of the enemy at least believed in the justice of their own cause, mistaken though that belief might be. But they were not fighting with the desire to kill, or to ruin another Christian country. Our opponents were not even the people of Ger- many, for we were not fighting against a people at all.</p>
        <p>Fighting a System. They were fighting- a system which bound a</p>
      </div>
