      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, AUGUST 22, 1914.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, AUGUST 22, 1914.</p>
        <p>BIRTHS. MARRIAGES. DEATHS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>PASSING WEEK.</head>
        <p>THE PASSING WEEK.</p>
        <p>TIIE MAYOR AN ALDERMAN. Councillor Peter Peacock, whO has been making records in all directions as Mayor, was on Tuesday night chosen by his fellow-coun- cillors to take the place as Alderman of the Borough, vacant through, the jamented death of Alderman Forshaw, Councillor Wilkinson was the senior councillor, but exceptional ser- vices to the town appear to have Weighed wish the Council, who, by a majority of fifteen to five, gave the honour to the Mayor. Twenty-one votes were cast. As there are 27 councillors, there were six ,abstentions—Councillors Dicken- son, Barnwell, lienshall, Jenkins, Roberts, and Skelland. Aldermen do not vote at elections of alder- men:</p>
        <p>LABOUR REPRESENTATION ON THE COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>• It was intimated at the monthly/ meeting of the Warrington Trades and Labour,Council on Tuesday night that, the Labour party would be prepared to contest three wards at the muni- cipal elections next November; but it was not stated which three wards would be involved. A hint was given that the advance guards would be seen at work in the. affected areas intrnedi- ately, so that people may judge for themselves of the signs that will be thus provided. There is no doubt that the working classes in War- rington are entitled to representation and that the Council'wduld gain by such representation, It was, for instance, a severe loss to .the town, as well as to the Council, wheip Mr. James Ball was rejected, He and Councillors Stott and Wright did much to show that the Labour party have brains and administrative ability, as well as ambition, and if those selected to follow in their footsteps are as well endowed and care as little for their own persorfal glorifi- cation, we shall all welcome ,their accession..</p>
        <p>community. Sir Walter Scott, refei ring to the citizens in " Quentin Durward," wrote: "It /may be said4that, not excepting the citizens of Ghent, they are at once the fiercest and the most untamable in Europe " ; and later Scott wrote: , " With their breathes unrepaired and their arsenals scarcely supplied, the sight of an archer's bonnett is sufficient to stir them to uproar." If we substitute "German's helmet" for "archer's bonnet" we realise the truth of Sir Walter Scott's words to this day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>NEXT NOVEMBER.</head>
        <p>NEXT NOVEMBER.</p>
        <p>Which are li-11-it-o-ix;iiiTift-t7e-e wards to be tackled by the Labour party? As We have said, we shall have to look out for signs; but here is a list of the sitting members who will retire or appeal for re-election three months hence c</p>
        <p>Town Hall*Ward.—Dr. Fox (C.).• Whitecross Ward.—H. Roberts (I.). Bewsey.Ward.—J. Dickenson (L.). Orford Ward.—J. Halliwell (L.). St. John's Ward.—F. T. Lashford (C.). Fidr.field Ward.—J. G. Craik (I.). Howley Ward.—,T. Chantler (C.). • St. Austin's Ward.—Dr. JOtseph (C.). Latchford Ward.—J. Pemberton ,(C.) Thk is, there will be five Conservative, two Liberal, and two Independent seats from which tO Select:</p>
        <p>The Trade and Labour Intelligence Depart- ment appears, to have been slightly, at fault. It was stated that the questiOn of representation on the Council had arisen because they had been ponsidering the advisability of .contesting the coming vacancy in Orford Ward. They evidently thought Councillor Wilkinson would bb the new Alderman, but he is not, arid there is no kacancy in Orford. They decided not to contest this ward because there would be only a short, unexpired-remnant of time to serve, but Councillor Wilkinson was only elected last November, so there }would have been nearly three years &amp;office for Councillor Wilkinson's successor. But, as we have said, there is no vacancy.</p>
        <p>The Mayor was chosen Alderman. His Worship sits for Winteeross Ward, so that the vacancy will le there;.and the unexpired terrn is only fifteen months.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>NATIONAL ANTHEM.</head>
        <p>THE NATIONAL ANTHEM.</p>
        <p>At p. time when unusual importance attaches , to the singing of the National Anthem, and when it will be frequently on our lips, it is of interest to note that plough it has been a cher- ished heritage for so long, as little is known of its origin as of , the life of that other great nalional asset, Shakespeare. The question of its authorship has not been, and probably never will be, settled. "God save the King " was sung at the Theatre Royal, Drury-lane, in September, 1743, on the receipt "Of the news of the defeat of Sir John Copes army atrPrestonpans, and in the following month' it appeared, for the first time words and music, in the "Gentleman's Magazine' •</p>
        <p>It has been claimed that the author was James Oswald, an Edinburgh musician, because God save the King" was named in 1769 on the dial- plate of Windsor 6hurph " Osweld's are," Oswald having been aPpdinted chamber com- poser to George 111. in 1761. Oswald published under many fictitious names, proving that he was not anxious to make public claim to much eXcellent work; and" God save the King" was published, anonymously, in 1745, by John Simp- sop, for whom Oswald was doing a great deal of work. Dr. John Bull, the 'famous composer, who lived 1563-1628, ,is lipid by some to be the author of the tune as we now know it. Dr, W. 11. Cummings is of, the opinion that the tune is derived from an air by Bull, contained in a, manuscript dated 1610, Bull's tune having been, in the lapse of years, "altered and improved by I he -vox populi, an inevitable and desirable pro- cess in the formation of a national Melody." _ _</p>
        <p>lienry Carey (1602-1743) was originally credited generally with being the composer, but without much aundation. „ It is, however, Universallv. admitted that ours is one of the very finest National Anthems in ,existence. and the melody is used by Germans, Danes; Swiss and Americans. Beethoven,. re- ferring to the introduction of the air into his "Battle Symphony," said:. "I must Show the English a little. what a blessing they have in God save the ".</p>
        <p>A COMPLIMENT FROM THE I FLEET. 3 Mr. • Patrick O'Brien, of 125, Wilderspool- road, writes to us tinder. Wednesday's date "I have just received a letter from my son, E. 11. O'Brien, E.R.A., Hibernia; who is with Admiral Jellicoe's Elect (another son hying on the Africa same Fleet), M vlnchi .10 says . Thanks for the "Examiner." Its map holds a proudly prominent position in our mess, being proniptly commandeered .by one of the messmates and pinned on the notice hoard. It really is the best map we have seen of the affected area. " Ile also !adds that the health of the crew is very 4400 d, and he never knew the men of the -Navy to he. in such splendid spirits, or more quietly cOnfident and determined. thought this item might at the moment interest you and your leaderS."</p>
        <p>HOW 10 COMMUNICATE WITH MEN-SWAVAII. In a P,B;,,Mr. O'Brien says : " May I add I have heard that some friends in Warrington and neighbburhood are troubled because they, do not know the whereabouts of the ships their relatives are on? Neither do v+e. as, they are not allowed to say. If they wish to coKimuniente with them just address— their name-, rating, and name of ship, care of G.P.O.—who Will forward quite safely." , WARRINGTON AND LIEGE.</p>
        <p>Liege is of particular interest to WarringtOnians, for, like Warrington, it revels in the title of "the !town of many industries." The present siege is not by any means the fhist to which this Belgium city has been subjected; indeed, practically the whole of its history is written around the numerous attacks upon the place occasioned principally by the indomitable spirit of the burghers, who, early in history, set princes and rulers at defiance. They are, and have been for centuries, a very industrious</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>FORWARD BACKWARDS.</head>
        <p>FORWARD BACKWARDS.</p>
        <p>The content bills of the .newspapers are doing their best to beat down the dulness of the times. One displayed the news— ' • French in Alsace; .and other came out with the line— French in Paris. , The latter, of course, referred to General French. On Thursday one bill said— . ' Forward ! - The Kaiser's Order. A companion bill said— " German ,Refreat. Extraordinary Position.</p>
        <p>It was indeed an extraordinary position that the result of a forward order should .be a retreat. We hope that will be the kind of forwards that will be "made in Germany" till the war is brought to a satisfactory con- clusion. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>"BUY AND PAY."</head>
        <p>"BUY AND PAY."</p>
        <p>There are signs/ that the shock to trade occasioned by the declaration of war has spent its force and that we are gradually and surely returning to our former condition—or there- abouts. If confidence is not restored it will not be the fault of the Government or the newspapers. The Master Printers' Federation are taking up the matter in practical fashion, and soon the following notie\es will be seen liberally distributed over the town :</p>
        <p>MOTTO FOR PATRIOTIC BRITONS: " BUSINESS AS USUAL." BUY AND PAY.</p>
        <p>Last week we urged the patriotic duty of going on as usual and of getting and keeping out of debt. It is to be hoped that everyone will continue to show the confidence they have in themselves and their c ountry by refusing to allow even this greatest of calamities to paralyse their activities. Orders should .be given now for goods we need or may need in the immediate future. •</p>
        <p>A PRAYER FOR OUR SOLDIERS. Sons of Britain, fathers, brothers, Loving well our freedom's laws, Shall the Work be all for others? No—ye too can help the cause, Pray to God for our defenders, " Give them victory in the wars."</p>
        <p>Tenler mothers, ye whose dearest Give their lives that wrong may right, Loving wives who give your neareist One to help the country's mighf, Pray to God to guard our soldiers, " Give them victory in the fight."</p>
        <p>We of loyal hearts left ruling, Waiting news from o'er the sea, Is there nought for us the doing? Nought to help, for you and me, Yes, we can pray " God help our soldiers Give them glorious victory." G. HUBI-NEWCOMBE.</p>
        <p>EVERYWHERE, No one can ever make good by making bad.</p>
        <p>To-morrow never comes; neither does yester- day.</p>
        <p>Social friendships among women are mere rivalries.</p>
        <p>Although actions speak louder than words they can't be heard so distinctly.</p>
        <p>In this hypocritical ago one of the few pleas ant things is to see the people act natural.</p>
        <p>The most plausible talker finally reaches a place where he is compelled to make his talk good, or be exposed.</p>
        <p>What has become of the old-fashioned • man who went home to dinner (not lunch) every day at noon, and told what he had on his return?</p>
        <p>The politicians never hate each other so much as.they let on, and there is also a suspicion that they don't love the people as sincerely as they say they do. • • ,</p>
        <p>One of of the longest wharves in the world, almost a mile in length, or to be exact, 4,700 ft., is at Porf Los Angeles, California. It extends into the Pacific in a long, serpentine curve. Near the Truth.</p>
        <p>' An unfortunate misprint in a daily news- paper : • `•` War news in a few Wes."</p>
        <p>The. Old Refrain.</p>
        <p>First Old Lady: "My dear, what do you think of this war? Isn't it terrible?" Second Old Lady: "Awful! But it can't last Milo.; the Powers will surely intervene."— "Punch."</p>
        <p>New Strokes. • "Come on swimmin'. I'll show you some new strokes." • "No; last time I went dad showed me some new ones."</p>
        <p>Shocke4 and Ashamed. A dear old lady writes to say that She .was shocked to • read that Sir Ernest Shackleton's ship, on leaving the Thames, was hooted at by Sirens, and that such conduct makes her ashamed of her sex.—".Ptinch."</p>
        <p>Was a Repeater. r Beaulah : When -he kissed me last night I asked him to tell no one., Belle: And did he? "Why, it wasn't two minutes before he 're- peated it."</p>
        <p>Remedy Too Expensive. Get out the old-fashioned household remedy book and scratch out two remedies, one advis- ing raw beefsteak spread on a bruise and the other advising bacon for a felon. We can't waste beefsteak and bacon on br-uises and felons these days. •</p>
        <p>, mOroving. Father: "And bow are yon getting dn at school, Johnny?" Johnny: "Oh, I have learned to say 'Thank you and 'lf you please' in French." Father: "That's more than you evdr learned in English." .</p>
        <p>Preachers for To-morrow. Wycliffe—Morning and '‘ evening, Rev, ;G. Slack, 'Wakefield. Chrietadelphiane—Evening, Mr. J. P. Jen- nings, Warrington. ' Elmwood Avenue Congregational—Morning and evening, Rev. C. P. Pitt. Bewsey-road Wesleyan—Morning, Mr. H. Wardman; evening, Rev. H. Middlewood. Bold-street' WeekYan—Morning, Rev. 11. Williamson; evening, Rev. J. Gunnell. Legh-street P.M.—Morning and evening, Rev, A. J. Chessum. Latchford Baptist—Morning and evening, Rev, J. S. Chedburn. St. John's Presbyterian—Morning and pven- ing, Rev. W. Reid. Golborne-etreet Baptist—Morning and even- ing, Rev. H. Ogden.</p>
