      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <p>CAPTURE THE ENEMY'S TRADE.</p>
        <p>While our captains of war are planning, the destruction of the cruel machine that the GERMAN EMPEROR has set in motion to afflict the greater part of civilised mankind, our captains of industry are being offered facilities for attacking Germany and Austria in their most vulnerable parts—their Oversea trade. Thanks to the 'superiority 'of the Navies of France and Great Britain, the oceans of the world are as free of danger for us as ever they were, while ,for our adversaries they are absolutely closed. -Internally, as externally, we are in a strong position. Germany and Austria have the whole of their man- hood in the field, and industry would practically have ceased even if a por- tion of the products of the works could have been carried to the markets of the world. In these favoured isles we hav,3 not only an outlet for our goods, but we have the reserve forces at home ready and able to keep our commerciai flag flying in all directions. Confi- dence having been rapidly restored, and masters and employes having' risen splendidly to the occasion, we are progressing to an extent thai. in the circumstances of a gigantic war, is really wonderful. In some .cases particularly cases in which Warring-• ton is specially interested, trade •is better than it was in pre-War days, largely owing to the demands of the Government for the goods turned out by our wireworks and our tanneries. But there is an all-round return to satisfactory business conditions that has been most gratifying. We have, however, to carry the war into the enemy's camp, and (hero appears to be every conceivable chance to do se' successfully at • the presiiiit moment. Industrially Germany and Austria are absolutely helpless and we have but to sten in and pick" up the trade they have so laboriously built up during the last few decades. Other nations will be striving for so rich a prize, and we are fully entitled to 'a share, seeing that we are being put to so much expense and 'inconvenience by our callous and arrogant enemies. Last year German exports wire worth nearly £500,000,000, and her imports nearly £526,000,000, tic corresponding figures for Austria bding £141,433,000 and £115,129,000. To pick up some of this business during the war, and to retain as much of it as we can when the war is ended, is our immediate duty. It is as effective to impoverish an enemy as to slay him • it will curb his power, for future mischief as power- fully as anything could. Conversely to enrich ourselves will be to consoli- date our position and make us the better able to withstand the shock of any future gratuitous attack with which the KAISER may decide to favour The trade between this country and Germany and Austria that will now cease is large, though relatively it iS not of vital moment. • Last year We imported from Germany and Aus- tria £88,000,000 worth of goods, and material, while from the rest of the world we bought £681,000,000 worth. Our exports to those countries were --n5,000,000 and to the rest of the world £180,000,000, In our small amount of exports, was included coal, Of which Germany drained us to the, extent of 76,000,000 tons, a kind of eXp.ort that IS not regarded as likely to, be very pro- 4table to the national interests in the long run. Fortunately the Govern- ment:are as well prepared for this war upon the enemies' Markets as they were for the German explosiop. the Board of Trade Intelligence Depart- ment has long been in touch with all the facts of foreign trade, and the Colonies, the Dominions and neutral foreign countries are now laying be- fore them, thrOugh the proper chan- nels, i all the inforrnation needed for getting quickly on the track of their German add' Anstrian trade.</p>
        <p>It will be seen from our report of the meeting Of the Warrington Chamber of Commerce that our local manufac- turers are urged to take advantage of this "exceptional opportunity" for securing a portion of the' trade that now goes to Germany and Austria, and that they are given the Means ,for get- ting in touch with those Who are direct- ing the Board of Trade 'campaign. Warrington stands to gain as much, at home and abroad, as any town in ' England. Speaking for the tanning ' trade, the "Leather Trades Review" says. "the opportunity of ,establishing the light leather industry on a very large and sound basis in this country was never, so bright as it is now, and no stone should be left unturned to produce, those special kinds of Con- tinental leathers which have .• been imported into this country in enormous quantities during the last few years." Similarly it is 4ged that). foreignH markets hold out much promise to th trade. Last year Germany sent us i 7,000,000 worth of cotton manufac- tures. Warrington cbuld do its share in supplying that, little item, as well as any' shortage .there may, be in the supply of cotton to other countries. In the German iron trade at home and abroad Warrington will tate a sub- stantial hand. On the whole, we can look upon our prospective industrial gains as the silver lining to an exceed- ingly sad and depressing cloud.</p>
      </div>
