      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>Motor Drays its France.</head>
        <p>Motor Drays its France.</p>
        <p>It is now known that the motor drays com- mandeered from Messrs. Crosfield, Messrs. P. Walker and Son, and Messrs. Greenall, Whit- ley and Co., and other local firms, are in France.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>Motor 'Buses Commandeered.</head>
        <p>Motor 'Buses Commandeered.</p>
        <p>The motor 'bus service ' from Latchford through High Legh to Knutsford has been sus- pended as the Government have commandeered the vehicles. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>Warrington's German Prisoners.</head>
        <p>Warrington's German Prisoners.</p>
        <p>The German -prisoners- t-ra-ien-a ---i;;;:tUight ago from the ship at Walton lay-by have now been removed from. the Police Station to the Work- house.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>Co-operators Thanked.</head>
        <p>Co-operators Thanked.</p>
        <p>At'the Warrington Trades Council on Tues- day a vote of thanks was passed to the Warring- ton Co-operative Society for their efforts to keep down the price of food during the war scare.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>Postcards from "Terriers."</head>
        <p>Postcards from "Terriers."</p>
        <p>Picture postcards depicting the place in Scotland where the Warrington men are quar- tered have rolled into Warrington this ,week. The " Terriers" are reported to be in the pink of condition, although the work is hard.</p>
        <p>Are They German Flags? Motor cars, motor cycles and cycles have been decorated with little Union Jacks to show the owners' patriotism. Unfortunately the minia- ture flags are upside down upon the sticks, which indicates "distress." Germany, has made a lot of these flags—ere these some of Ger.: man production?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>Latest _War "Catch."</head>
        <p>Latest _War "Catch."</p>
        <p>"Have you heard that two British ships have gone down?" was yesterday's latest war "catch" in Warrington. ."No; down where?" the innd- cents inquire. "The Ship Canal." A Warring. ton wag says "if the Germans ever get to Namur they will want no more."</p>
        <p>Postcards from the Front. ,</p>
        <p>Now the British censor has announced the fact that British troops are in France it can be stated that the Ist Battalion South Lanca- shires arrived there a week past on Sunday. Several French picture postcards have reached Warrington during the week, but they contain no information. Letters from soldiers at the front .are expected to be strictly censored for some time., -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>An Enthusiastic Frenchman.</head>
        <p>An Enthusiastic Frenchman.</p>
        <p>An enthusiastic Frenchman living in a cer- tain. suburb of Warrington has had four flag poles erected in his garden. From one flies the flag of France, from another the .Union Jack, from a third the Belgian flag, and from the fourth floats the Russian flag. He is reported to have a fifth pole in readiness upon which he will hoist the Japanese flag as soon as they declare war on Germany.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>Liptons and their Staffs. _</head>
        <p>Liptons and their Staffs. _</p>
        <p>Lipton Ltd., throughout their numerous centres at home and branch establishments all over the world are paying half salaries and wages to all members of their staffs who have been called upon to serve with H.M.. colours. The situations of all employes serving their country will be kept open, and these men will be fully reinstated on their return to business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>Grow Garden Produce.</head>
        <p>Grow Garden Produce.</p>
        <p>Amateur gardeners in add around Warring- ton are taking the hint of the Board of Agri- culture and are going in for the cultivation of winter vegetables to augment the food supply. In quite a number of cases flower beds have been milled up to find room for vegetables. A gardener informed "The Examiner" represen- tative that leeks, Brussels sprouts, greens, win- ter cabbage can now be planted, and winter turnips should give a good crop.</p>
        <p>" Hell Upon Water." " To be in the gun turret of an ironclad during a naval engagement," said a Warrington ex- naval men, "is to be in a hell upon water: The guns make a fearful din, and we have to plug our ears with cotton wool to prevent the drums, bursting through the intense vibration. Orders are given by signal. Should a shell burst against the turret it becomes awful, although the tough steel armour can withstand such a battering for a long time."</p>
        <p>A Local War "</p>
        <p>A scare arose in a certain suburb of Warring- ton on Wednesday afternoon to the effect that the Water supply had been poisoned by German spies. The water had been turned off for some time, and when it came on again it was a brown- ish yellow colour, and this caused consternation amongst the worthy residents. It turned out that the discolour was due to rust in the pipes becoming soluble in the water, but it was some time before the "hoax " subsided and the worthy citizens felt safe to use the water to make their afternoon cup of tea. • Handsome Liner Gutted.</p>
        <p>As is now known, ,Britain's' newest and great- est liner, the Aquitania, has been engaged carry- ing troops across to France, and prior to taking up that 'duty the handsome vessel was gutted from bow .to stern. A Warrington joiner en- ggged upon demolishing the state rooms tells The Examiner " representative that the work was executed in a short time. Valuable pianos aud other costly fittings were bundled on to the quayside as if so much rubbish.</p>
        <p>How Troop Trains Are Run. .According to a local railwayman_ troop trains such as carried the Territorials away from War- rington, have preference over every phase of traffic during war time. A.special code of sig- nals is flashed to each' signal cabin to denote the approach of a troop train, and it is the duty of the signalman to clear the line immediately for its safe passage. orne of the trains which have been recently run are reported to have covered tracks of country at the rate of 70 to 75 miles per .hour, whereas the • ordinary ex- presses do not exceed GO miles.</p>
        <p>Help for War SUfferers. At the Trades Council on Tuesday Mr. C. ' Dukes gave his report on the proceedings of the Distress Committee meeting held in the Town Hall the previous week, when three of the members of the Trades Council were ap- pointed on the committee. A resolution was carried to submit more names of working men (whom they nominated) for co-option on the various ward committees. Mr. Dukes said that the ladies' committee, which had been formed, had started a voluntary sewing class, which was itself creating unemployment by doing sewing 'many an out of work woman could do.' ' No Letters From the Front. Warrington people who have relatives at the front were hopeful of receiving letters, but the censor has now intevened and .only official printed postcards can be sent. The postcard which has been prepared for use in the field is in the following form : I am quite well. I have been admitted into hospital sick and am going on well. Wounded f and hope to be discharged soon. I have been sent clown to the base.</p>
        <p>Letter. • { I have received your Telegram. Parcel. Letter follows at first opportunity. I have received no lately. letter from you for a long time. Signature only Date .. ........ The remarks which do not apply are to be struck out and no additional writing is to be allowed on the card.</p>
      </div>
