      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>LOCAL INTELLIGENCE.</head>
        <p>LOCAL INTELLIGENCE.</p>
        <p>Schools Re-opened. elementary schools in Warrington re- after the summer vacation on Monday. . as stated that there were still a good num- .ol.,:teineivlecirenau-ayfrom school owing to the r epidemic.</p>
        <p>• The Eclipse. 3eB were turned sluward&amp; yesterday to re'lB! a very distinct eclipse, which was to 3 observable in all its developments, the scudding heavy clouds obscured 'Jew for a few seconds at intervals.</p>
        <p>• collnelllor S. Taylor's Return. 14tilinileilic'e Samuel Taylor attended the special 'llesd 8 cf the Warrington Town Council on this being his first public appearance %lee underwent the serious operatibn a few age. He was cordially welcomed b -"lei' members of the Council.</p>
        <p>LII Incident at the Bridge. , 'cli:sldeiting. incident eccurred at noon on aY at the Warrington Bridge. A horse iajd to a trap ran away and for a moment "red the pedestrians on the bridge. The P.ointarnan and a constable dashed at pdtiii animal and pluckily brought it to a before it did any damage, although People had a narrow escape.</p>
        <p>Debtors and the AVar. '(Thnur Judge Gurner, who succeeded rie ':'tanger on the County Court Circuit, i Includes Warrington, sat for the first earl, IN'arrington on Monday. He was we!- • C. Mr. J C Rees (Liverpool) on behalf .., e and Mr. A. Browne on behalf of the R 5 practising in Warrington.—ln reply, ()sncrur said that in view of the tuoratorium •144,;„g,°1ng to s-uspend the first payment of summonses fOr two months.</p>
        <p>Street llishaps. .;t,ogular mishaps occurred in the vicinity arrington Bridge on Monday. A. horse AVY dray fell in front of a tram., and „ a Promptitude of the driver prevented a collision. As the midday tram was pro- g to Stockton Heath a young rough-haired r , uo underneath. The driver pulled up aIY to save the dog's life. The animal by illeano got fixed on the top of the motor, ItB finally extricated without injury.</p>
        <p>Footballer Gets Damages. the Warrin. ton County Court on Thurs- o bef g 1, re his Honour Judge Gurner, James 4, Earle-street, Warrington, sued I,:therton, herb beer manufacturer, Bew- elt, for damages in respect of an injury at defendant's shop.—For the plaintiff arcane said his client, who was a well- vv arrington footballer, was supplied ;rl4.- of herb beer in the shop, and whilst 4 'linking it the handle of the mug came ritelit his hand in su'ch a way that he was )t for three weeks.—For the defence, Mr. ~) contended that defendant did not know flat the mug was sound.—Judgment was 'cr the plaintiff for £4 lls., with costs.</p>
        <p>Pt'ath of An Old Footballer. Reesorn, at one time a well-known • Illember of the Warrington Football lq on Monday and was interred at Hill Thursday afternoon. Amongst his Eles as a player was that in the cup final !tleY in the first year of the N.U. Cup. Played full-back, although previous to ' had played in other positions. For Years he has been assistant trainer to it.rington first team, and was looking to resuming his duties in that capacity E'as°ll- He was also an enthusiastic So two sections of sport will miss his • At the funeral on Thursday the club l'esented by Messrs. T. F. Barber, T. I'. '"Y, J. F. Forster, and Harry Ashton.</p>
        <p>Lrers consisted of trainers and checkers, Club sent a handsome wreath.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. l's011):11 meeting of the Wycliffe P.S.A. on sh 8.8 Presided over by Mr. Ashworth who a or address on "Ambition." Mr. fer- ia I,:Polte upon "The evils of war," which erba'or,e Utterly opposed to the principles of 4 }C. Mr. B. Farrar, secretary,, in- • had er brethren that the National Cam- en postponed in consequence of the s,` 4150 pleaded for a generous collection. ql,a'l3 to be devoted to the Prince of Wales' f' bistress Fund. If we could not go rlitl.°llt, we could at least assist those de- eh.;llPon the men who had been called up •mes u. • air. Gray led the prayer, and the • 6'4 read by Mr. Wright. A quartette )seci/lco the Bewsey Adult Schools tastefully •tio,tWo pieces of music. As a result of the tro'! -t2 was sent to the Mayor, who will if to the Distress Fund.</p>
        <p>44 6 A Most Sordid Story." a Most scrrdhl story" was the comment boe t b.,, , Brighouse in summing up at the A're,(l on Wednesday upon a married Ann Libbiter (32), 9, East-street. James Libbiter, said on Satur- li his wife his wages. She went out "e returned was under the influence , Later at night she went out, and a,. fie heard of her was that the body was et', the Police Station. It had been 9- co—ku the Mersey, near Bishop's NV, him, Satiirday evening.—Dennis Collan, teallo was censured •by the Coroner for I,lls the Court when summoned to do lteClaY, said he lived in a lodging-houe iliarket-,street. He met' the woman in iu'rtleie and went with her to waste t 4 liver from Bishop's Wharf. While ,Man ju- came up to them and wanted to IflE' Woman ran away and fell into. the then ran for the police.—A sergeant • s'j,ttrw the woman was got out of the Tre was then alive, but died soon :lete was a cilt on her head through No" 'tone in the river bed. She had t,allen ten feet.—Dr. C. Law said death eYncope following upon shoc,k, Ice the fall into the water.—The Jui? lulea Verdict in agreement with the meth- rile.---The Coroner • (to the husband): , sordid story. lam expressing the pa,:2f the jury and myself when I say with you. • s, whiand for Leather. ,p4kti-",r,Math of the strike is being dealt and strenuous efforts made to get Zfiat tangled organisation straight- 'Jnt after such a lengthened stoppage a tcrutd to be a large accumulation of s,sr vertake. However, in a very short to 3'tbing should be running smoothly the urgent call anticipated. e/Ything in a state of flux one cannot, )oRt' Pe too sure of anything. Some of )part4lllPortant markets have absolutely alab' and others are in a state of 'Ris- k, % The purchasing powers of I go, karket may even be seriously Wi- th' 6,4„,10ng time; on the other hand, it f n there must be a very large isttisk °ldtwear and accoutrements both by "rovernment and those of our Allies; eilalleri3ttY of the numbers in the fiel