        <p>- • qu.</p>
        <p>Improvement at Fish Market. This week a much welcomed improvement. has been effected at the • Fish Market. A screen has been erected the whole length of the building to give protection against wet weather, and now traders and customers can transact their business in comfort.</p>
        <p>Open-air Service. At 8 o'clock to-morrow evening the Warring- ton Free Church Council will hold their next open-air meeting In Knutsford-road, near the Bridge Foot. Mr. R. Corbett will preside, and addresses will be given by the Revs. H. Ogden and A. J. Chessum.</p>
        <p>Warrington Deserter llanded Over. ,the Warrington Police Court on Thurs- day, Nathaniel Redfern, 11, School-brow, was ordered to be handed over to a military escort as a deserter. In October, 1910, he deserted from the 2nd liinq's Royal Rifles. When found by the police in a public-house he denied his identity, and, in consequence, did net come under the terms of the free pardon,</p>
        <p>Iron and Steel Workers and the War.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Elwell writes : The Associated Iron and Steel Workers' Trades Organisation have decided that in all cases where members are called to the front or for reserves they must be kept in benefit on the books without payment, and upon re-starting they will be just in the same position as when they went away.</p>
        <p>From New Zealand to Ragged School. Councillor Skelland this week received the following :—" Turakina, New Zealand, 5/7/14. Dear Sir,—Enclosed postal order for 10s., a small thanksgiving to God for His priceless gift of a true woman. We were married on the 18th ot' last month. I pray that God's blessing may rest on your good work for the children.—Yours sincerely, P. JArtmAN." Coun- cillor Skelland has previously received dona- tions from the writer, who appears to have come under the influence of the Ragged School at some period of his life</p>
        <p>Arrears of Health Insurance Contribu- tions: Reservists and Territorials. The National Health Insurance Commission (England) desire to announce that any notice with regard to arrears due to unemployment during the past year which Reservists, Terri- torials, or insßred persons who have joined the regular forces' (or in their absence their rela- tions), may receive, does not apply to them now - that they have been called up, and that they should write the words "called up" across it and return it to the society, who will iseue the special instructions applying to them at a later date.</p>
        <p>Feared He Was Going Blind. Coroner Brighouse held an inquest on Wed- nesday upon Joseph Ashurst (37), of 19, Old- road, who was found drowned in the canal, near Black Bear Bridge.—Mr. Charles Gould, a confectioner, residing in Wildersppol-causeway, said he employed deceased. Lately he seemed, to be strange, as he feared he was going blind.— George Lister, a canal employe, living at Latchford Locks, said he found the cap and jacket of the deceased on the bank. He got his grappling irons and lcund the body, but life was extinct. The jury returned a verdict of "Found drowned," and congratulated Mr. Lister on the prompt manner in which he had acted.</p>
        <p>Death of Mr. George Mellor. ,The death took place suddenly of Mr. George Mellor, of 72, Dudley-street, Warrington. The deceased was a well-known insurance agent and had only returned to his office after a hard day's collecting before he expired. The interment took place at the Rochdale Cemetery. Deceased was a prominent member of the R.A.0.8., he having attained, only a few months ago; the Third Degree, or K.O.M. of the Order: He was also the secretary of the " Golden 'Fleece" Lodge, No. 134, in which capacity his valuable services will be greatly missed. The R.A.0.8. representatives from Warrington who attended were: Bro. T. Davies, 8.0. H., K.G.H.; Bro. T. Cooper, K.0.M., K.G.H.; and Bro. J. Walsh, K.0.M., K.G.H. • A Bather Drowned.</p>
        <p>An inquest was held on Wednesday on Wil- liam Johnson (15), a moulder's apprentice, of 3, Admiral-street, who was drowned. in the Mersey On Tuesday evening. The mother said. that her son left home at 6.5 p.m., and by seven o'clock she was informed that he was drowned in the river a mile away from his home.— Samuel Ralph said lie was swimming with Johnson, when the latter was seized with the cramp. Ralph tried to get hold of Johnson, and once did so, but had to leave*go, and John- son sank immediately.—Joseph Hatton, captain of the Warrington Grappling Corps, described how he .found the body on Wednesday morning. —The jury returned a verdict of "Accidental death." - - - - -</p>
        <p>Death of Mrs. Metcalfe. We record, with regret, the death of -Mrs. Metcalfe, aged 49, wife of Sergeant Metcalfe, of the Warrington Police Force, which occurred on Wednesday of last week, at Canterbury- street, Latchford. Mrs. Metcalfe had been a sufferer for some time, and for a fortnight had been confined to her home. The funeral-took place, amid many tokens of respect, on Satur- day afternoon, at the Warrington Cemetery. The coffin was carried to the grave by members of the Police Force, and Inspector Forster and other members 'of the police staff, Inspector Wainwright, and Mr. Law, Police Court Missionary, were present at the service. Floral tributes were endorsed : "From her loving husband," "From Fanny and Darling May," "From Mary," "Mother, Annie. and Stephen," "From her old friends, F. and A. White," "From Mrs. Green," "From Nellie Clare," "From Shot," Mrs. Watson, Mrs. Leather, "From Mr. and Mrs. Clarke," " Gertrude and John," and -Mrs. Furlonger. A handsome wreath was sent by the Members .of the norough Police. - The service -was con- ducted by the Rev. H. H. Corsbie.</p>
        <p>Disputed Business Sale. At the Warrington County Court on Thurs- day, before Judge Gurner, Ralph Jepson, Blackbrook Cottage, Fearnhea.d, sued Samuel James Griffiths, Fearnhead House, Fearnhead, for .17 10s., being the balance of the account for certain goods sold in connection with the sale of a greengrocery business lately carried on by the plaintiff.—Defendant made a counter- claim for £75 damages.—Mr. W. B. Forshaw said that the plaintiff sold the business, includ- ing a horse, Furry, spring cart, phaeton, harness, scales, boxes, hampers, and the remaining stock to the defendant for £45, stating that the 'pro- fits would be about £2 or £3 per week. When asked for the balance of the account plaintiff said he was not satisfied with the business.— Mr. Browne, for the defendant, said plaintiff had misrepresented the business, and that the customers had been interfered with.—After the plaintiff's case was closed, the solicitors endea- voured to effect an amicable settlement. Hav- ing failed to ,do so, tb*37 consented to leave it to his .honour, who ordered the return of the lurry, sprinc, cart, harness and scales, and the claim was struck out.—The plaintiff was ordered to refund £25 to the defendant and was given the costs of the action, the defendant being awarded the costs of the, counter-claim.' Local Subscriptions to National 'Belief Fund. The local subscriptions to date to the Prince of Wales'. National Relief Fund, which yester- day stood at £1,450,000, aye as follow: A. H. Crosfield, £200; Herbert S. Bleckly (Ist iastalment), £5O; Miss M. G. Rylands, £25; William Bennett, &lt;225; collection it Wycliffe ICongregational Church, ..t2O Ss. 10d.; Thomas Atherton, £2O; His Worship the Mayor (Alder- man Peacock), £2O; Edward Massey, £l5: churchwardens of Holy Trinity Church, „tl3 2s: Bd.; Herbert Woods, £10; Moore Wesleyan Chard). and Wesley Guild, ...85 18s. 8(1,7, John Melbourne, Ltd., £5 55.; 'Lionel Fairclough, £5; J. Roy Coventry, £5; John Hamblet, £5; F. J. Grocott, £5; Hamblet Bros.. £4 45.; George H. Gill, £3 38.•, Mr. and Mrs. F. H. .Shaw, £2 25.; Wycliffe P.S.A., £2; collection at St, Luke's Church, £1 16s. id.; All Saints' "Uission, per X. H. Smethurst, £1 55.; collection at Welsh Church, £1 is. 6d.; " Guardian " 'Office em- ployes, 10s. 7d.•, Margaret L. Smethurst, 10s.; James E. Smethurst, 10s.; Miss Sumner. 10s.; amount previously acknowledged, £1,153 as. 6d. Total, .21,600 ss. 10d. In addition to the above Messrs. Richmonds have sent .2500 direct to the Prince of Wales' Relief Fund.</p>
        <p>111.-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>EXAMINER, MiTURDAY, AUGUST 221 1914.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, MiTURDAY, AUGUST 221 1914.</p>
        <p>WORKERS AND THE WARDS</p>
        <p>Three Labour Candidates Probable at the November Election.</p>
        <p>WARDS NOT YET SELECTED.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington Trades and Labour Council was held on Tues- day evening at the Socialist Hall, Mr. E. Handles presiding.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said the local branch of the Dockers' Union had again become affiliated to the Council. They all welcomed the new dele- gates and hoped they would take an energetic interest in the work of the Council. (Hear, hear.) .</p>
        <p>WORKERS AND NOVEMBER ELECTION. Mr. C. Dukes said that a meeting of the Joint Committee in reference to Labour representa- tion had been held to consider the advisability or otherwise of contesting for the vacant seat in Orford Ward. Their decision was that rarner than contest a seat they could only hold for, a period of, say, twelve months, it was in the in- terests of the general Labour movement to keep off until November, when they intended to con- test at least three wards. Every trade union could submit the names of fit and eligible men, and the Joint Committee would appoint their candidates from those who were brought for- ward. To enter these contests meant they must get money, and the speaker outlined a suggested scheme. Which wards they were going to contest he could not say, but people would see their men at work in the certain wards when the time drew nearer.</p>
        <p>A Delegate: "Moving troops secretly," and laughter.</p>
        <p>RECENT TRADE DISPUTES.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. Howard said that in recent local trade disputes they had been dependent upon trade unionists of other works for material support. He suggested that the Council should tall a conference of secretaries of all local branches of trade 'unions to inquire if some scheme could be devised to institute .systematic workshop collections. If such a method were adopted they would no doubt be' able to obtain larger amounts.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: I think it is a very good idea, but would Mr. Howard bring it forward at the next meeting,.as_it is getting so late?</p>
        <p>Mr. Howard: It win suit me. This course was adopted.</p>
        <p>FEEDING STRIKERS' CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. Duke's gave his report of an interview with the Mayor and Councillor H. Roberts with reference to the feeding of the children of the tanners.out of work. At the time of the meeting they were faced with the difficulty that it was a holiday period, and that the children Could not be fed out of the rates. To obviate that and other difficulties, the Mayor and Councillor Roberts agreed to defray the cost if the sub- scriptions to the fund to be. opened al not realise expectations. They thought that every- thing had been arranged well and carried out in a very satisfactory manner—(hear, hear)—an he only wished that all the workers' children were as well fed. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>CAIRO-STREET CHAPEL NEV</head>
        <p>CAIRO-STREET CHAPEL NEV</p>
        <p>PASTOR.</p>
        <p>The Rex. Irvine Lister Appointed.</p>
        <p>In succession to the Rev. Dr. Stanley Mellor, who was recently appointed, pastor of Hope- street Unitarian Church, Liverpool, the Rev. Irvine Lister, of West Didsbury, Manchester, has accepted an invitation to the pastorate of Cairo-street Unitarian Chapel, and will com- mence his duties there upon the first Sunday next month.</p>
        <p>Mr. Lister is 31 years of age. Educated for the ministry at the Manchester University and also at the Yorkshire United College, Bradford, he was, until three months ago, minister of the Congregational Church at TJppermill, near Old- ham, but resigned the position, after having held it'for four years. on account of his in- ability to reconcile it with his theological beliefs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>WOMEN LIBERALS.</head>
        <p>WOMEN LIBERALS.</p>
        <p>To Sew For Our Soldiers at the War. MRS. CROSFIELD AS NURSE.</p>
        <p>At a largely attended meeting of the Women's Liberal Association, held at the John Crosfield Memorial Hall yesterday afternoon, for the pur- pose of instituting war sewing meetings, Mrs. Osborne explained that she had been ill for the last fortnight, or a start would have been made earlier. _ .</p>
        <p>Mrs. Arthur Crosfield, who with her sister is training at Hampstead General Hospital to give practical help wherever needed, wrote express- ing her regret at not being able to be present, but enclosing a cheque for ..e25 to the fund for buying material for those attending the sewing , parties. She added "While at this supreme crisis in European history it is only right that we should be pre- pared for emergencies, and while no patriotic citizen will be disposed to under-rate the dangers of such a war, if our arms are so far crowned with success that the demand made upon us by no means exhausts the stock of what we are qble to provide, not a penny of the money, or a minute of the time devoted to helping IA to carry through olir project would be wasted, where the whole of the surplus would be available for admirable use afterwards ,in our own Infirmary in Warrington. I there- fore invite the cordiul and earnest co-operation of all those who are willing to help us with contributions in money, or in material, or in both; and we send as cordial an invitation to all those who can spare the time to do so to assist us by attending the sewing classes, which will be held at least twice a week in the Crosfield Memorial Hall, as well as in the different wards of the town. _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>Hearty Welcome to All.</head>
        <p>Hearty Welcome to All.</p>
        <p>_ "One other suggestion I venture to make, namely this: that at the present time we give a particularly hearty welcome to anyone !who will take a hand in this work, whatever their poli- tical or religious opinions may be." It was decided to begin sewing meetings in the Memorial Hall, and to hold them Monday and Thursday in each week from two to eight o'clock, tea being 'providetl. Although the meetings are organised by the Liberal women, they will be open to all who desire to assist, irrespective of ,Party, creed or similar consideration. _ .</p>
        <p>Mrs. Osborne goes away to-day for a fort- night's rest, but the work will he begun forth- with. . _ _</p>
        <p>Cheques, parcels and otber gifts may he sent to 'Miss Crosfield, boa. treasurer, and Mrs. Osborne, hon. secretary, 22, Bold-street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>UNIONIST' WOMEN AT WORK. '</head>
        <p>UNIONIST' WOMEN AT WORK. '</p>
        <p>In connection with the Warrington Unionist and Tariff Reform League, sewing classes have been held and many useful garments made for the soldiers and sailors who are fighting for the country. Lady Greenall has subscribed £2OO to the fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>WARRINGTON AMBULANCE MEN:</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AMBULANCE MEN:</p>
        <p>Interesting Letter From Crosfield's</p>
        <p>Naval contingent.</p>
        <p>FIRM'S GENEROSITY APPRECIATED.</p>
        <p>Mr. Rose, one of the managers at Messrs. Crosfield's and superintendent of the St. John Ambulance Association for the Warrington district, has received an interesting letter from Crosfield's naval ambulance men now at Daven- port. Some 20 men of the contingent have joined the Royal Navy Sick Berth Reserve, and three men have joined the Home Hospital Re- serve. The letter is as follows : ROYAL NAVAL BARRACKS, DEVONPORT.</p>
        <p>Dear Mr. Rose,— Just a few lines to let you know from time to time our whereabouts. Now that our First Officer Cawley has been called out, I take it he told you where our men had been drafted to. We had orders to go with the Marines Battalion abroad, but cannot tell you where. That is, F. I. Flood, Cook, Dunne, Jones, E. Newns, and myself; Barton is still waiting for a ship. Lewis is going to remain in the sick quarters at Devonport Barracks. L 0. Roberts is at Mill Bay receiving room at the telephone night and _day, and sleeps in the same MOM. Hughes and Gannon have arrived in dock here off the Illustrious, they have been enjoy- ing cruising round the coast of France chas-</p>
        <p>• lag prize ships. Fisher's boat landed here for a day and he was allowed to go ashore for an hour. He has been sailing for a fort- night; I cannot tell where they have been. All men on the ships have had instructions not to write home to say where they are, or where they are going. The other boys have gone to Port Said, Hong Kong, and Sydney to join the Eastern Fleet. I had a postcard from them and they are en- joying it immensely. lam feeling more satisfied now they are getting settled and comfortable—then I can start for myself. Well, Mr. Rose, we have all heard about the generosity of our firm, and the kind thoughts of our directors towards our wives and families, and it is the wish of all con- cerned that you convey our thanks to them, which I am sure you will only beloo pleased to do. It has certainly • given every man more encouragement to carry out the work he has undertaken to do.—Believe us to re- main, your faithfully, (Signed) Sergeant I. E. Newns, Corporal I. 0. Roberts, Corporal R. Cook, Private H. Jones, Private I. E. Newns, Private F. Barton, Private H. Lewis, Private A. Fisher. On behalf of those at sea: (Signed) Corporal W. Mellor, Corporal A. Webb, Private I. Bat- tersby, Private E. Jones, Private Ditchfield, Private S. Roe, Private W. Bowden, Private A. Fisher.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>SUFFRAGISTS MAKE BANDAGES ' In Aid of the Red Cross. •</head>
        <p>SUFFRAGISTS MAKE BANDAGES ' In Aid of the Red Cross. •</p>
        <p>The local branch of the National Union of Women's Suffrage Societies, having abandoned its political work in order to help those who will suffer by the war, a sewing-room was opened on Thursday afternoon at Atkinson's Cafe, Bridge-street. About 300 members and friends had been circularised by the committee, 'and many are now engaged in making clothing, bandages, &amp;c., for the Red Cross Society at Raddon Court, Latchford. Garments for dis- tribution amongst local women and children will afterwards be sent to the Mayoress.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>TIIE RED CROSS SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>TIIE RED CROSS SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The fled Cross Society's preparations at Itaddon Court, Latchford, are nearing comple- tion. The classes in nursing and first-aid methods are well attended, and the members are enthusiastic in their efforts to make them- selves efficient. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>ST. JOHN AMBULANCE.</head>
        <p>ST. JOHN AMBULANCE.</p>
        <p>During the week about 20 members of the local detachment of the St. John Ambulance Brigade have received ordePs to be in readiness to Mobilise. _ _ _</p>
        <p>It is desirable that working parties should be inaugurated On behalf of the association, to make various articles of clothing to be used in the hospitals in the United Kingdom and those attached to the Expeditionary Force, and for other purposes connected with aiding the sick and wounded in Warrington. All clothing and gifts ,received will be placed at the dis- posal of the Queen, the President of the Ladies' Committee of the Order. , Parcels and gifts should be sent carriage paid to the St. John Ambulance Warehouse, 56, St. John's-square, Clerkenwell, London, E.C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>GARMENTS ALREADY SENT.</head>
        <p>GARMENTS ALREADY SENT.</p>
        <p>The Ladies' Sub-committee of the Warrington</p>
        <p>Distress Committee met at the Town Hall on Thursday, when 327 garments were despatched to base hospitals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>INFIRMARY OFFERED.</head>
        <p>THE INFIRMARY OFFERED.</p>
        <p>'Room for the Accommodation of the Injured. At a meeting of the Board of Management of the Warrington Infirmary yesterday, called to consider how far the resources of the institu- tion could be placed at the disposal of the Gov- ernment, jr.N J. H. Smethurst presided. On the nroposition of Mr. W. P. Rylands, seconded by Dr. Fox, the following resolution was carried unanimously: "That the secretary inquire whether the authorities would need hospital accommodation in this country for the treat- ment of serious cases of wounded. If so, that the authorities be advised that there exists in the recent extension to this institution a ward capable of accommodating an additional 25 beds. That this ward is unfurnished at the present time, but the Board of Management would be pleased to furnish the ward and pro- vide the necessary nursing staff and medical attention."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <p>was the " Day " foretold by yours and you whispers here, and there with beery clamours— 'on and your rat-hole spies and blustering crew Of loud Potsdamers.</p>
        <p>And 10, there dawns another, swift and stern. When on the wheels of wrath, by Justice' token, Breaker of God's own Peace, you shall in turn • Yourself be broken. 0.8.</p>
