......,_ . , ..,..„ ~, , t , By permission of the Proprietors of "Punch. 'l'flE WORLD'S ENEMY. The KAISER: "Who HOCS there?" Spirit of Carnage: "A Friend—your only one." 4x-ou shared our griefs with seeming-gentle eyes; This You moved among us cousinly entreated, In Still hiding, under that fair outward guise, A heart that cheated. Y . And now the mask is down, and forth you stand Known for a King whose word is no great , '-• matter, . A traitor proved, for every honest hand To strike and shatter. , 1 - -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>IYARRINGTONIANS' DANGER.</head>
        <p>IYARRINGTONIANS' DANGER.</p>
        <p>Miss Evelyn Clarke's Escape from Central Germany.</p>
        <p>THE "PERFIDIOUS ENGLISH."</p>
        <p>Several Warringtonians were on the Continent when war broke out, and they had an exciting time ere they once more saw the welcome white cliffs of Old England. Miss Evelyn M. Clarke, daughter of Councillor Clarke, 'Stockton Heath, was in Central Germany when was was declared. For days it was impopsible to get into com- munication with her, but finally the young lady, to the intense relief of her many friends, man- aged to make her escape from the Fatherland, and arrived safely home this week. Two Warrington lady school teachers were in Switzerland and had an exciting journey through France. Pour Padgate people were in Holland and had the greatest difficulty in reach-- ing home, having to spend the night on the deck of a crowded steamer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>MISS CLARKE'S ADVENTURE.</head>
        <p>MISS CLARKE'S ADVENTURE.</p>
        <p>Aided in Her Escape by the. American</p>
        <p>Consul.</p>
        <p>TRE ITER WITH COURTESY.</p>
        <p>"We were treated with the greatest courtesy by, everybody," said Miss Evelyn M. Clarke, of Grappenhall-road, Stockton Heath, when inter- viewed by "The Examiner" representative with reference to her _experience.</p>
        <p>Miss Clarke, who had been spending a holiday near Heidelberg, found great difficulty, owing to the mobilisation of troops, in reaching Frank- furt-on-Maine, where a friendly cabman gave her and her friend the first intimation that Britain had joined in the hostilities against the Fatherland. At Frankfurt they visited the British Consulate, but were informed that Britain was at war with Germany and that there was no British Consul. However, through the energies of the American Consul, emergency passports were forthcoming, and then, on August 7, they were able to proceed towards the Dutch frontier. After spending the following night partly in Coblentz station and partly in a train, in which it was compulsory to 'travel with the windows closed and without lights, they arrived at Cologne. On Sunday, August 9, accompanied by four Dutch people, they arrived at a little town on the Dutch frontier, hoping to be able to join the Dutch. The party had now grown and con- sisted of five ladies and two Englishmen.</p>
        <p>"ALL ENGLISH MUST GET-OUT." The party took ticket to Rotterdam, boarded the train, and were expecting to start, when they heard the order which they had by then learned to dread, "All English must get out of the train." This little frontier town was a holi- day resort, and the party had every liberty dur- ing the stay until August 14, when they pro: ceeded to Flushing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>FRENCH WOMAN KICKED.</head>
        <p>FRENCH WOMAN KICKED.</p>
        <p>They were warned to speak as little English as possible, and probably it was due to com- plying with this request that they were treated so kindly, for it was stated that a French woman who made a "fuss" was kicked by one of the military. Every day during which the party was de- tained on the frontier they paid a visit to the captain in charge to ask if he had received any notification that British subjects could proceed, and at last they were informed that all the women could move, and the men who did not belong to the British Army or Navy could also I do so.</p>
        <p>Without luggage, for all their boxes had been detained at Frankfurt, they crossed the Dutch frontier into comparative safety.</p>
        <p>I WAR "POPULAR" WITH GERMANS. Asked if she thought the war was popular amongst the Germans, Miss Clarke replied in the affirmative, adding that the soldiers were Suit of enthusiasm and had decorated their trains with leaves and chalked all along the carriages:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>" ST. PETERSBVRG VIA PARIS."</head>
        <p>" ST. PETERSBVRG VIA PARIS."</p>
        <p>She witnessed no anti-English demonstrations, but stated that English names and the French word " cafe " were covered up, and she had heard the English referred to as "perfidious." . . . . .</p>
        <p>It Vas impossible to get into communication with England, for several letters and postcards and a telegram Were never delivered at Stock- ton Heath.</p>
        <p>Early on in the war a statement was issued in the Orman newspapers to the effect that the King of Servia and 20,000 men had been taken prisoners, and announcements of Belgium cruelty were common.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>IN THE DANGER ZONE.</head>
        <p>IN THE DANGER ZONE.</p>
        <p>Four Padgate Residents Stranded in</p>
        <p>llolland.</p>
        <p>RACE BY MOTOR TO CATCH BOAT.</p>
        <p>Upon the declaration of war, traffic in Hol- land was considerably dislocated, as was experi- ened by Mr. and Mrs. Edward Slater, of Pad- gate Nurseries, and Mr. and Mrs. Thomas Duck- worth, also of Padgate. Upon Tuesday, the 4th instant, this party of four left England by Ihe steamer " Rievaulx Abbey," of the Wilson sailing from Hull to Rotterdam. Arrived there, they proceeded upon a three hours' rail- way journey to Voorhout, where they had ar- ranged to spend ten days with some friends upon a bulb farm. When they had been there six days they found that, since war had been declared, the greater part of the train and boat services had been suspended. They therefore decided to retiqn as soon as possible. With some difficulty 'tau contrived to reach Rotterdam by catching an early morning train; but found upon arrival that the boat service had been suspended. They learned, however, after much inquiry, that a boat would leave the Hook of Holland an hour before midnight, and agreed to Make every effort to catch it.</p>
        <p>Anxious to lose no time, they motored to the station, only to be informed that all trains to the Hook had been cancelled. There was only one thing to do if they were to catch the boat, and that was to motor the distance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>RACE AGAINST TIME.</head>
        <p>RACE AGAINST TIME.</p>
        <p>A car was accordingly engaged; then followed an exciting race against time along the tops of the dykes, the advance being considerably de- layed by numberless " holdings up" for iriquiry into their business. The necessary twenty miles nevertheless were at length covered, and the be- lated party arrived at the docks. Here they foiind that the boat about to sail belonged to the Great Eastern Line, and they would have to sacrifice their Wilson Line return tickets. There remained just sufficient time for them to book, which they were allowed to do on the understanding that they should be provided neither with food nor sleeping accommoda.tion. On the quayside they purchased some fruit, however, and also got a few hours' sleep upon the ship deck. The boat was crowded with tourists return- ing to England. Some had been motor cycling, but had been deprived of their cycles on the German frontier. A lady on the boat had only arrived in Holland that morning, and, learning the state of affairs there, had straightway em- barked again to make sure of getting home. she spent the time from her entering the boat in the morning until 7 o'clock the next morning, when they arrived at Harwich, without food of any kind. This was only one of many case.</p>
        <p>WARSHIPS SIGHTED. Steaming through the North Sea, along the lower part of our East Coast, the boat passed numberless torpedo and other war vessels be- longing to this country, which kept a sharp look-out for any suspicious ocean traffic. It was also passed by the Dresden, which carried a load of passengers from Antwerp. At 7 o'clock in the morning the boat arrived</p>
        <p>at Harwich, where the party disembarked. They then proceeded by an easy train to Warrington, and soon afterwards arrived at Padgate, to be welcomed by a large number of friends.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>DASJI FOR HOME.</head>
        <p>A DASJI FOR HOME.</p>
        <p>Iyarrington Lady .Teacher's Trying</p>
        <p>Experience.</p>
        <p>CROWDED TRAINS AND LITTLE FOOD.</p>
        <p>Another Warrington resident who had gone abroad for her holidays was Miss Emily Brown, a member of the staff of Arpley-street Schools, who was touring through France and Switzer- land. It was her intention to proceed to Bel- gium, there to finish her holiday: Miss Brown had been away for about ten days: which she had spent in arid around Lucerne. Upon her reaching Bale, en route for Belgium, however, the train was held up, and its occupants in- formed that the train service to that country had been suspended for an indefinite period. ,•</p>
        <p>The dimly-fit station was crowded a'nd in a state of great confusion, the Customs office being completely deserted. Everybody was asking questions, but nobody seemed able to answer them. After a time, however, Miss Brown was advised to return via Paris to England, as all other routes were closed—advice which she, to- gether with many others, decided to act upon. They managed to catch a train to Paris, but this only carried them as far as Belfort, where they had again to alight. Here also the station was practically in darkness, and crowded with people and luggage in still greatq confusion. After much difficulty, the party contrived to board a Paris train, although the only space available was a crowded corridor, and the only seats their luggage. In this manner they even-, tually reached Paris; it was impossible to pur- chase either food or any other refreshment at any of the stations, which were all guarded by soldiers. The train made frequent stoppages outside country stations, and several times long trains passed in the opposite direction, carrying French soldiers and guns to the frontier.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>ARRIVAL IN PARIS.</head>
        <p>ARRIVAL IN PARIS.</p>
        <p>It was only with great difficulty that the party contrived to obtain a vehicle to convey them and their luggage to the Gare du Nord, where they booked for Boulogne. This station was "simply besieged" by people desirous of leav- ing the French capital, and the heat was intense during the journey to Boulogne. Here again great difficulties were experienced, and no vehicle was to be had to convey the luggage to the boat.</p>
        <p>After a quick sea passage, Folkestone was reached, the party having journeyed for 26 hours" continuously without a rest or break of any kind. The party consider they were extremely fortunate to reach England at the time they did, as those who later decided to.return must, from all accounts, have suffered greater discom- forts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>TEACHERS' EXPERIENCES. Miss M. Jolley and. Miss Morris in Belgium.</head>
        <p>TEACHERS' EXPERIENCES. Miss M. Jolley and. Miss Morris in Belgium.</p>
        <p>An assistant mistress at the Warrington Secondary School passed through a thrilling ex- perience in escaping from the Continent, and at one point the boat on which she sailed was fired upon by the French. The lady (Miss Marion Jolley, a daughter of Mr. and Mrs. Harold Jolley, Palmyra-square) was accompanied by Miss Morris, another assist- ant mistress, and they were in Belgium when war was declared.</p>
        <p>On Saturday morning, August 1, Antwerp, says Miss Jolley, was full of the military, and she and her companion became a little nervous, particularly as they did not know which side Belgium was on. However, they were received with the greatest courtesy and kindness by everyone. Once on board an English-bound vessel they kept close to the coast, all going well until they reached Dunkirk, where a gun was fired from the French coast. The signalling to the Trench coast did not seem to satisfy the authorities, for they were examined by a French boat before they were allowed to proceed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>MAYOR AS ALDERMAN.</head>
        <p>MAYOR AS ALDERMAN.</p>
        <p>Elected to Succeed the Late Alderman Forshaw.</p>
        <p>A THREE TO ONE MAJORITY.</p>
        <p>The Mayor is now an Alderman. His eleva- tion to the Aldermanic ,Bench took place at a special meeting of the Town Council on Tues- day evening, called to elect a successor to the late Alderman Forshaw. The Mayor presided at the start, but said that under the circum- stances which had arisen he would be glad if someone else took the 'chair, and he suggested that Alderman Bennett should do so.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT : The senior Alderman should take the chair, and I move that Alder- man Smethurst do so.</p>
        <p>This was carried unanimously and Alderman Smethufst took the chair.</p>
        <p>Voting papers were ''''''''''''' filled in by _ the councillors, the. eldermen not voting, and handed in to the chairman.</p>
        <p>RESULT OF THE VOTING. Alderman SMETHURST announced the result of the voting as follows:— for Councillor Peacock.—Councillors Wilson, Peacock, Osborne, Shaw, Gough, Archer, Lash- ford, Chrimes, Chantler, Cunnington, McKee,' S. Taylor, Fox, Broadhurst and Temberton-15. For Councillor Wilkinson.—Councillors Haw- thorn, Wilkinson, Joseph, Lindley, and Locker. —5.</p>
        <p>For Councillor Craik.—Councillor Craik--1.</p>
        <p>Alderman SMETHURST : I declare Councillor Peter Peacock duly elected an Alderman of the borough of Warrington. (Applause.) The Mayor was then sworn in.</p>
        <p>THE THANKS. The IIAY ,EaeViAknoYwOleßdging the hondur, said: I beg to thank the Council very sincerely for the great honour conferred upon me in elevating me to the position of an alderman of the county borough of Warrington. I may state that during my term of office I shall endeavour to give satisfaction to the rate- payers and to my colleagues on The Council. I can assure yon I do not wish to be an alderman for life. I shall take a- definite and firm stand on that point, and although I have now been elected an alderman, at the end of the term— five years—l shall retire and go before the rate- payers and seek re-election in some particular ward. (Applause.) I want to make my position quite clear. I thank those who have voted for me, and I hope that those who voted for the other gentlemen will bear no animosity, and that we shall work with that harmony which is necessary for the good working of the tow/li' (Applause.) .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>WRITECROSS WARD.</head>
        <p>WRITECROSS WARD.</p>
        <p>Liberal Candidate to Fill Vacancy.</p>
        <p>James Thomas Barton, common agent, NO, Lovely-lane, has been selected Liberal candidate for the vacancy in White- cross Ward, occasioned by the elevation. of Councillor Peacock (the Mayor) to the Alder- manic Bench. An executive meeting of the,Whitecross Ward Committee was held on Tuesday evening, when several names were submitted. In the end it was unanimously decided to invite Mr. Barton to fill the vacancy. Mr. Barton was waited upon and gave his</p>
        <p>consent. At a meeting of the whole of the Whitecross Committee last night many eulogistic spcleches were made, and it was decided with unanimity that the selection of the Executive Committee be approved• Ur. Barton is away on holidays and was un- able to be present. The last day for nominations is Monday. Withdrawals will be open until Tuesday, and he t election will take place on Tuesday week.</p>
      </div>
