      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>" Shuttlewerth."</p>
        <p>The appearance of this familiar signature at the foot -of the advertised appeal to Lancashire in hehrlf of the National Relief Fund sent me back in thought 'th a period, well-nigh a quarter of a century ago, when, as Sir Ughtred Kay- Shuttleworth, the present Lord-Lieutenant of the county :W4B the Liberal M.P. for the Clitheroe•Division—an office in which he was succeeded by Mr. D. J. Shackleton, the well- known Labour representative who has since received a useful appointment on the perman- ent staff of the Home Office: -r n those days I was stationed in that part of the county, and it was my lot to regularly report, first the one and their the other; and a greater contrast, as to appearance and manner and in many other respects, than was afforded by ,the two it is impossible to imagine. To be sure, Clitheroe took very kindly to both, and Lord. Shuttle- worth's loyal and disinterested services while member for that constituency were. had in grateful remembrance—nay, living as he still does In that very district, he has maintained the old intimate relations and continues on the best of terms with the people of those parts. The appeal in question is well assured of a worthy response thereabout.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>An Old Peace Monument.</head>
        <p>An Old Peace Monument.</p>
        <p>It is peculiarly pleasant just now to recall that, when I, sojourned in the Calder Valley, at the extremity of the West Riding of York- shire which touches the border of Lancashire referred to in the preceding paragraph, the most striking object in the landscape- was a huge stone-built obelisk surmounting one of the higher hills of the Pennine Chain, so that it was conspicuous over a radius of a dozen miles or more. The pastor of Golborne-street Baptist Church (Rev. H. Ogden) was born under its very shadow, for it immediately- overlooks his native town of, Todmorden. This Stoodley Pike, as it is called, is a peace monument with an extraordinary history. The erection of the original structure was commenced precisely 100 years ago with the idea of commemorating the triumphant entry of the allies into Paris at what was supposed to be the close of the Napoleonic Wars. The escape of Bonaparte and the resumption of hostilities led to a tem- porary suspension of the work, but the design was carried out in the following year. Now comes the ingular part of the story. The peace monument actually collapsed on the very day of the departure of the Russian Ambas- sador in view of the Crimean War; and the present one was built on the site to commemor- ate the signing of peace with Russia. It occurred to me that many Warringtonians who doubtless have noticed the obelisk when travel- ling Yorkshire-ward, just as others coming from the opposite direction have been inter- ested in Rivington Pike, might welcome this brief explanation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>An Unenviable Position.</head>
        <p>An Unenviable Position.</p>
        <p>As noted in the last issue, the first Sunday since the opening of the war was observed as one of peculiar solemnity by Christians of all denominations; but I was most unfortunate in my cboice of a place of worship on that day of days, and I have by no means got over it yet: I happened to be on a visit to a town many, many mi;ps from Warrington, sad for the Sunday morning service I went to one of the largest Nonconformist churches in the neigh- bourhood. To my amazement and intense dis- appointment, there was not the slightest allu- sion, in prayer, hymn, or sermon, to the topic of the hour! The psalms and spiritual songs had to do wholly and solely with ordinary every-day experiences, and the preacher sought to comfort his hearers with doctrine which seemed to me to be of the essence of selfishness. Surely my experience was as tkiique as it cer- tainly was disgusting. The unspeakable per- son in the pulpit might at least have remem- bered the innocent sufferers from the war.</p>
        <p>Angina Pectoris. If any of my readers are under medical treat- ment on account of this painful malady, let _them not be unduly depressed by the circu stances attending the death of Mr. James Ricll- ardson, whose funeral took place on Saturday last. It is not at all uncommon for people so afflicted to reach the allotted span. May I add a. few encouraging Words of personal experi- ence, trusting those who are not specially con- cerned to skip this paragraph? At the early age of 29 I was suddenly prostrated by illness, and my complaint was diagnosed as angina pectoris. Thenceforward for some time I "suffered many things of many physicians," among them being an eminent heart specialist, of Manchester, now deceased, whose prescription nearly killed me. For years I was subject to alarming attacks in the small hours of the morning, but was generally well on the way to recovery by the time the doctor arrived. I used to stay at home for days after, to get back my strength; but I stopped all that sort of thing long ago. Nowadays, when the worst comes, I inhale amyl nitrate, which I have by me in glass capsules wrapped in cotton wool. In the morning I rise and go about as usual— feeling a bit washed-out, it is true; still, I never fail to recover my strength within 24 hours of the attack. Now for my reason for burdening this column with these particulars. I have passed my 60th year, so that for fully half my life I have been more or less handi- capped by a distressing heart affection; but I think I am now better, assuredly I am not Worse, than I was when the symptoms first be- came manifest. I strongly urge younger fellow- sufferers to face the enemy boldly and be deter- mined to overcome. A patient with heart weakness may easily die of fright. Well, then, refuse to he frightened. I have finally decided that I shall not die of "angina" at all. More than 20 years ago. I was told by a doctor that I.had only six months to live. I said I would make it at least :Ai times that, and—well, here I am. It, is surprising what these ,poor bruised and batted bodies of ours can stand iyhen we make up our minds not to give in until we can bold out no more.</p>
        <p>Ass-tronomical. The item "Not an Airship Light" in last week's local news reminded me of an amusing incident which occurred in a Yorkshire town in which I -used to live. A humble resident became possessed of a good-sized telescope, and on fine nights was often to be seen studying the heavenly bodies with its aid. The town lay in a deep valley, and the main road into Lanca- shire skirted an adjacent hip. Once, with his much-prized instrument poihted in that direc- tion, he excitedly called upon a friend who was passing to "have peep at Saturn with its rings." The, invitation was eagerly accepted, but to the disgust of the amateur astronomer the other declared the object to be 'a gas- 14t) on the Burnley-road" !</p>
        <p>Howlers.</p>
        <p>The paragraph headed "What a hen lays on" brought to mind a few others which may be reproduced as somewhat, appropriate. The astronomical reference preceding this recalls that of the youthful student who wrote in his examination paper "Leap year is so, called because the earth goes round the sun for three years, but in the fourth year it leaps over it." At present we are all more or less interested in English history, with special reference to war, and I /IN, add these delightful samples respecting The Young Pretender was so called'becanse it was pretended that he was born in a frying-pan." "Lord Pay-leigh was the first man to see the Invisible Armada." "The father of Ijenry IV. was John O'Groats." "The American War .was started because the people would persist in sending their parcels through the post without stamps?, "Prince William wa.s drowned in a. butt of Malmsey wine; he never laughed again- "Richard IL • , is said to have been murdered by some histor- ians; his real fate is uncertain.: :There are fire continents, a, e, o, and U. St. Andrew is the patent saint of Scotland. The patent saint of England is Union Jack." An old soldier is called a vegetarian."</p>
        <p>WHY TROUBLE TO CHOP SUET for Puddings or Pastry? Buy Shredded ATORA Beef Suet, ready for use, goes further, sweet and ,wholesome. Your grocer sells it. Insist upon ATORA, refuse substituted brand's.</p>
        <p>My dear Children,— ' I have no desire to bother you with a long letter this week, as_l know you are hack at 'school once mora.and must be Very busy With your new lessons. I hope to be able to an- nounce a new compctition shortly. Below I give some riddles, sent in by our enthusiastic contributor, Fred Shelley. Your. loving UNCLE JACK.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="4" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>SOME RIDDLES.</head>
        <p>SOME RIDDLES.</p>
        <p>_ Why is Westminster Abbey like a fender?— Because it contains the ashes of the great (grate). What bird is a weight-lifter?—A crane.</p>
        <p>STORY OF THE SARDINE. Whila'scanning a daily PP" the other day an advertisement headed in bold type arrested my attention. On perusal I diseovered it to be _a glowing account of the grandeur of the Nor- wegian lakes, which could be witnessed by the outlay of a very modest sum of money. A burning, :unquenchable desire to view the exquisite beauty of the lakes overwhelmed me; but, alas the sum mentioned, so meagre from the advertiser's point of view,. represented quite a little fortune to me. Consequently, I tad to adopt the route by which even the poorest can travel,—the white, winding road of imagination. From its siimmit I gazed upon the azure, sun- kissed waters of the Norwegian fjoi us, and drank in the loveliness of the surrounding scenery, which filled me with delight. Suddenly I remembered that in these pictur- esque fjords, which seem to breathe an air of peace and security, dwelt numbers of little fishes, which crowd in from the open sea in huge shoals. There, amicbt the magniacent scenery of the Norwegian eat, are the leeding beds of the silvery little fishes commonly knov n as "Skipper Sardines." , During the sunny summer days they take up their abode in what they believe to be a haven of rest and safezy ; but alas! the fate awaiting them proves to )e quite the reverse. Fishermen, provided with modern equipment, including . swift-moving motor boats, come from distant Stavanger. With marvellous *alacrity and accuracy, im- mense numbers of ,the fish are surrounded by pets, and their. doom is sealed. They are car- ried back by their captors to Stavanger, where hundreds of fair Norwegian maidens thread their silvery bodies on long wires, and they are then quickly transported to the curing rooms. Here small fires of oak bark are kept burning continuously, and after about fifteen minutes' smoking, the bright glitter of the silver fish is changed to bronze. The curing being completed, heads, tails, and fins are re- moved, and the little fish are packed carefully into tins. The remaining operations—boiling, cooling, wrapping, and labelling—are left until the fishing season is over, and these employ many hundreds of people to whom this is their only source of livelihood. • Ido not suppose that nany of your readers— though fond of the fish—are aware of the various processes through which they have to pass, and I trust the above description will prove of interest to them.</p>
        <p>THE GOBLIN GLEN. - I crept last night down the moonlit track, Down the moonlit track on the mountain side, The wind sprites howled in an angry pack, And bent the pines with a shivering crack</p>
        <p>O'er the chasm wide; - But I hid in the shadows out of their ken,, And hurried along to the Goblin Glen.</p>
        <p>Softly I crept 'mid the swaying reeds That grew by the side of the rippling creek, Softly I crept 'mid the stagnant weeds, And, trembling, I knelt and told my beads, For my heart was weak • As the water which slipped from the brook, and then Bubbled its way through the Goblin Glen.</p>
        <p>The pale moon shone with a tranquil light, And, lo! on the breeze a murmur stole Of shrill, sweet voices and laughter bright That stirred the depths of the dusky night To its inmost soul, And the voices came echoing oft again Through the shadowy nooks in the Goblin Glen.</p>
        <p>Parting the rushes, I peered about, And a strange, wild scene met my frightened</p>
        <p>gaze— Small dancing figures moved in and out, With many an elfin cry and shout, ' In a mystic maze. Backwards and forwards and round again— 'T-was the fairy dance in the Goblin Glen.</p>
        <p>Glittering fire-Ries illumined the scene With an ever-changing colour and light, And in the coil moon's silver sheen The dew-drop gladdened the woodland green With its snarkle bright. Oh, the wonder! It held me still just then By the reed-grown creek in the Goblin Glen.</p>
        <p>But a pebble slipped 'neath my aching knee. And forward I fell with a cry of fright, Then, raising my head again to see,. Only the moon in cold majesty Ruled the silent night. Not a trace or sound of the elfin men. Disturbed the peace of the Goblin Glen.</p>
        <p>But, far away on.the mountain height. I heard, on the breeze, an angry shriek. As though the elves had paused in their flight, And had sent a curse through the magic night On the mortal weak Who had watched their revels without their ken And I shuddered and crossed myself again, As I hastened away from the Goblin Glen.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>INDEPENDENT RECHABITES.</head>
        <p>INDEPENDENT RECHABITES.</p>
        <p>Members Engaged in the War.</p>
        <p>The quarterly meeting of the above district was held in the Brunswick-road Schoolroom, Earlestown, on Saturday. The Council, consist- ing of 15 district officers and 36 representatives, Sisters Ashurst, Knox and Simeock, represent- ing the female tents, was presided over bjr Bro. , J. R. Barker, D.C.R. The D.S. presented his report, which showed I that for :the past quarter sickness amongst the members had been exceptionally heavy, while the claims for funeral benefits had been com- paratively light. The membership of the dis- trict both voluntary and State, had reached the total of 3,078 members. The executive reported that during the quar- ter several loans to members for purchasing their own houses had been Cleared off, and one or two other mortgages had been arranged; also the Investments Committee had visited all the properties, which was considered satisfactory. The executive submitted the following resolu- tion: "That any members of .the district unable to meet their contributions owing to being called up for active service, or through distress conse- quent of the war, should be forwarded on to the D.S. for consideration and assistance, given from the district special fund." This was en- dorsed.</p>
        <p>Bro.' Peter Howard, D.S.J.T., „presented his report of the quarterly meeting of the Juvenile District, held at Orrell, which showed that the district consisted of 24 juvenile branches, with a total membership of 1,688, being an increase of 88 members during the term; also one new tent had been instituted at Stockton Heath. with 29 members. The total juvenile funds amounted to .4511 Bs. M.</p>
        <p>It was recommended that owing to the war, superintendents and secretaries of juvenile tents should keep a strict look-out, and should they find any juveniles not being able to meet their contributions, the tents should arrange to tem- porarily meet their payments until the next dis- trict meeting. The report was adopted. The State business was then proceeded with, minutes of the last meeting passed, and pay- ments made during the quarter were sanctioned. Bro. S. Fernley, permanent State secretary, stated that a suite of rooms had been taken in Legh-street, Warrington, for a district office, and would be ready for September Ist. Bro. S. Fernley was appointed secretary and Bro. T. Massey treasurer for the next term. It was de- cided/to accept the invitation of the "Excel- sior " Tent, Padgate, far the next district meet- ing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>TILE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, AUGUST 22, 1914.</head>
        <p>TILE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, AUGUST 22, 1914.</p>
        <p>„... , 10,1 , T' . , T , ~. ...„- .., - * . RI , „,.. , . _....„... ..,_</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>The Sewerage Scheme.—lt was explained at the monthly meeting of the Parochial Com- mittee on Tuesday that the report re Stockton Heath sewerage had not yet come to hand.—The Chairman: That means we shall hare to wait a little bit longer.</p>
        <p>• Clerk as Soldier.—At the monthly meeting of the Stockton Heath Parcichial Committee on Tuesday, the Deputy Clerk explained that the Clerk (Mr. G. P. Ashton) had been granted permission to absent 'himself from those meet- ings in order to take up his military _duties.— The Chairman : Mr. Ashton is giving his time to his country, at any rate</p>
        <p>A Flooded Street.—Councillor MeDermid com- plained at the meeting of the Parochial Corti- mittee on Tuesday that until quite recently the southerly end of Warburton-street had been under water, which was very objectionable to the residents there.—It was decided to call the attention of the Surveyor to the matter. '</p>
        <p>Again Delayed.—The Ship Canal Company wrote to the Parochial Committee, which met on Tuesday, in. regard to the application to erect a public convenience near Twenty Steps Bridge. They explained that Mr. Dunlop, who had the matter in hand, had been off work owing to illness, and therefore the matter would not be gone into for a few weeks.</p>
        <p>Members Present.—There were present at the monthly meeting of the Stockton Heath Paro- chial Committee, held in the Council Schools on Tuesday evening, Councillors G. Pilling .(pre- siding), J. Whitley, J. llallows, F. Pearson, F. Rose, W. Mclntyre, C. Dukes, and A. Bottom- ley, with Messrs. T. H. Warburton (deputy clerk), S. Wright (surveyor), and E. Bradshaw (clerk to the Parish Council).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>TO RELIEVE DISTRESS. A Special War Committee Formed.</head>
        <p>TO RELIEVE DISTRESS. A Special War Committee Formed.</p>
        <p>The Runcorn Rural District Council decided at a meeting on Tuesday to form a number of committees to deal with the various parishes of the district in grivps in regard to distress. We understand that a committee has been formed for Stockton Heath, Latchford Without and Walton Inferior. At the meeting of the Parochial Committee on Tuesday, Councillor Bottomley asked if any- thing had been dime in regard to the formation of a relief committee for the district. Did the wives of those who had gone out know where to go for relief until such time as they got it from the Government?</p>
        <p>The Chairman: We have had a meeting to- day and everything will be in .working order before the week is out. Councillor Bottomley : Are you in communica- tion with the Soldiers' and Sailors' Families' Association?</p>
        <p>The Chairman: No. With the County Coun- cil.</p>
        <p>Councillor Bottomley : That is for a relief fund brought about by dislocation of trade. I mean has anything been done for the wives of Army or Navy reservists? The Chairman: The committee will meet im- mediately, and all cases will be dealt with where husbands have gone to the front. Councillor Bottomley said that if the com- mittee could (Pal with the cases he referred to, they were in a rather different position from the committees in Lancashire. He thought the committee would find they could not deal with these cases. However, he did not think this mattered, as by the time the committee's ar- rangements were working, these people would be getting relief from the Government.</p>
        <p>Co.uncillor John Hallows said they would have a kood working'committee in Stockton Heath. The Chairman: We shall have very little dis- tress in a district like ours.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>VICTORIA-SQUARE PASSAGE. Question of Making It Wider.</head>
        <p>VICTORIA-SQUARE PASSAGE. Question of Making It Wider.</p>
        <p>A plan of the Victoria-square passage was sub- mitted at the Parochial. Committee meeting on Tuesday, from Mr. R. Garnett (surveyor under the private street works). The Deputy-Clerk said he had received a telephone message from Mr. Garnett, suggesting that the corner of the county police premises should be taken down if the committee could see their way to accede to his Suggestions. It would mean that a corner of the building would be used for the passage. The county authorities adhered to their previous offer that if the Coun- cil took the part down at all they would only agree in consideration of the Council foregoing any claim that the Council had on them for a contribution to private street works. The cost of the private street works against the county was £lB. Added to that the Council would havq the cost of taking down the wall, rebuilding in a new line, and paving the space they had un- covered.</p>
        <p>-The Chairman (Councillor Pilling): It means another ot3o. The Deputy-Clerk : About X 25. Councillor Hallows: That has been up before and we did not accept it. Councillor Pearson: If we don't accede to their request, what shall we do? The Chairman: It will remain as it is. Councillor Bose: Is the passage to be used by vehicles?</p>
        <p>The Chairman: No. Councillor Rose: If it is only for pedestrians, there is no need to take the wall down.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: F;ill only widen one por- tion of the corner and leave the other part.</p>
        <p>Councillor Rose: I propose that we do not accept the terms of the County Council, and that the matter remains as it is. - -</p>
        <p>---- -- Councillor Hallows: I don't see why we should spend ,t 45 over it. The Chairman: It will mean more than that. Councillor Hallows: I second the resolution. Councillor McDermid : Could we not meet on the spot? • The Chairman : I think it is wide enough for pedestrians. Councillor Dukes: I move that the matter be deferred until the next meeting. The Chairman: It is hot wise to do that. Councillor Pose: If the passage is used for vehicles, it will be more dangerous than it is now.</p>
        <p>The amendment was withdrawn, and the ques- tion of the blacksmith's sign was then discussed. The Chairman: If we allow the sign to re- main where it is, it cannot be very much in the way. __</p>
        <p>Councillor Rose: Whom can we approach to have the road stopped for vehicles? •</p>
        <p>The Chairman :•The District Council. Councillor Hallows: I think they would if we recommended it.</p>
        <p>The Chairman Will someone move that we recommend the District Council to put posts UP?</p>
        <p>Councillor Bottomley: It will not stop traffic going to the smithy? The Chairman: No. The resolution as suggested by the 'Chairman was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>HIGH LEGIT.</head>
        <p>HIGH LEGIT.</p>
        <p>Called to His Regiment.—Another adjourn- ment was ordered by the magistrates at the Lymm Petty Sessions on Wednesday in the case in which Mr. Dudley Aukland, of St. Helens, was summoned for failing to have a lighted rear lamp attached to his rotor Cycle and sidecar.— The Clerk stated that a letter had been received stating that defendant had been called to join his regiment. —An adjournment for inquiries to be made was ordered.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>JOHNSTON WATSON'S</head>
        <p>JOHNSTON WATSON'S</p>
        <p>Scientifically fitted GLASSES cure HEADACHES. SIGHT TESTING OPTICIAN, 84, BRIDGE ST., Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>HATTON SHOW.</head>
        <p>HATTON SHOW.</p>
        <p>The 20th Annual Event Produces a Splendid Exhibition.</p>
        <p>THE PRIZE LIST.</p>
        <p>The Hatton. Hortichltural Society held its 20th show on Saturday, in a field lent for the occasion by Mr. R. L. Warburton. The Vi „r of Daresbury, in introducing Mrs. Henry Greenall, of Hill Cliffe, spoke of the great pleasure he had in performing that duty, and as Mrs. Greenall was coming to be / a neighbour amongst them, he gave her a hearty welcome in the name of the parishioners. Mrs. Greenall, in opening the show, said it was twelve years since she performed that cere- mony before. She was surprised but pleased at the splendid growth the show had made. She specially advised young women to ,take an in- terest in horticultural pursuits as an interest- ing as well as profitable hobby.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
