son; 2, E. Taylor; 3, Jas. Kippax. Cos Lettuce: 1, E. Taylor; 2, John Taylor; 3, T. Britian&amp; Carrots: 1, Jas. Kippax; 2, J. Miller; 3, T. Britland. Turnips: 1, T. Britland; 2, T. Wil- kinson; 3, F. Kippax. Beetroots: 1, Jas. Kip- pax; 2, E. Mills; 3, J. Spring. Parsnips: 1, T. Wilkinson; 2 and 3, J. Spring. Sprays Pars- ley: 1, E. Taylor; 2, J. Taylor; 3, Jas. Kippax. Tomatoes (red): 1 and 9 P. Cliffe; 3, J. Green., Rhubarb: 1, H. L. Williams; 2, E. Taylor; 3, T. Britland. Collection of Herbs: 1, T. Brit- land ; 2, E. Taylor; 3, J. Taylor. Collection of Vegetables, 6 varieties, outside grown; 1, Jas. Kippax; 2, T. Britland; 3, F. Kippax ; be, T. Wilkinson.</p>
        <p>Fruit.-Apples (cooking) : 1, T. Britland ; 2,11. L. Williams; 3, A. Platt. Apples (dessert): 1, P. Cliffe; 2, Platt; 3, T. Britland.- Pears; 1 and 2, J. Miller. Gooseberries: 1 and 2, J. Mil- ler; 3, E. Mills. Currants (red): 1 and 2, E. Taylor; 3, J. Taylor. Currants (black) : 1, J. Miller; 2, T. Britland; 3, E. Mills. Plums: 1 and 3, J. Rutter; 2, J. Cliffe. • M iscellaneous Classes.--Sweet Peas (distinct varieties), 12 sprays in each vase, pea foliage only allowed 1, H. L. Williams; 2, P. Cliffe; 3, E. Taylor. Collection of Cut Blooms, in vases, arranged for effect, not less than 12 varieties, to be cut from the open garden: 1, P. Cliffe; 2, F. Kippax ;3, E. Taylor. Special prizes for the bet two specimens Buttonhole Bouquets of Gar- den Flowers, one for lady and one for gentle- man (not grown under glass), arranged by ex- hibitors 1, Miss Woods; 2, P. Cliffe; 3, Missl E. M. Hatton. Collections of Wild Flowers and Grasses, &amp;c.-1 For children between the ages of 10 and 14 years: 1, John Harrison; 2, Percy Evans; 3, Percy Cliffe. For children under 10 years of age: 1, Eva Done; 3, Gertie Harrison. Special Prizes, for the prettiest Floral Decoration for the dinner table (lady competitors only): 1, Mrs. A. Leyland ; 2, Miss D. Swifts; 3, Miss Woods; he, Miss E. M. Hatton: Four 11b. Pots Honey (produce of exhibitor's 'own bees), gathered during 1914: 1, E. Iratton ; 2, W. S. Basnett; 3, S. George;be, J. Ward. Three Sections of Comb Honey (produced by exhibitor's own bees during 1914), approximate weight 31b. :1, F. Hatton; 2, W. S. Basnett. Six Brown Hen Eggs: 1, Exors. J. Blackshaw ; 2, T. B. Blackshaw ; 3, J. Har- greaves. Six White Hen Eggs: 1, T. H. Fair- hurst; 2, J. Hargreaves; 3, S. Read. Special Prizes for Home-made Bread: 1, Miss M. J. Elli- son; 2, Miss Derbyshire; 3, Mrs. T. H. Fair- hurst.</p>
        <p>Farmers' Classes.—Special Prizes for 4 half- pounds of Butter (slightly salted): 1, Miss A. Fairhurst; 2, Exors. of J. Blackshaw ; 3, Mrs. H. Hankey. Potatoes: 1, R. L. Warburton; 2, Mrs. H. Hankey. Swede Turnips: 1, T. B. Blackshaw. ; 2, S. Read; 3. W. Fowles. Common Turnips: 1, S. Read; 2, R. L. Warburton; 3, T. H. Fairhurst. Mangels (long red): 1. T. IT. Fairhurst. 2, R. L. Warburton.; 3, A. Broome. Mangels (Yellow Globe): 1, T. H. Fairhurst; 2, T. 1. Blackshaw. ; 3, S. Read. Wheat: 1, R. Tickle; 2, S. Read; 3, W. Fowles; he, T. B. Blackshaw• Oats (white) : 1, T. H. Fairhurst; 2, W. Fowles; 3, Exors. J. Blackshaw. Farm Produce (six varieties) : 1, R. L. Warburton; 2, A. Broome; 3, Mrs. H. Hankey. Yellow Man- gels: 1. T. I-J. Fairhurst ; 2, T. B. Blackshaw. Swede Turnitis : 1. H. Brocklehurst ; 2, Exors. J. Blacksbaw. Yellow Globe Mangels (special prizes by Gartons Ltd.): 1, T. B. Blackshaw; 2,,,</p>
        <p>A. Broome. Swede Turnips (special prizes by Gartons Ltd.): 1, T. B. Blackshaw; 2, S. Read. Yellow Globe Mangels: 1, T H. Fairhurst; 2, T. Whitlow. Purple or Green Top Swede: 1, T. Whitlow; 2, 'l'. H. Fairhurst. Mangels (any one variety): 1, W. Fowles; 2, R. Tickle.. Swede Turnips (any one variety) : 1, W. Fowles. Man- gel: 1, T. H. Fairhurst; 2, R. L. Warburton; 3, S. Read. Swedes: 1, S. Read; 2, T. Whitlow; 3, R. L. Warburton Mangels (any one variety): 1, T. H. Fairhurst; 2, W. Fowles; 3, S. Read. Swedes (any one variety) 1, W. Fowles; 2, S. Read; 3, R. Tickle. Potatoes (any one variety): 1, R. L. Warburton; 2, I'. H. Fairhurst; 3, S. Read. Special Prizes for box of 20 Potatoes: 1, S. Read; 2, R. L. Warburton; 3, T. H. Fair- hurst.</p>
        <p>Poultry.—Wyandotte Cock (any variety): 1, Stanley Done. Wyandotte Hen (any variety) : 1 and 2, J. W. Basnett; 3, Mrs. I. Hewitt; he, S. Done. White Orpington Cock (any variety) : 1, J. W. Basnett; 3, Mrs. I. Hewitt; he, R. L. Warburton. White Orpington Hen ( any variety) : 1 and he, B. Parsonage, jun.; 2, 'qrs. I. Hewitt; 3, J. W. Basnett. Orpington Cock (any other colour or variety) : 1, J. W. Basnett; 2, Mrs. I. Hewitt. Orpington Hen (any other colour or variety) : 1 and lie, Mrs. I. Hewitt; 2, B. Parsonage, jun.; he, J. W. Basnett. Leghorn Cock (any variety): 1 and he,. Mrs. I. Hewitt. Leghorn Hen (any variety) : 1, Mrs. I. Hewitt. Rock Cock (any variety) : 1 and 2, A. • Cowap. Rock Hen (any variety) : 1, 2 and he, A. Cowap; he, Mrs. T. Ditchfield. Rhode Island Red Cock (any variety) : 1, 2 and 3, Rev. Johnstone; he, W: Dickinson and W. Simpson.. Rhode Island Red Hen (any variety) : 1 and 2, Rev. Johnstone; he, Mrs. I. Hewitt. Bantam Cock (any variety) : 1, A. Cowap; 2, E. Bretherton ; he, J. Green and W. Fowles. Bantam Hen (any variety): 1, E. Bretherton ;2, A. Cowap; he, W. Fowles. Cock (any variety), pure or cross bred, for table purposes: 1, Mrs. I. Hewitt; he, Mrs. Hodgkin- son. Hen (any variety), pure or cross bred, for laying purposes: 1, Mrs. I. Hewitt. Indian Runner Drake: 1, J. W. Basnett;, lie, Mrs. T. Ditchfield. Indian Runner Duck: 1, J. W. Bas- nett; he, Mrs. T. Ditehfield. Drake (any other variety or colour) : 1, R. L. Warburton; 2, Mrs. John Whitlow; he, T. B. Blackshaw. Duck (any other variety or colour) : 1, R. L. Warbur- ton. Special Prizes for the best Cpek or Hen, any variety, whose owner has never been awarded a prize for poultry at any show before: 1, S. Done; 2, W. Simpson; 3, 'Mrs. Evans; hc, T. Rutter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>LYMM.</head>
        <p>LYMM.</p>
        <p>Hospital Caretakers.—At a special meeting of the Lymm Urban District Council on Monday night, Mr. T. Warburton presiding, Mr. and Mrs. John Carp were appointed caretakers at the Hospital. Five married couples made appli- cation.</p>
        <p>Strategy.—A Lymm policeman on night duty recently asked a cyclist why he was riding with- out a light, and received the reply that "It had just gone out." The policeman, however, found there was no lamp on the cycle This story was related at the Lymm Petty Sessions on Tuesday.</p>
        <p>Uncertificated Midwife.—At the Lymm Petty Sessions on Wednesday Mrs. Lena Leather, Agden-lane, Broom Edge, was summoned for practising as a midwife, she not being a woman certified under the Midwives Act.—Dr. Mere- dith Young, medical officer of Cheshirte, prose- cuted, and Mr. Arthur Browne, solicitor, War- rington, defended.—The case was dismissed, but costs were not allowed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>LYII3I BATHERS.</head>
        <p>LYII3I BATHERS.</p>
        <p>Time Limit Fixed by the Council for</p>
        <p>Bathing in the Dam.</p>
        <p>The lake and some of its admirers were the cause of strong expressions being made at the special meeting of the Lymm Urban District Council on Monday evening. Councillor Gotobed said he must call the attention of the Council to the bathing in the lake. He did not object to the bathing itself, but he did object, and very strongly, too, in the interests of decency, to the way in which it was being carried on.</p>
        <p>Councillor Groves; On Saturday night it was disgraceful:</p>
        <p>The Chairman later said they all knew that bathing should not be allowed in the day time; Councillor Kershaw: It should not be at night time. (Laughter.) Councillor Groves suggested a notice pointing out that bathing was strictly prohibited. Councillor Parker: Monstrous. _</p>
        <p>- Councillor Gotobed : I have no objection to the bathing, provided they are properly cos- tumed.</p>
        <p>Councillor Kershaw suggested that Councillor Groves' idea would only provide an appoint- ment for a sign writer.</p>
        <p>A TIME LIMIT.</p>
        <p>- - - • Councillor Gotobed said there was such a thing as fixing hours of bathing, and he sug- gested that it be allowed before eight o'clock in the morning, provided the bathers were pro- perly costumed..</p>
        <p>Councillor Critchley : I support it. Councillor Ingham: There ought to be a time limit. , Councillor Gotobed's suggestion was agreed to; bathers to take their own risks. Councillor Cowper asked if all the county was to be allowed to bathe there. They came "from Warrington and all around." Councillor Groves later queried if they were going to allow people from "all over the globe" to come into Lymm. Councillor Critchley : They will not; come from towns before eight o'clock in the morning They have their own baths there.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>LYMM COUNCIL EMPLOYES.</head>
        <p>LYMM COUNCIL EMPLOYES.</p>
        <p>Half-wages to be Paid to Those Who Have Offered Themselves for Service.</p>
        <p>The agenda for Monday's special meeting of the L,ymm Urban District Council contained the following motion, submitted by Councillor, R. H. Gotobed</p>
        <p>"That all employes of the Council called out, or who may volunteer for service during the war, be paid half wages during their absence, an.d that their positions be kept open for them on their return."</p>
        <p>The Clerk (Mr. Wm. MuHard) said he had written to the Local Government Board with reference to the legality, and had received the following reply, which quoted the words 'a Mr. Herbert Samuel: "I am advised that it is com- petent to any local authority to grant leave of absence to persons in their employ who have been called out for active service as members of the Army Reserve or Territorial Forces, and to make reasonable payment in respect of their wages or salaries to persons authorised by 'them to receive the money. The question whether any notification to local authorities on the sub- ject is necessary will receive consideration. The wages book should be signed by the wife or other person who is authorised by the employe to re- ceive the payment." Councillor Gotobed said very few words of his were needed, as they were all patriots. When they had men who were going out to risk their lives for the country, the Council could not do less than see that those who were left behind were provided for. Councillor Groves seconded. Councillor Lockyer asked if the Clerk had received any notification of the action of the doctors, for Dr. Leonard Thorpe had informed him that the medical men in the district had decided to attend sick cases in the families of the men called away without making a charge. The Clerk: I have heard nothing. The annsuncement was responsible for several complimentary references being uttered. Councillor Cowper said it was magnificent that they had married men who were willing to serve the country. They deserved the praise of every one of them, much to the disgrace of a lot of young fellows, who were doing nothing at all. The Chairman said he agreed with every word that had been said. They could not do too much for the men who had gone away. Councillor Ingham: I am of that opinion, too. The motion was carried.</p>
        <p>Four Gladiolas (annual).-1, ;0„ 2, ' Four Gladiolas (H.P.).-1, T. Sn''' ; Hampson. Group of Cut Flowers.-1 s and specia` ilver medal, T. Smith; 2, L HatuPs,.°ll,;,nuni." Twelve blooms Chrysanthemum 1, T. Smith; 2, J. Goulden; 3, J. W,allltte. Light" foot; 3, P. H. Davies. ensteunsl, T. Smith; 3 J 1' Three sprays Japanese Chrysan_ B. Darlington; 2, G. E. Carman. kheuse; Bouquet of Wild Flowers.-1, B. 133 C 2, J. S. Thomas; 3, A. Aim Best named tray of Wild Flowers, ieties.-1, S. Bingham; 2, N. Backliouse,;, Best named tray of Wild Flower,i 33, r, ieties.-1, Doris Bingham; 2, N• vtailef; 1.</p>
        <p>Davies,</p>
