      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>PENKETH SHOW.</head>
        <p>PENKETH SHOW.</p>
        <p>Garden and Cage Bird Society hold</p>
        <p>an Attractive Exhibition.</p>
        <p>DETAILS AND PRIZE WINNERS. `*</p>
        <p>The Penketh, Sankey and District Garden and Cage Bird Society held their 21st annual exhi- bition at WarTington-road, Penketh, on Satur- day, in fine weather, and with a fair attendance of visitors. ,Mr. J. B. Aitken (in the absence of the Presi- dent, Miss Bolton) opened the show. In an introductory speech he pointed out that this was the coming-of-age of this event. In its earlier stages it was held in the Penketh Infants' School, but it had grown to such proportions that a huge tent was required to hold the ex- hibits. From the commencement he had never failed to put in an appearance, and was de- lighted to see what good work was being done year after year. Owing to the unsettled state of the country it was doubtful whether one should be held this year, but the committee eventually decided to rid themselves of the thought of war for the nonce and carry out the looked-for annual event. The show was then declared open.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>KEEN COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>KEEN COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>A brief look round showed that there was keen competition in various classes. The junior section especially was well patronised, and in the bouquet of wild flowers, best tray of wild flowers, best essay on wild flowers and best coloured illustrations of plants, showing clearly the children's conception of the flora of the countryside, there were good specimens. Display exhibits of carnations by Mr. J. Car- rington and Mr. G. Taylor (Penketh), and roses by Mr. Byron (Bold) helped to enhance the worthy efforts of the Staging Committee. The judges were: Garden section, Mr. G. Haigh (Woolton), Mr. J. Stoney (Liverpool); bird section, Mr. A. Wellings ; bread section, Mr. Johnson; essay and brush work, Mr. James Watson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>PRIZE WINNERS.</head>
        <p>THE PRIZE WINNERS.</p>
        <p>The winners in the various classes were as follow :</p>
        <p>,iest kept Flower Garden (large).-1, T. Smith; 2, 1. Hampson; 3, J. Berry. - Best Cottage Garden, without greenhouse (medium).-1, J. Ray; 2, G. E. Carman. Best Cottage Garden, without greenhouse (small).-1, J. Berry. Cottage Garden, with greenhouse.-1, T. Plant; 2, J. H. Davies. Kitchen Garden.-1, W. H. Roberts; 2, J. Car- man;3, T. Smith. New Allotment.-1, I. Hampson ; 2, G. E. Car- man; 3, W. Scott. Climbing R05e.71,..J. Berry; 2, G. E. Carman.</p>
        <p>. Window Plant (foliage variety), no flowers.- 1, W. Lightfoot; 2, J. Ray. Window Plant, flowering.-2, T. Plant. Pour Cottage Greenhouse Plants.-1, I. Hamp- son ; 2, T. NA._</p>
        <p>Best Cottage Garden Greenhonse.-1, J. H. Davies; 2, T. Plant. Best Stand of Plants and Cut Flowers from cottage garden and 4reenhouse.-1. T. Plant. Twelve Roses (open):-1, C. E. Parker; 3, J. Whalley. Twelve Roses, hybrid teas and teas.-1, J. 11. Davies.</p>
        <p>Six Roses, H.T.-1, J. Moores; 2, W. Pye; 3, J. Appleton. Six Roses, tea scented noigette.-1, J. Moores; 2, J. Whalley.</p>
        <p>Six Roses, 11. P.-1:, J. Whalley; 2,G. E. Car- man.</p>
        <p>Six Sprays Roses, climbing varieties.-1, W. Pye; 2, J. Waine. Three Roses, H.P.-1. W. Scott; 2, J. Whalley. Three Roses, H.T.-1, J. Ray; 2, D. Fowles; 3, W. Dwerryhouse.</p>
        <p>Collection of Sweet Peas.-1, W. Dwerryhouse; 2, I. Hampson 3, T. Smith. Twelve Spikes of Sweet Peas, six sorts (special prize by Titterington, of Preston).-1, W. Light- foot.</p>
        <p>Three distinct varieties of Sweet Peas, six sprays each.-1, W. Dwerryhouse; 2, W. Light- foot; 3, J. H. Davies. Hollyhocks.-1, I. ITampson; 2. J. Waine. - Antirrhinums.-1, J. H. Davies; 2, J. Ray; 3, I. Hampson. Asters.-1, J. Goulding; 2, W. Roberts. Carnations.-1 and special, J. Carman; 2, J. Ray; 3, 0. E. Carman. Phlox.-1, J. Carman; 2, J. Smith. Six Violas.-1, T. Smith; 2, W. Dwerryhouse. Twelve Violas.-1, 11. C. Bingham; 2, T. Smith. . .</p>
        <p>Eighteen Violas, three varieties.—l, T. Smith; 2, J. IL Davies.) Six Pansies.-1, W. H. Roberts. Six Dahlias.-1, W. H. Roberts. Dahlias (cactus).—l, J. Goulden; 2, W. Roberts. Six Dahlias (peony).-1, J. Goulden. Dahlias (pompon).---1., W. D. Roberts; 2, J. Goulden. Marigolds. two varieties.-1, W. H. Roberts; 2, J. Goulden. Four Gladiolas, any variety.-1, I. Hampson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <p>THE</p>
        <p>,•:**) • ~:ok) 4:4 4:4.0 •:•• 41:4 0)11)4:1040 0:”.) *7/. 0:4, 4:1 4. 07.0, 4:0 0:0 4:lr 4.) 0:,.• I.) 4:0 1:1 41.:”:":11 40,14..4. 044+ +40,400 444&gt; 4. 4:4 41, 4:11. 40 I.) 4:1 .10 4401, 00 •:.• 4::•• 4:9 ttO 11:4.• 4:0 0:0 •:11, 4"• ) • colours of the Moment for blouses, costumes, and the little coats worn with white dresses, are deep orange, emerald green, sealing wax red, all neutral tints, and parchment white.</p>
        <p>also thoroughly understand field sanitation, and have gained military experience with the troops at Aldershot. Perhaps the finest nursing or- ganisation in the world is Queen Alexandra's Imperial Military Nursing Service, which is always prepared for any emergency. Hundreds of fully qualified nurses have this week offered their services to this organisation. Each nurse must be British born, have had three years' training, possess a certificate—not only for effi- ciency in her work—but for good character. She must also be well educated, and have .a certain social status.</p>
        <p>The Fragile Blouse. The more expensive blouses, of the fragile type, have been exceedingly beautiful this year on account of the delicate handwork which has been lavished on them, not only in the matter of embroideries, but in tucking also. Bunches of tiny tucks are stitched by hand on many of these beautiful garments, fashioned of crepe chiffon and other filmy fabrics. These blouses are very loose, consequently most comfortable to wear. The sash is an important part of them, and the ends are in many instances beau- tifully embroidered by hand to match the cor- sage. With white blouses large ties of black tulle are worn. Many of the autumn blouses have'sleeves of a different material—an econom- ical fashion. There will be a much closer con- nection in the future between the blouse and the skirt with which it is worn. This is at present limited to braces, or points, collar and belt, but the idea will not rest here. It is quite possible that short sleeves will be revived for blouses, although scarcely so suitable for autumn wear. Furs In Autumn.</p>
        <p>Summer furs in strips are charmingly com- bined with chiffon for scarves, stoles and muffs, and dresses of all kinds are trimmed with peltry. An evening dress thus decorated may be worn in summer or winter, which is an advan- tage. At the same time fur of any kind is out of place on a very hot summer day. Tailored suits will have fur collars and cuffs, therefore those women who invested in odd, lengths and pieces of good fur during the sales will have no reason to regret these purchases at summer sale prices. High collars of fur will trim coats, and bands of fur to match will be seen on skirts of tailor-mades. The most expensive, as well as the cheaper furs have been so successfully imi- tated that many of them will be used for trim- ming. Hats will be largely trimmed with the lighter furs, which will be seen in combination with tulle, chiffon and cloth. Brown pelts look remarkably. xvell with tulle in the prevailing shade of .blue. Seal and pony also go with taffetas, especially when shot.</p>
        <p>EXAMINER,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>Women's Help.</head>
        <p>Women's Help.</p>
        <p>• Women in all stations of life are anxious to do what they can to succour our sick and wounded soldiers and sailors, and in many parts of the country they are forming local organisa- tions to divert energy and resources into the most useful channels. All who can sew or knit can make hospital and invalid garments, for which they can obtain the patterns from the British Red Cross Society, Devonshire House, Piccadilly, London. There are eight standard patterns, which the society will forward on re- ceipt of an application and remittance of is. 6d. The Duke of Devonshire has placed his London house at the serrice of the society, and it is now its headquarters. Many wealthy , people are offering the use of their mansions for hospital accommodation. The most effectual way for helping the society is a handsome donation, but people of humble means can only offer the help that is possible to them, and if it takes the form of a gift of a few garments or some soft house- hold linen it will all be useful.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>New Silhouette.</head>
        <p>The New Silhouette.</p>
        <p>All the new wraps, whether in tweed or fur, show the influence of the new line, and coats flare out round the skirts. Sometimes the backs are very full, after the fashion of the cape, whilst the fronts are like a short coat with a sash of fur. All linings are exceedingly hand- some. Last year's models will require drastic alterations if they are to be brought abreast of the new styles. The new line also demands an upright carriage, the slouch, FO Marked a characteriktic of recent modes, will therefore, disappear, nor will this be a matter for much regret, as it was far from smart. Among the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>COOKERY CORNER. Some Useful Dishes.</head>
        <p>COOKERY CORNER. Some Useful Dishes.</p>
        <p>To saute a food is to cook it quickly in a fry- ing pan in a little fat. Boil the sprouts and drain well. Heat a frying pan, put in the but- ter and add the sprouts and seasortings. Place over a hot fire and shake frequently. Cook five minutes and serve hot.</p>
        <p>Cauliflower au Gratin. Place a whole cooked cauliflower on dish for serving, cover with buttered crumbs and place on oven grate to brown crumbs; remove from oven and pour one cup white sauce around cauliflower.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>Beet Greens.</head>
        <p>Beet Greens.</p>
        <p>Beets are usually thickly sown, and as the young beet plants begin to grow, they must be thinned. These young plants make delicious greens, particularly if the root has attained some little size. Do not separate the root from the leaves, but examine them very carefully leaf by leaf. Wash thoroughly in many waters. Cook in plenty of boiling water, adding a tea- spoonful salt for every quart of greens. Boil rapidly until tender, which will take about thirty minutes. Drain, chop rather coarsely, and season with butter and salt.</p>
        <p>Boiled Onions. Put onions in cold water and remove skins while under water. Drain, put in a saucepan and cover with boiling salted water, boil five minutes, drain and again cover with boiling salted water. Cook one hour or until soft, but not broken. Drain five minutes and season with butter, pepper and salt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>Wholesome Drink.</head>
        <p>A Wholesome Drink.</p>
