        <p>During hot, dusty weather, a supply of this should be kept in the house for the, benefit of children, or unexpected callers, to' whom a glass of the beverage will be a veritable boon: Peel -8 lemons very thinly so as to have the rind almost clear. Put this into a large jug with Vb. loaf sugar, a small piece of cinnamon, and, if liked, 2 or 3 cloves. Pour in 3 pints boiling water, and cover. Leave till quite cold, and then add the strained juice of the lemons. Just before use, strain it into another glass jug. The rind may be used again if more boiling water .is poured'on to it, and more sugar added, with the juice of one lemon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>MEMS. FOR MOTHERS. Some Cooling Medicines.</head>
        <p>MEMS. FOR MOTHERS. Some Cooling Medicines.</p>
        <p>Mothers are often puzzled at this season to find a cooling medicine for their children. A medical expert now recommends a small dose of health salts, but mothers should remember that not more than a small teaspoonful should be given to a child under seven years of age. Two teaspoonfuls is a good dose for ah adult. The medical man writes: "Health salts are much in demand at all times, but especially in the summer. There is a perfect host of them on the market, and analysis reveals the fact that practically all of them contain tartaric acid and bicarbonate of soda with Epsom salts, sulphate of soda, and a flavouring agent. Here is a favourite. Bicarbonate of soda, two ounces; powdered tartaric acid, and powdered cream of tartar, of each one ounce and a half; powdered dried sulphate of soda, one ounce; powdered white sugar, six ounces. Another favourite is: Tartaric acid, nine ounces; bicarbonate of sodium, seven ounces; chlorate of potassium, two drachms; dried sulphate of magnesia, three drachms; sugar, three ounces."</p>
        <p>• " Fruit Salts." "It is important to note that the term ' Fruit Salt' is registered, and in all probabiliteany- one using the term fruit salts in a public man- ner might be proceeded against. The best sub- stitute for the most popular fruit salt on the market is as follows: Powdered tartaric acid, powdered bitartrate of potash, and powdered bicarbonate of soda, of each two ounces; sul- phate of magnesia, one ounce; effervescing citrate of magnesia, two ounces; powdered white sugar, four ounces; artificial Carlsbad salts consists of dried sulfil'atte of sodium, twen- ty-two ounces; sulphate of potassium, one ounce; chloride of sodium, nine ounces; bicar- bonate of sodium, eighteen ounces. These in- gredients must be powdered separately and well mixed. Effervescing Carlsbad salts may be made thus: Dried artificial Carlsbad salts, eleven ounces; bicarbonate of sodium, six ounces; tartaric acid, five ounces. Mix and dry, and keep in well stoppered bottles. Ginger beer powder is made as follows: Bruised ginger: one ounce; cream of, tartar, one ounce and a half; oil of lemon, six drops. Dissolve two pounds of sugar with this powder in a gallon of boiling water, then add two gallons of cold water, and when sufficiently cold ferment with yeast. The white of an egg mixed with the yeast facilitates fermentation."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>FUNERAL OF MR. J. RICHARDSON.</head>
        <p>FUNERAL OF MR. J. RICHARDSON.</p>
        <p>.-.L-____.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>Ltst of Mourners and Wreaths.</head>
        <p>Ltst of Mourners and Wreaths.</p>
        <p>The funeral of the late Mr. James Richardson, Palmyra House, Springfield-street, took place at the Warrington Cemetery on Saturday. Many respects to the deceased's memory were shown both at St. Paul's Church and the graveside. The officiating clergyman was the Rev. W. Bracecamp.</p>
        <p>The principal mourners were: Mr. Stanley Richardson (son), Dr. Harold Joseph (son-iii- law), Dr. G. W. Joseph, Messrs. James Richard- son and William Richardson (nephews), S. Bur- dett and D. Whitham, Alderman J. Smethurst. Messrs. H. Bonchier, Herbert Hatton, H Cham- bers, Spencer Wallhead, Sidney Wallhead, W. Hodgkinson, John Mainwaring, W. S. Haddock, T. Welsby, E. Rose, A. Ferguson and Dr. Peacocke.</p>
        <p>Amongst the public organisations represented were:— Warrington Conservative Association. Messrs. G. H. Smith and Herbert Ilayes. Warrington Conservative Club. Messrs: It. Collin, W. H..Bellian and R. 11. Daintith. . . _</p>
        <p>Warrington Cricket and Bowling Club.—Mr. G. W. A. Brown.</p>
        <p>Warrington Society.—Alderman Bennett and Mr._ James Richardson.</p>
        <p>_ The staff of Messrs. W. and J. Richardson were represented by Messrs. John Shaughnessy, W. Thomson, E. Maskell and John Highton. Amongst others present at the 'thurch or at the graveside were: Messrs. Arthur Browne, G. D. Southerden, Cecil Joseph, A. C. Rinks, Leon- ard Henshall, Robson J. Chorley, A. Stanley Wright, John Fairhurst, J. C. Robinson, J. Fairclough, J. Hankinson and E. London. . .</p>
        <p>captain C. F. Bouchier, son-in-law of the late Jr. Richardson, was unable to attend, as he had been ordered to join his regiment._</p>
        <p>_ Floral tributes were sent by the following: His loving son, "Ethel and Charlie," "Harold and Betty," Peter Shailagh and "Constance," " Bunty, Mag, Winnie, Marjorie and Jim," Mrs. Richardson and "Maud," "Billy," "Jack and Gertrude," "Hilda and Cissie," maids and chauffeur; Alderman and Mrs. Smethurst and, family, Mrs. Walter Young and family, Mr.. and I Mrs. Thomas Cooper, Dr. G. W. Joseph, "Reggie and Cecil," Mr. and Mrs. A. J. Shilcock, Ver- non-street Tanning Company, Mr. and Mrs. J. Mainwaring and family, Mr. and Mrs. Arthur Pearson (Kidderminster), Mr. W. S. Haddock, Mr. and Mrs. Chambers, Mr. and Mrs. Gill, Mr. and Mrs. Wells, Mr. and Mrs. Johnson and family (Withington), Mr. and Mrs. Henry Bouchier (Culcheth), office staff of Messrs. W. and J. Richardson, Alderman and Mrs. Tinnion and family, Mr. and Mrs. Welsby, Mr. Charles Bunch, Mrs. Hugh Ferguson and family... Mr. and Mrs. J. E. Birtles, Messrs. Bennett and Bennett, Mr. and Mrs. Arnold Nield, Warring- ton Conservative Association, the Firth Com- pany, Dr. G. W. N. Joseph, Mr. and Mrs. Walter Burdett, Mr. and Mrs. Hodgkinson and family,' "Ellen," Mr. and Mrs. G. Smith, Mr. and Mrs. S. Burdett, Mr. and Mrs. Spencer Wallhead, Mr. H. Collin, Mr. Arthur Browne, Wariring- ton_Conservative Club.</p>
        <p>Messrs. W. Hodgkinson, Ltd., had charge of the funeral arrangements.</p>
      </div>
