      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>SATURDAY, AUGUST 22, 1914.</head>
        <p>SATURDAY, AUGUST 22, 1914.</p>
        <p>BOWLING.</p>
        <p>ORKS' ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>Works' v. Caledonian Association.</p>
        <p>The following were the successful players at the Horse and Jockey Green on Saturday W. Thomason, M.H.; P. Wright, C.; A. War- burton, Whitecross; C. Tranter, M.H.;*W. Ash- brookei Cf.L.; T. Garnett, C.L.; H. France, R.; W. Blackledge, P.K.; J. Simcock, C. and J.; F. Brandwood, P. and K.; E. Coyne, P. and K.; J. Pollard, P.P.; F. Howard, N. H. Higham, P. and K.; H. Broadhurst, C.; J. Miller, C. The charity match between members of the Works' Association and Caledonian Association will take place this afternoon, when a large attendance is anticipated on the Brooklands Hotel Green.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>LANCASHIRE'S SUCCESS. An Easy Win Over Derbyshire.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE'S SUCCESS. An Easy Win Over Derbyshire.</p>
        <p>Lancashire played .their final match in con- nection with the British Crown Green County Association Championship for the " Crosfield" Cup and medals on Saturday. At Buxton and Liverpool they beat the Derbyshire men some- what easily. Scores, Lancashire names first :</p>
        <p>At Buxton. Lancashire won by 85 points. R. Smith Leigh Railway, 20; F. Dawson, Buxton, 21. J. C. Howe, Didsbury, 19; W. Chappell, Bux- ton, 21. G. Johnson, Angel, 21; P. Lord, New Mills, 8. C. W. Smith, Roebuck, 21; H. Moores, Chinley, 12. J. Hart, Wigan Subscription, 21; J. Ford, Chinley, 12. H. S. Connolly, R., 21; E. Emmerson, B'ton, 10 A. Gleave, Warrington Bull's Head, 21; A. Oldfield, Buxton, 10. J. W. Ward, Royal Ex., 21; G. Nadin, Chinley; 12- J. Rothwell, Leigh Railway, 21; W. Bradshaw, Mellor, 14. P. Talbot, Newton-le-Willows, 21; R. Cussens, Whaley, 8. S. Wroe, St. Helens R., 21; T. Walker, Whaley, 17. F. Haslam, Irlams-o'-th'-Height, 21; J. Clay- ton, Buxton, 16. Total—Lancashire, 249; Derbyshire, 164.</p>
        <p>At Liverpool. Lancashire won by 88 points. ' R. Lomax, Eccleston, 21; J. Goodier, Mellor, 19. ' W. Holden, Rainhill, 21; J. W. Thornley, Compstall, 11. J. Gleave, Roebuck, 21; H. Keough, Comp- stall, 13. L. Gardner, Eccleston, 15; J. Smith, Mellor, 21 J. W. G. Coombs, R., .21; C. Gurside, Fairf'd, 10. R. Rothwell, Whitefield, 21; A. Matthews, Osmaston, 6. W. Parr, Rainhill, 21; W. Walker, Comp- stall, 3. J. Mercer, Eccl'ton, 21; E. Dodd, New Mills, 15 A. Greenhalgh, Withins, 17; T. Bagguley, Fairfield, 21. R. Cragg, Park Hotel, 21; G. Lomas, Fair- field, 11. W. Derbyshire, Medway, 21; J. Verity, Comp- stall, 9. R. S. Bridge, Park Hotel, 21; F. Wright, Whaley 8.. 15. Total—Lancashire, 242; Derbyshire, 154. On the home-and-home games Lancashire won by 173 points.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>FRIENDLY MATCH.</head>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCH.</p>
        <p>Parish Church V. Penketh Heath-road Club. Played at the Rectory on Saturday, resulting in a win for the home team by 27 points. Scores, Parish Church names first : R. W. Antwis (captain), 15; A. Jones, 9. A. N. Other, 15; J. Ray, 14. S. Tilling, 11; Johnson Howard, 15. J. R. Squire, 15; C. Mbnks, 5. A. Yates, 15; E. Glover, 8. T. J. Hopkins, 15; James Howard, 7. A. N. Other, 15; A. Platt, I. J. Hill, 9; P. Johnson, 15. T. Wilkinson, 15; L. Monks, 8. E. Baston, 9; H. Dobbins, 15. R. Done, 10; W. Bennett, 15. R. Cook, 11; W. Howard, 15. 11. Benion, 15; J. Jackson, 10. A. N. Other, 9; R. Graham, 15. Total—Parish Church, 179; Penketh Heath- road, 152.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>" CROSFIELD " CUP.</head>
        <p>" CROSFIELD " CUP.</p>
        <p>W. Farrington's Success.</p>
        <p>After having been postponed twice on account of inclement weather the annual main for the " Crosfield" Cup took place on the Liberal Club green on Saturday. There were 0 entrants and the play Was of a very high order. After many exciting games, refereed by Mr. J. Broad- hurst, W. Farrington won the much-coveted trophy, having beaten B. Forster in the final. He had played a most consistent game, and amongst his most notable achievements were his victories over S. W. Jeffery (last year's win- ner) and A. Gleave, the latter being defeated in the semi-final. In the final Forster seemed to fall off in his play_ and was defeated somewhat easily. The prize-winners were: W. Farring- ton, B. Forster, J. Parsons, A. Gleave, J. Feeney, and R. Hitchmough. In presenting the prizes Councillor 17:1kinson complimented the winner on his succes. Being a young man, he made them feel that the old men would have to give up the game. The games had been marked with a splendid sports- manlike spirit, and the competition had met with every success. In reply, Mr. Farriugton said he was proud to win the cup, and he hoped that next year, if he was beaten, it would be by Mr. Forster, who had played so hard that day. The President of the green, Mr. Joseph Wil- kinson, was away on holidays.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>LAWN TENNIS.</head>
        <p>LAWN TENNIS.</p>
        <p>" CAUSEWAY " V. CROSFIELD'S RECREATION.</p>
        <p>Played at Wild-e—rs-pool on Saturday. Details: S. Woods and J. Paterson beat A. Percival and S. Hayes, 6-1, 6-5; beat H. Sandy and G. Evans, 6-3. 6-5; beat H. Winter and G. Whit- field, 6-0, 6-0. H. Bibby and W. Green beat A. Percival and S. Hayes, 3-6, 6-3, 8-6; lost to H. Sandy and G. Evans, 4-6, 6-1, 2-6; beat H. Winter and G. Whitfield, 6-3, 6-0. W. Woods and R. Walsh beat A. Percival and S. Hayes, 6-2, 5-7, 8-6; lost to H. Sandy and G. Evans, 6-2, 4-6, 4-6; beat H. Winter and G. Whitfield, 6-0, 6-0. Miss A. Wilson and Mrs. W. Woods beat Misses 'Daintith and Daintith, 6-0, 6_3; beat Misses Daintith and Moffat, 6-0, 6-1. Miss D. Isaac and Mrs. Green heat Misses Jones and Daintith, 6-5, 6-5; beat Misses Daintith and Moffat, 6-3, 6-1.</p>
        <p>Causeway won 11 events ,to 2; 24 sets to 6; 170 games to 92.</p>
        <p>Exo Kills Rats &amp; Mice (registered). Government testhpony with every box. The World's gr eatest. No danger or smell from dead vermin. Safe, Sure and Quick. Chemist agents: Harold Smith, 77, Bridge Street, Warrington; Joseph Taylor dr Co., Radcliffe: J. Dawson, Coins. Bd., 6d., 1/-, 2/-, post 2d. Makers, The " Exter- n:Lino " Chemical Co. Ltd., Dundee.</p>
        <p>_i_._</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>CRICKET.</head>
        <p>CRICKET.</p>
        <p>TO-DAY'S WARRINGTON MATCH.</p>
        <p>To-day the Warrington first team play Wigan at home, and the second strings meet at Wigan.</p>
        <p>Cairo-street Unitarians v. Widnes St. Ambrose's</p>
        <p>Played at Arpley on Saturday. Scores:— Cairo-street2nd.—J. Bailey b Oliver 7, A. Forbes c Tegg b Cleworth 0, A. 11. Mather 1, Oliver 17, A. Ellsmore lbw b Chidlow 4, J. Hal- lam b Cleworth 5, W. Hankinson b Oliver 3, P. Hill 13 Oliver 0, F. McKie c Cleworth b Oliver 7, F. Ellsmore c and b Oliver 0, R. Hill not out 2, J. Whittaker it. Prescot b Oliver 0, extras 3; total 48.</p>
        <p>Widnes St. Ambrose's.—E. Barton c Mather b A. Ellsmore 1, J. Oliver c A. Ellsmore b Mather 1, J. Chidlow b Mather 0, N. Prescot c Hallam b Bailey 13, T. Howard b Mather 0, A. Allen run out 8, 11. Cleworth run out 6, J. Hodgkins b A. Ellsmore 0, W. Smitheringale c P. Hill b Mather 0, R. Webster c P. Hill b A. Ellsmore 0, H. Tegg not out 0, extras 0; total 29.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>Moore and Daresbury V. Barnton.</head>
        <p>Moore and Daresbury V. Barnton.</p>
        <p>Played at Barnton on Saturday. Scores : Moore.—J. J. Lee-Jones c Molyneux b Kirk- man 10: F. Wilkinson lbw b Kirkman 0, S. de Prez b Powell 3, Wilkinson b Powell 0, Booler b Powell 3, S. Taylor e Kirkman b Powell 2, Hatton run out 20, Booth not out 0, Cartwright c and b Powell 0, Taylor b Capper 5, 11. de Prez b Capper 4, extras 2; total 49. Barnton.—Ellis b Booler 34, Burgess run out 12. Powell c Wilkinson b de Prez 32. Kirkman c Taylor b S. Taylor 23, Molyneux e Booth b de Prez 4, Bower b de Prez 1, Capper not out 2, Hughes b de Prez 0, Kirkman b de Prez 0, extras 0; total 108.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>RUGBY.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON CLUB PRACTICE.</p>
        <p>The ,Warrington N. 17. players will hold a practice game this afternoon, when lovers of the game will have an opportunity of weighing up the capabilities of the teams. The players have not yet signed on, but this operation will prob- ably be performed some time next week. Most of the old players are likely to turn out, and some "dark horses" may be discovered in the practice game. As regards training, the men are reported to be making satisfactory pro- gress.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>A meeting was held on Wednesday night, Mr. J. Wright presiding. A long discussion ensued respecting the formation of the Second Division, and it was finally decided to limit the division to twelve clubs, and the following were elected: Bank Quay Recs., St. Barnabas, Orford White Star, Paddington, Hollins Green, Earlestown St. John's Mission, Latchford Baptists, Newton Congregationalists, Earlestown Y.M.C.A., Elm- wood Congregational, Fairfield United and Fair- field.</p>
        <p>The following clubs have entered for the First Division; Orford St. John's, Latchford A.F.C., Great Sankey, Grappenhall and Crosfield's Recreation. It was decided to defer the arrange- ment of fixtures until the Ist of September, when further applications for admission to the League will be considered.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>ANGLING.</head>
        <p>ANGLING.</p>
        <p>Warrington Workingmen's Associa-</p>
        <p>N.A. CENTRE.</p>
        <p>IMPROVED CONDITIONS.</p>
        <p>As we announced last week, both the Bay Horse and Gladstone Arms' annual matches were under consideration. The Bay Horse Club held a general meeting on Monday, when it was decided to fish their match at Aintree, in the Leeds and Liverpool Canal, on August 30th, instead of August 23rd, thus putting it back for a week. Other particulars may be learned at the club house from T. Bryers, who is acting secretary, until Mr. Potter again returns to take up his duties. We hear that Mr. Potter has got through the most serious part of his illness, and will probably be in harness again in the course of a few weeks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>MArZHES TO PROCEED.</head>
        <p>MArZHES TO PROCEED.</p>
        <p>The Centre have not yet decided where they will fish, their annual match, but we hear that there will he no postponement, as several of the officials fail to see what their members would gain by bitting things off to a later date. As far as abandoning the match altogether is con- cerned, the officials would not listen to</p>
        <p>There are two sides to their decision. -Se;eral say we should not put any expense on our mem- bers as regards railway fares, entries, &amp;c., as at present they can ill afford it, and the match itself would sure to be a failure. The other side is, if a member has paid his subscription and is still in work when the date of the match arrives, why should lie be barred from taking part in a contest that has been arranged from the beginning of the season, because some of his club mates are not in as fortunate a position? It is optional in all matches' whether members go or not, and if the entries suffer to any great extent, well, the members who take part in the contest will not expect the same amount of prizes as they would if things were all going well. Last, but not least, there are already Prizes given for the annual matches, both at the Bay Horse Club and the Gladstone Arms, by our kind patrons, and these alone will be worth com- peting for without either society spending a ' single penny of their funds. Other arrangements are also in hand to pro- vide a match for the members who cannot make lit convenient to go to Aintree. As we predicted in last week's issue, the canals were all the better for the fine spell of dry weather. Norton improved greatly. A couple of members were responsible for 38 good sizeable roach and three eels; another member had 12 roach, and another was amongst the eels to the extent of seven and one roach. Several others had good match practice amongst the small fry. All members who are entitled to compete in the centre match for the N.A.A. championship, please note that it will be fished off at Norton this week-end. All competitors to be in their places as advised at last Monday's meeting. Further particulars from the Centre secretary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>PRIZES.</head>
        <p>PRIZES.</p>
        <p>_ We cannot yet state what the full list of prizes will be, but we understand that the first three in the clubs will surpass those of previous years. The first prize alone should ensure great com- petition : Gladstone Arms (Centre) : Ist, Challenge Cup, gold medal, and prize value .02 10s. Bay Horse: Ist, the tea urn that was so much admired by members when on view in Bridge- 'street a few weeks ago. When members come to look at other branches of sport and total up their subscriptions and membership aud look at their first prizes, in comparison to our own, they must admit that they have patrons that any society would be proud of enrolling. A.S.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>ENLISTED.</head>
        <p>ENLISTED.</p>
        <p>When the name of William Henry Mallin, labourer, 1, Marsh-street, Rotherham, was called at the Warrington Police Court on Monday morning, the Chief Constable said'the man was before the Court the previous week charged with being on enclosed premises, but the police, after inquiries, were satisfied that the man was not there for any unlawful purpose, other than sleeping out. The Chairman of the Bench before whom the prisoner had previously been brought had been communicated with, and with his permission the man had been let out of prison and ,had enlisted. Mr. Nicholls asked that the minor charge should be dismissed.— The magistrates concurred.</p>
