      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <p>rgszEzzrzmAkotor-cycles and 1 -- Accessories. Repairs by competent men. Petrol and tyre". WO KW Mari-, STOCHTON HEATH CraigrE LONOCCII ROAD. Telephone 63 Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>Cars.</p>
        <p>CHAUFFEUR'S DEATH.</p>
        <p>Mr. H. ELowarth's Demise After an Accident.</p>
        <p>A SINGULAR AFFAIR.</p>
        <p>An inquiry concerning a'singular motor car accident, which occurred at St. Albans in April, was opened by Coroner Brighouse at Warring- ton on Wednesday, and adjourned for further evidence.</p>
        <p>The subject of the inquest was Harry Howarth (23), a chauffeur, living at 3, Willis-street. He was employed by Messrs. Garland, Bridge-street. That firm was represented by Mr. Longland, solicitor, and Mr. T. S. Steel was for the rela- tives.</p>
        <p>James Howarth, the father, said deceased acted as a motor car driver for Messrs. Garland, and he was often sent out to bring home cars. On 23rd April last be left Warrington for Lon- don to bring back a car. About 11.45 p.m. that night he learned that his son had been injured in a motor car accident near St. Albans. The accident was caused through a collision with a cyclist.</p>
        <p>After lying in the hospital at St. Albans, be was taken home. At the time he sustained a fracture of the skull, a fracture of the bridge of the nose, and he had lost tile sight of his right eye. About a week ago he had a, relapse, lost a great quantity of blood, and died on Monday morning.</p>
        <p>DID NOT KNOW HOW IT OCCURRED. The Coroner: Did he tell you how the accident occurred P—He said That he did not know any- thing whatever about it. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>_ Mr. Longland : There is no dispute about the accident.</p>
        <p>The Coroner: The employers admit the cause of death, and so we need not have a post- mortem examination.</p>
        <p>Mr. Longland: That is, so.</p>
        <p>The Coroner: I think there ought to be an adjournment to ascertain how the accident came about. I have a statement from the police here for the 'purposes of the St. Albans police. So far as the deceased's recollection goes, it seemed he left London at 6 o'clock and reached St. Albans at 8 o'clock, where he stopped to ask a constable the way. Then while proceeding up a hill on his proper side of the road something suddenly came upon him, and after that he re- membered nothing more for eight days. Tie was then told that he had been in collision with a cyclist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <head>INJURED BY THE SCREEN.</head>
        <p>INJURED BY THE SCREEN.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. Steel: lie told me he could not under- stand how he met with his injuries—whether they were done by the wind screen or the steer- ing wheel. _ _</p>
        <p>i'he Coroner: When a collision occurs be- tween a cyclist and a motor car the cyclist usually comes • off worst I think it would be better to adjourn the inquest _</p>
        <p>Mr. Steel: I understand the cyclist was a soldier, who might now be out of the country. The cyclist was also injured; and was for a time in the hospital...</p>
        <p>Mr. Longland said the soldier had returned to his duties. He had been cycling from Weedin Barracks to a military tournament in London, and was found unconscious on the road some time after the accident.</p>
        <p>The inquest was adjourned until 9.30 a.m. on Monday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>YOUTH'S INJURED EYE. .A Compensation Claim That Failed.</head>
        <p>YOUTH'S INJURED EYE. .A Compensation Claim That Failed.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Court (in Mon- day. before his Honour-Judge Gilmer, an appli- cation was made on behalf of Walter McGrail</p>
        <p>(18), planer, 8, Woreley-street, Golborne, for compensation in respect of an accident alleged to have occurred in the course of his employ- ment at the Vulcan Foundry Works, Newton. Mr. Rees, barrister, said the application was made by McGrail's father. On May 19th, 1913, a piece of steel entered McGrail's right eye when he was following his employment. He was of the opinion that the respondents would con- tend that the accident did not happen, and sec- ondly that McGrail did not give proper notice of the alleged accident, and that be was not in- capacitated. When he received his injury McGrail went to an ambulance man who ex- tracted the piece of steel. He also informed the foreman of the accident. McGrail saw Dr. McArthur, who saw the eye was very inflamed. The day after the alleged accident another workman also informed the foreman about McGrail's eye. The eye recovered, and the youth went back to work on May 21st, working until June 16th, although the eye was irritable all the time.. On June 16 McGrail did not work for four weeks, during which time he received 10s. per week for insurance money. He thee worked until September 10th, when he was sent to Wigan Infirmary. In consequence of what he was told there he informed the foreman that he was suffering, from iritis, and also saw Miss Poole, who had to deal with compensation matters at the works. The doctors would give evidence to the ;effect that McGrail, although not at present totally incapaeitated. would not be able to do the fine work of a planer. Several witnesses were called' to bear out counsel's statement, Dr. McArthur stating that applicant could do ordinary plain work, but not fine work. For the resnondents, Mr. Elliott said that they denied that the accident occurred or that notice. was given of any alleged accident. The fore- man was never informed of any accident, and the youth did ,not mention any accident at the. works. There was no case to wet. He had not called evidence to prove that there was an acci- dent, and secondly that if there was an accident, he had not called evidence that incapacity was due to such accident if it occurred. The first intimation the firm had of the accident was when the youth went to see about compensation. In summing up, the Judge said the youth had stated his case in as vague a manner as he had ever known. He had given many dates to differ- ent people as to when the accident happened. The evidence of the doctors was not sufficient to prove to him that iritis was due to the accident. ITe gave judgment in favour of the respondents, with costs.</p>
      </div>
