      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
        <head>ALSACE-LORRAINE</head>
        <p>ALSACE-LORRAINE</p>
        <p>FURTHER FRENCH ' SUCCESSES AND ADVANCE.</p>
        <p>On the Road for Strasburg.</p>
        <p>On the Franco-German border considerable French forces are harrying the Germans in encounters whose results may open a road for important movements. France has now a strong hold on several valleys leading down on the German side of the Vosges to the Rhine. French troops also behaved brilliantly in an attack upon the German troops holding Rlamont and Cirey, two posts on the Lorraine frontier due west of Strasburg. They attacked these posts on Friday night and re-took them, put- ting to flight a Bavarian Corps. On Saturday they advanced into Lorraine, crossing the Ger- man frontier.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday it was reported that French troops had secured the brest line of the Vosges up to Mount Donon.</p>
        <p>1,000 GERMAN PRISONERS.</p>
        <p>The Press Bureau issued the following The French troops in the course of a rapid advance along the Valley of Schirmeck have secured a thousand prisoners. A visit to the scene of the fights of the last few days in Upper Alsace has shown the great destructive effect obtained by the Allied Artillery. The trenches abandotted by the enemy were full of dead and wounded. s</p>
        <p>"TOWARDS STRASBURG." A steady advance into Alsace by the French Eastern Army•was indicated by Monday night's official dispatches. The French had driven the Germans back from the Vosges, and are marching towards Strasburg through the valley of the Breusch. At all points success is claimed for the French arms. . . . _ _ _</p>
        <p>An official statement, issued by the Press Bureau, said that by their fresh advance at Cirey the French had forced the Bavarian Corps which had retired before them on Sat- urday to retreat still further.</p>
        <p>The French were reported active in the Rhineward valleys of the Vosges and on the Lorraine plateau south of Metz. Amongst their captures are 12 field guns, 19 motor wag- gons, and some 1,500 prisoners. General Joffre, the Commander-in-Chief, issued a statement saying the Germans were falling back in disorder. They had abandoned equipment, ammunition, and stores. On Thursday same the news that French Cavalry had entered Luxemburg. In Alsace the French troops were pushing forward day by day, and appear to have had little difficulty in overcoming such resistance as the German forces have hitherto been able to make.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <head>COUNTER-STROKE TO BRUSSELS.</head>
        <p>A COUNTER-STROKE TO BRUSSELS.</p>
        <p>As a set-off to the German success on the left wing reported yesterday, the Allies had the continued success of the French arms on the right wing to record. The French at that time held a front of 40 miles in Lorraine that stretches from a point north of Saarburg, through Mouchingen, to Delme. They had thus driven a broad wedge between the great German frontier fortress Metz and Strasburg, while their right flank had been skilfully covered by their operations in the Vosges. In Alsace they had succeeded in re-taking Mulhausen, with a trophy of six German guns. Since the war began, it seems, at least twenty pieces of German field artillery have fallen into French hands.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0186" n="art0186">
        <head>RUSSIAN AVALANCHE. MOVES AFTER A RAPID MOBILISATION.</head>
        <p>RUSSIAN AVALANCHE. MOVES AFTER A RAPID MOBILISA- TION.</p>
        <p>Last week-end the Russian armies began to make themselves felt upon the frontiers of Austria-Hungary and Germany. The Southern ' Arrai of the Czar entered Galicia in three columns, following three separate lines of rail- way converging upon, Lemberg. Russian troops defeated several invading forces from Germany and Austria. "The Russian avalanche" was reported on Tuesday by the "Times" military correspon- dent to have begun. That Russian troops should have succeeded in penetrating. far into Eastern Prussia before the German forces On the Belgian and French frontiers had been able to score any success against France and Bel- gium is a fact of the first importance. Russia's mobilisation has been much more rapid than anyone thought possible before the war. On Sunday, the seventeenth day after the issuing of the call to the colours, the general advance began. According tO an authorised statement by the "Times" correspondent in St. Petersburg, strong pressure will soon be felt on the eastern flank of Germany and Austria. TWENTY. ARMY CORPS TO INVADE PRUSSIA. _ _ _</p>
        <p>A Central News message; dated Ronie, Friday, says an official message from St. Petersburg states that the Russians have occupied :Lyk, after two days' fierce fighting. • Twenty Army Corps will carry out the inva- sion of Prussia. The , Grand Duke Nicholas is directing the operations.</p>
        <p>RUSSIA'S OFFER TO POLAND. The Grand Duke Nicholas, General-in-Chief of the Russian Army, has issued a proclamation promising that if Russia is victorious the nation of Poland will be re-constituted by the restitu- tion of those portions of its territory which for a century and a half have been in Prussian and Austrian hands. Poland will be granted local autonomy under a Lieutenant-Governor ap- pointed.by the Czar, and she will have freedom for her own language and her Own religion. A deep impression has been made on the Polish leaders, and it is said the offer will lead to enthusiastic support of the Russian war policy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <head>AUSTRIA.</head>
        <p>AUSTRIA.</p>
        <p>Austria has renewed her attack on Servi.a. Heavy fighting on the Danube, Save, and Drin river frontiers is reported. Unofficial reports credit the Servians with having heavily de- feated the Austrians.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <head>NORTH SEA.</head>
        <p>NORTH SEA.</p>
        <p>"A CERTAIN LIVELINESS APPARENT."</p>
        <p>Some fighting has taken place in the North Sea between British naval scouts and German cruisers. The London Press Bureau says no losses have been " reported or claimed," and adds that a certain liveliness is apparent in the southern waters. The "Manchester Guardian suggests that possibly the Germans were at- tempting too late to learn whether the Expedi- tionary Force was crossing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>MEDITERRANEAN.</head>
        <p>MEDITERRANEAN.</p>
        <p>THE HUMILIATED GERMAN CRUISER.</p>
        <p>The sale to Turkey by Germany of the war- ships Goeben and Breslau is no longer doubted.</p>
      </div>
