      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0202" n="art0202">
        <head>PROGRESS OF THE WAR.</head>
        <p>PROGRESS OF THE WAR.</p>
        <p>suannary of Authentic News from All Involved Areas.</p>
        <p>SPECIALLY COMPILED FOR "THE EXAMINER."</p>
        <p>WEEK.</p>
        <p>BRITISH FORCE LANDED. .</p>
        <p>trust. Your duty cannot be done unless your health is sound. So keep constantly on your guard against any excesses."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0203" n="art0203">
        <head>IN BELGIUM.</head>
        <p>IN BELGIUM.</p>
        <p>BRUSSELS TAKEN BY THE ENEMY.</p>
        <p>Allies' Strategic Retreat.</p>
        <p>Events have moved rapidly during the :Week on the Belgian left wing. A great concentra- tion of German strength took place upon the positions covering Brussels, and on Thursday night the British Press Bureau issued the fol- lowing _p</p>
        <p>" The bulk of the Belgian Field Army, con- fronted by superior numbers, have fallen back. Communication with Brussels has become diffi- cult since early this morning. "The Belgian troops have admirably per- formed their duty of delaying the hostile ad- vance and enabling their allies to complete their concentration without interference, and their retirement, which has been anticipated for some time, has been dictated by the strategi- cal situation."</p>
        <p>It became clear that an attempt was to be made to turn the Allies' left. Tirlemont, Diest and Louvain, during the early days of the war the Belgian headquarters, fell into the invaders' hands, clearing the way to Brussels, which fell into the hands of the Germans. The city was given up without a shot being fired. This was done in order to save the city, if possible, from 'German savagery, the Prussians having threatened to lay the place in ruins if their entry was opposed. Before the Belgian Army retired upon Ant- werp, by instructions of the Government they made a desperate resistance. At Aerschot, near Diest, two regiments held their ground against overwhelming numbers for two hours. They inflicted severe loss on the enemy, but themselves suffered heavily. Meanwhile the Germans were passing very large bodies of troops over the Meuse between Liege and Namur. The Belgian Army has nobly fulfilled its ap- pointed task. The stubborn opposition shown to the Germans has upset the German plan and given time for the Allies to concentrate. The hostile armies will now enter upon the battle that is to come on terms of equality, so far as completeness of preparation goes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-22_WidnesExaminer_art0204" n="art0204">
        <head>BATTLE AT DINANT.</head>
        <p>BATTLE AT DINANT.</p>
        <p>In the Belgian Ardennes the invaders •were moving last week-end. Where the Meuse runs northward to its turn to the east at Namur they attempted to cross the river. Near Dinant they had a heavy engagement with French troops, who repelled their attacks. The action lasted from 6 a.m. to about 6 p.m., when the Germans had been driven about nine miles south to a point between Givet and Rochefort.</p>
        <p>. .. The " Times " special correspondent at Namur witnessed this battle, and bears witness to the</p>
      </div>
