        <p>CHOKED BY A PEA. Remarkable Death of a Warrington Child. A pitiful story was unfolded at the local Coroner's Court on Monday afternoon, when Mr. S. Brighouse inquired into the circum- stances attending the death of Bessie Birch, the five-year-old daughter of Mr. and Mrs. Harry Birch, Cliff Dene, Thelwall New-road, Latchford Without. Mr. James Birch, 29, Legh-street, Warrington, said the deceased was his granddaughter. About 7.30 on Sunday night he was standing in Cairo- street, having just come out of chapel, when the deceased ran to him and asked him for a pea: In reply to the Coroner, witness said he car- ried marrowfat peas to give to the deceased to please her, and she was aware of it. When he handed her a pea she ran back to her mother, and then walked- to the top of Cairo- street. The girl began to jump, about amongst the children, and later she was observed to point to her throat. An aunt put her finger down the girl's throat, but was unable to release the pea, and the girl was taken to Dr. Fox's surgery. As he happened to be out, the child was carried to Dr. Joseph's surgery. The doctor endeav- oured to remove the pea, and after Dr. Fox had been summoned, an operation was performed. The father said that he understood the child died on its way to the surgery. The Coroner said it was a very sad case, and might have happened just as easily to one of his own, or any of the jurors' children. A verdict of "Accidental death" was re- turned, death being due to suffocation caused by I the pea.</p>
