        <p>Thus the German Navy loses in a humiliating manner a super-Dreadnought more powerful than any British vessel in :the Mediterranean, and a fast light cruiser. During the first days of the war the British squadron covered the transportation of French troops from Northern Africa to Europe. The German shics did not even attempt to interfere. FRENCH FLEET SINKS AUSTRIAN CRUISER. The French Fleet in the Mediterranean have made a sweep of the Adriatic as far as Cattaro, and a small Austrian cruiser of the Aspern type was fired upon and sank. The Aspern and her class are vessels of 2,400' tons, with a speed of about 18 knots. They carry 8 4.71 n. guns and are protected with a 2in. armoured steel deck amidships. They were built about 14 years ago. The British Fleet has chased the Austrian warships .which have been bombarding the Montenegrin coast into Pola. JAPAN'S ULTIMATUM. GERMANY TO QUIT ASIA. Japan has delivered a seven days' ultimatum, which expires at noon to-morrow, to Germany. o.ur Eastern Allies request the Germans (1) To withdraw immediately from Japanese and Chinese waters the German men-of-war and armed vessels of all kinds, and to disarm at once those which cannot be withdrawn. (2) To deliver on a date not later than Sep- tember 15th to the Imperial Japanese author- ities, without condition or compensation, the entire leased territory of Kiaochan with a view to the eventual restoration of the same to China. We append the text of the communication from the Press Bureau The Governments of Great Britain and Japan having been in communication with each other, are of opinion that it is necessary for each to take action to protect the general interest in the Far East contemplated by the Anglo-Japanese Alliance, keeping specially in view the independence and integrity of China, and provided for in that Agreement. It is understood that the action of Japan will not extend to the Pacific Ocean beyond the China Seas except in so far as it inay be necessary to protect Japanese shipping, lines in the Pacific, nor beyond Asiatic waters westward of the China Seas, nor to any foreign territory except territory in German occupation on the Continent of Eastern Asia.</p>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE. • The Irish week at the Royal Court Theatre has been attended with success. Every evening with the exception of last night "The Wearin' o' the Green" has been presented, in the course of which well-known Irish songs are intro- duced. All the artistes are exceedingly clever. Barney O'Hea, as rendered by Mr. Chalmers MaCkey, is a character which keeps the audi- ence in a merry mood, whilst the sympathies of Norah M'Grath are well shown by Mrs. Chal- mers Mackey. "Still Alarm " Next Week. Glenville and Osmond's London company in "The Still Alarm will be the attraction next week. THE HIPPODROME. The naval athlete, Brian, gives a clever exhi- bition at the Hippodrome this week in his "Pastimes on a Battleship." He gives a mar- vellous exhibition of weight lifting, in which he balances a field gun and later a ifilick-firing guni with the gunner. A popular musical " turn" is provided by the Five Musical .Tritons, whilst Dainty Doris, comedienne and dancer, pleases the audience immensely, as does Maggie Carr in her clever dancing act. That versatile come- dian and vocalist, Frank Cass, was well received on his return visit. Next Week's Programme. Next week's programme includes: Pearls of Music, Hayes and Merritt, bounding marvels; the Westwoods, in cycle act; Sisters Royce and Sid, vocalists and dancers; Ivor Vinter, little I surprise; Ethel Holland, contralto; and the Perfection Pictures. STAR KINEMA. On Monday, Tuesday and Wednesday "The I Price of a Ruby" will be the principal attrac- tion, and for the remainder of the week "The Third String" will be the star film. THE FOOD PRICES. • Still Further Decline in the Cost of Provisions. There has been a still further slight decrease in the cost of provisions in Warrington, and ap- proximately the prices yesterday were : Butter, is. id. to is. 3d. Bacon, 9d. to is. id. Cheese, 81d. to 10d. Lard, 7d. to 10d. Margarine, 7d. to is. Granulated sugar, 3d. to 31d. Lump sugar, 4d. to 41d. Flour (per stone), 2s. to 2s. 4d. Eggs (per dozen), is. 3d. to 2s. Meat Prices. Beef (English), 7d. to 11d. Beef (chilled), sd. to 9d. Ileef (frozen), 7d. English lamb and mutton (choice joints), 10d. to is. Canterbury lamb, 71d. to 81d. Frozen mutton, exl. BEST FOODS DURING THE WAR. Hints by the Warrington Medical Officer. Dr. G. W. N. Joseph, medical officer of War- rington, has issued a notice on the subject of the best foods to buy during the war. Amongst the vegetable foods lie recommends the following : Dried peas, haricot beans, lentils, " seconds " flour, oatmeal, potatoes, onions, beetroot, car- rots, cabbage, bananas, raisins, currants, Sze. As animal foods he recommends Herrings, bloaters, kippers, frozen meat, drip- ping, margarine and cheese.</p>
        <p>STARVING BELGIANS. "To the Rescue of an Afflicted People." • Dr. Charles Sarolea, the editor of "Every- man," who is a Belgian, has started a Belgian relief fund. The Belgian Government, he points out, cannot at the present juncture devote itself to the relief of private citizens. "The Belgians (he writes) are nearly ruined. All trade ha's come to a standstill, and the misery is too far-reaching for the Belgians to cope with it single-handed. This is not a Bel- gian war, it is a European conflagration, and it is for the whole of Europe to come to the rescue of an afflicted people, and to prevent the horrors of famine being added to the horrors of the battlefield. It is in order to assist the afflicted population of the provinces of Liege and of Lemberg, who are the worst sufferers, that we are opening in Everyman ' a special relief f und ." All contributions should be sent to Dr. Sarolea (Belgian Consul), editor of "Everyman," 21, Royal-terrace, Edinburgh.</p>
